window.AIBRIEFS=window.AIBRIEFS||{};Object.assign(window.AIBRIEFS,{"1974/73-2024":{"facts":"Organizations and individuals from the Rochester, New York, area sued the neighboring town of Penfield and members of its zoning, planning and town boards, alleging that Penfield's zoning ordinance, by its terms and as enforced, excluded people of low and moderate income, and with them many members of racial and ethnic minorities, from living in the town. The ordinance allocated 98% of vacant land to single-family detached housing and only 0.3% to multifamily housing. The plaintiffs were Metro-Act of Rochester (a nonprofit), Rochester property taxpayers who said Penfield's practices raised their city taxes, and low- and moderate-income minority residents of the Rochester area who said they could not find housing in Penfield. Rochester Home Builders Association sought to intervene, and the Housing Council in the Monroe County Area sought to be added as a plaintiff. They claimed violations of the First, Ninth and Fourteenth Amendments and of 42 U.S.C. §§ 1981, 1982 and 1983, and sought declaratory and injunctive relief and damages.","procedure":"The District Court for the Western District of New York dismissed the complaint, holding among other things that none of the plaintiffs had standing, and denied the motions to add Housing Council and to let Home Builders intervene. The Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit affirmed on standing grounds, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did any of the plaintiffs, whether individuals, taxpayers or associations, allege facts sufficient to give them standing to challenge Penfield's allegedly exclusionary zoning practices in federal court?","holding":"No. None of the petitioners met the threshold requirement of clearly alleging facts showing that he was a proper party to invoke the court's power, whether standing is viewed as an Article III case-or-controversy requirement or as a matter of prudential limits on federal jurisdiction. A plaintiff challenging exclusionary zoning must allege specific, concrete facts showing that the practices harm him personally and that he would benefit in a tangible way from the court's intervention.","reasoning":"Justice Powell explained that “In essence the question of standing is whether the litigant is entitled to have the court decide the merits of the dispute or of particular issues,” a question with a constitutional dimension (a personal injury fairly traceable to the defendant and redressable by the court) and a prudential one (generally no standing for generalized grievances or to assert the rights of third parties, unless Congress provides otherwise). The low- and moderate-income plaintiffs had never lived in or owned property in Penfield and had not been denied any permit; their inability to live there depended on third-party builders, and the record suggested it reflected the economics of the housing market, so they failed to show a substantial probability that relief would let them live there. The Court stated: “We hold only that a plaintiff who seeks to challenge exclusionary zoning practices must allege specific, concrete facts demonstrating that the challenged practices harm him, and that he personally would benefit in a tangible way from the court's intervention.” The Rochester taxpayers' injury was conjectural, would result from decisions of Rochester officials who were not parties, and rested on the rights of excluded third parties, which prudential rules barred them from asserting. Metro-Act's claim that its Penfield members lost the benefits of an integrated community was distinguished from Trafficante v. Metropolitan Life Ins. Co. because no claim was made under the Civil Rights Act of 1968. Home Builders could not seek damages for its members without their individual participation and identified no current project blocked by the town; Housing Council's only member with a Penfield project, Penfield Better Homes, had not shown that its 1969 dispute remained live when the suit was filed in 1972.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, writing that “the Court reads the complaint and the record with antagonistic eyes,” that Metro-Act and Housing Council had standing, and that the case should go to trial. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices White and Marshall, dissented, arguing that the opinion has overtones of outmoded notions of pleading and that its rejection of almost every kind of plaintiff “can be explained only by an indefensible hostility to the claim on the merits”; in his view at least three groups of plaintiffs had alleged enough to proceed.","disposition":"Affirmed, 5-4. Justice Powell delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun and Rehnquist. Justice Douglas dissented; Justice Brennan dissented, joined by Justices White and Marshall.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep422/usrep422490/usrep422490.pdf"},"1973/72-1410":{"facts":"Illinois officials administered Aid to the Aged, Blind, or Disabled (AABD), a program funded jointly by the State and the federal government. Federal regulations required states to decide applications within 30 or 45 days and to send checks within those periods, but Illinois's own manual and practice did not comply. Jordan, whose disability application went unacted on for almost four months, sued state and county officials for himself and a class, alleging violations of the federal regulations and of equal protection.","procedure":"The district court declared the Illinois manual provision invalid, enjoined future compliance with the federal time limits, and also ordered state officials to release and remit AABD benefits wrongfully withheld to eligible applicants from July 1, 1968, to April 1971. The Seventh Circuit affirmed, rejecting an Eleventh Amendment objection by treating the payments as “equitable restitution.” The Supreme Court granted certiorari because of a conflict with the Second Circuit.","question":"Does the Eleventh Amendment bar a federal court from ordering state officials to pay retroactive welfare benefits wrongfully withheld, and did Illinois waive its immunity by taking part in the federal-state program?","holding":"Yes. The Eleventh Amendment bars the retroactive portion of the decree because the award would be paid from the state treasury and is in practical effect damages against the State; Ex parte Young permits only prospective relief. Illinois did not waive its immunity merely by participating in a federally funded program.","reasoning":"Justice Rehnquist reviewed the history of the Eleventh Amendment and Hans v. Louisiana to show that an unconsenting State is immune from federal suits by its own citizens, and that a suit nominally against officials is barred when it is in essence one for money from the state treasury (Ford Motor Co.). Ex parte Young allows prospective injunctions, and their ancillary effect on state funds is permissible, but this award compensates for past violations and would to a virtual certainty be paid from state funds. Labeling it “equitable restitution” does not change that, and Ford Motor Co. would otherwise have to be overruled. The Court disapproved its earlier summary affirmances and Shapiro v. Thompson to the extent inconsistent, noting that summary dispositions carry less weight than an opinion on the merits. On waiver, the Court required express language or overwhelming implication, said “Constructive consent is not a doctrine commonly associated with the surrender of constitutional rights, and we see no place for it here,” and distinguished Parden and Petty because Congress here had not authorized suit against a class of defendants that includes States. Section 1983 does not itself waive immunity. The defense could be raised late because it partakes of a jurisdictional bar.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented: the case is governed by Ex parte Young, prospective and retroactive relief have the same effect on the treasury, many prior cases approved retroactive payments, and by joining the cooperative welfare program Illinois waived its immunity as in Parden. Justice Brennan dissented, adhering to his view that States surrendered sovereign immunity in the plan of the Convention for matters within Congress's enumerated powers, and noting that the Eleventh Amendment by its terms does not bar suits by a State's own citizens. Justice Marshall, joined by Justice Blackmun, dissented: by voluntarily joining a cooperative program and accepting its conditions, Illinois consented to the full range of section 1983 remedies, including retroactive payments, which Congress intended and which are needed to deter noncompliance.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was reversed as to the retroactive payments and the case remanded. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White and Powell; Justices Douglas, Brennan, Marshall and Blackmun dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep415/usrep415651/usrep415651.pdf"},"1976/75-1578":{"facts":"Sykes shot and killed Willie Gilbert and then told police and his wife what he had done. At the station he was read his Miranda rights, agreed to talk, and made a statement that two officers repeated at his Florida murder trial. His counsel never objected that he had been too intoxicated to understand the warnings, and the trial judge did not raise the issue or hold a hearing on voluntariness.","procedure":"Sykes was convicted of third-degree murder, did not challenge the statements on direct appeal, and later raised the claim without success in state post-conviction proceedings. The federal district court ordered a state hearing on voluntariness, and the Fifth Circuit agreed that the failure to comply with Florida's contemporaneous-objection rule barred review only if the right to object was deliberately bypassed for tactical reasons.","question":"Does a state defendant's failure to object at trial, as Florida's contemporaneous-objection rule requires, bar federal habeas corpus review of a Miranda claim unless the failure was a knowing and deliberate bypass under Fay v. Noia?","holding":"Yes. Absent a showing of cause for the noncompliance and actual prejudice from the alleged violation, a failure to make a timely objection under a state contemporaneous-objection rule bars federal habeas review of the underlying claim. The Court rejected the broad deliberate-bypass standard of Fay v. Noia as applied to such trial defaults.","reasoning":"Justice Rehnquist concluded that Florida's rule required a motion to suppress before trial and that nothing in Jackson v. Denno requires a voluntariness hearing without some challenge to the confession, so the default was an independent and adequate state ground. He extended the cause-and-prejudice test of Davis and Francis v. Henderson to this setting. A contemporaneous objection lets the record be made while witnesses' memories are fresh, lets the trial judge who saw the witnesses decide the facts, can lead to exclusion of the evidence and so promote finality, and forces the prosecution to confront the issue. The broad Fay rule could encourage defense lawyers to hold back claims and gamble on acquittal, weaken state enforcement of such rules, and turn the state trial from the main event into a “tryout on the road” for federal habeas. The cause-and-prejudice exception guards against a miscarriage of justice. The Court left its precise content open, but found neither here: Sykes gave no explanation for his failure to object, and the other evidence of guilt was substantial.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred, stressing that the Fay deliberate-bypass standard was designed for decisions personally made by the defendant, such as whether to appeal, and does not fit on-the-spot trial decisions that necessarily rest with counsel. Justice Stevens concurred, seeing the holding as consistent with how lower courts actually applied Fay and thinking competent counsel could have chosen not to object to this statement, so that collateral attack was unwarranted. Justice White concurred in the judgment: the Court's finding of no prejudice amounted to a harmless-error finding that sufficed, and he believed the bypass rule, applied to counsel's trial judgments, already protected the State, with the petitioner bearing the burden of explaining the default. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented: ordinary procedural defaults come from lawyer error or inadvertence, not tactics, so Fay's rule of treating only intentional forfeitures as binding should remain; the new test is undefined, the State's interests do not justify punishing clients for counsel's mistakes, and the result could leave constitutional claims with no court.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was reversed and the case remanded to the district court with instructions to dismiss the habeas petition. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, Powell and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep433/usrep433072/usrep433072.pdf"},"1972/71-1639":{"facts":"Oklahoma's merit system Act, modeled on the federal Hatch Act, barred classified state employees from soliciting contributions for political purposes, from serving on party committees or as officers of partisan clubs, from running for paid public office, and from taking part in party management or campaigns, while allowing them privately to express opinions and vote. Three employees of the Corporation Commission were charged with violating it by asking coworkers to do campaign work and, for two of them, soliciting money, for the re-election of their superior.","procedure":"Before the Personnel Board proceeded, the employees sued in federal district court seeking to have two paragraphs of the Act declared void on their face for vagueness and overbreadth. The three-judge district court upheld the provisions, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction to consider the case with CSC v. Letter Carriers.","question":"Is the Oklahoma statute unconstitutionally vague or substantially overbroad on its face, so that employees whose own conduct it may validly reach can still have it invalidated because it might be applied to protected expression by others?","holding":"No. The statute gives adequate warning of what it forbids and is not vague. For overbreadth, particularly where conduct and not merely speech is involved, the overbreadth must be real and substantial in relation to the statute's plainly legitimate sweep; this statute is not substantially overbroad, and any improper applications can be handled case by case.","reasoning":"Justice White found the statute clear enough for ordinary people to understand, noting that appellants' conduct fell within its hard core and that the Personnel Board could give advance rulings. Generally a person to whom a law can constitutionally be applied cannot challenge it based on possible unconstitutional applications to others, since courts are not roving commissions. The First Amendment overbreadth doctrine is a limited exception that allows challenges because a broad law may chill others' protected speech, but it is “strong medicine” to be used sparingly and as a last resort, and it weakens as the law reaches conduct rather than pure speech and falls within otherwise valid regulation. The Court therefore required overbreadth to be “substantial as well” as real. Section 818 is an even-handed, noncensorial regulation of political activity, and under Letter Carriers much of what it forbids is clearly valid. The Board's and the Attorney General's constructions confined it to clearly partisan activity, and possible improper applications, such as to buttons or bumper stickers, do not require striking it down entirely.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, relying on his Letter Carriers dissent: the First Amendment protects public as well as private political discourse by government employees, who have no watered-down rights, and the statute's reservation of only private expression shows it reaches public expression. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, dissented: the Act's key terms are left almost undefined and the Board's rules, including one banning political buttons and bumper stickers, show it reaches protected conduct, so it should be invalid on its face; the new requirement of substantial overbreadth is undefined, treats conduct differently from speech without explanation, and effectively overrules Coates v. City of Cincinnati.","disposition":"The judgment of the district court was affirmed. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas, Brennan, Stewart and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413601/usrep413601.pdf"},"1972/71-1043":{"facts":"Heller managed a Greenwich Village movie theater showing a sexually explicit film. After police saw part of it, a New York criminal court judge went to a performance and watched the entire film, then signed a warrant to seize the film and warrants to arrest the theater manager, projectionist and ticket taker, without notifying anyone at the theater. Police seized the only copy, three reels, and arrested Heller. He made no pretrial motion for the film's return or to suppress it, and nothing in the record showed whether another copy existed.","procedure":"Heller was tried 47 days after the seizure, convicted of obscenity under New York Penal Law section 235.05, and argued that seizing the film without a prior adversary hearing violated the Fourteenth Amendment. The New York Court of Appeals affirmed, holding that a judicial determination before the warrant issued was enough, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a judge who has viewed a film and found it obscene issue a valid warrant to seize it as evidence in a prosecution of the exhibitor without first holding an adversary hearing on probable obscenity?","holding":"Yes. Seizing a single copy of a film for the bona fide purpose of preserving it as evidence is constitutional if done under a warrant issued by a neutral magistrate on a finding of probable obscenity and a prompt adversary judicial determination is available on request. If other copies are unavailable, the film must be copied so exhibition can continue or be returned. The case was remanded on the substantive obscenity challenges in light of Miller and Paris Adult Theatre.","reasoning":"Chief Justice Burger observed that the Court had never recognized an absolute right to an adversary hearing before every seizure of allegedly obscene material, and that here the judge was a neutral magistrate who saw the entire film and could focus on the question of obscenity. Freedman v. Maryland and Thirty-seven Photographs require an adversary judicial determination for a valid final restraint, but only promptly, not before the initial seizure. Here the film was not subjected to a final restraint such as an injunction or destruction; one copy was held as evidence, there was no showing that exhibition was prevented, and an adversary trial occurred within 48 days. Any delay was due to Heller's choice not to file pretrial motions, though New York said it was ready to give immediate hearings. A Quantity of Books and Marcus involved mass seizures of publications for destruction without a judicial determination, and the Court did not disturb them, but seizing one copy as evidence is different. With a prior finding of probable cause and a prompt adversary hearing available, a preseizure hearing would add little protection, especially since films are compact and easily destroyed or altered. The Court remanded so New York courts could reconsider Heller's overbreadth, vagueness and private-viewing claims under Miller v. California and Paris Adult Theatre I v. Slaton.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, as he would have reversed outright because he thought the obscenity statute itself violated the First Amendment. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, dissented, concluding that the obscenity statute was unconstitutionally overbroad on its face for the reasons in his Paris Adult Theatre dissent, so that he did not reach the seizure question.","disposition":"The judgment of the New York Court of Appeals was vacated and the case remanded to reconsider the substantive challenges in light of Miller and Paris Adult Theatre I. Chief Justice Burger wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices White, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413483/usrep413483.pdf"},"1971/70-71":{"facts":"Bass, previously convicted of a felony in New York, was found on separate occasions to possess a pistol and a shotgun. The federal indictment did not allege, and the prosecution made no attempt to show, that either gun was possessed in commerce or affecting commerce. The government proceeded on the view that the statute bans all possession and receipt of firearms by convicted felons with no showing of a connection to interstate commerce.","procedure":"Bass was convicted under section 1202(a) of the Omnibus Crime Control and Safe Streets Act. The Second Circuit reversed, doubting the statute's constitutionality if read as the government urged. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict among the lower courts.","question":"Does the phrase “in commerce or affecting commerce” in section 1202(a) apply to the offenses of receiving and possessing a firearm, so that the government must show an interstate commerce connection in each case, or only to transporting?","holding":"The statute is ambiguous, and because it is criminal and the government's reading would significantly alter the federal-state balance, the narrower reading controls: the commerce requirement applies to possession and receipt as well as transportation, and the government must prove a nexus to interstate commerce in each case. The conviction could not stand without such proof.","reasoning":"Justice Marshall found the text and legislative history inconclusive. Grammatically, the natural reading is that the commerce clause qualifies all three verbs, and the government offered no sensible reason for limiting it to transportation while punishing all possession and receipt; the omitted comma carried no weight. The government's argument that the narrow reading would make Title VII redundant with Title IV was rejected, since the Titles overlap under either reading and Title VII was a hasty last-minute amendment. The legislative history, including Senator Long's remarks and the findings, leaned toward the government but did not explain why the commerce clause appears at all, and the Court concluded the statute was ambiguous (the government conceded the statute is “not a model of logic or clarity”). Two principles then decided the case: ambiguity in criminal statutes is resolved in favor of lenity, because of fair warning and because legislatures rather than courts should define crimes, and Congress is not deemed to have significantly changed the federal-state balance in policing local crime without a clear statement. The Court added that the nexus is met for receipt if the firearm previously traveled in interstate commerce, and for possession if it was moving interstate or its possession affects commerce. It did not reach the constitutional question.","separate":"Justice Brennan joined the judgment and opinion except for Part III, preferring to wait to decide what evidence suffices to show the nexus until a case presents it. Justice Blackmun, joined by the Chief Justice, dissented, arguing that the statute's structure and punctuation, the congressional findings and Senator Long's statements show Congress meant to reach every possession and receipt by a felon, that the Court's reading makes the statute nearly duplicate Title IV, and that the Court should have decided the constitutional issue.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals reversing the conviction was affirmed. Justice Marshall wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Douglas, Stewart and White and, except for Part III, Justice Brennan; Justice Blackmun and the Chief Justice dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep404/usrep404336/usrep404336.pdf"},"1971/70-5106":{"facts":"Grayned took part in a demonstration of about 200 people in front of West Senior High School in Rockford, Illinois, after Black students' grievances went unanswered. Demonstrators marched on a sidewalk about 100 feet from the building with signs about Black teachers and counselors. Witnesses for the city said the demonstrators chanted and made noise audible in the school, distracted students, and disrupted school procedure; defense witnesses said the demonstration was quiet and orderly.","procedure":"Grayned was convicted of violating two Rockford ordinances, an antipicketing ordinance and an antinoise ordinance, and fined $25 on each. He challenged only their facial validity, and the Illinois Supreme Court upheld both. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Is the antipicketing ordinance valid, and is the antinoise ordinance, which bars willfully making a noise or diversion that disturbs or tends to disturb the peace or good order of a school session on adjacent grounds, unconstitutionally vague or overbroad on its face?","holding":"The antipicketing ordinance, which allowed peaceful labor picketing but no other picketing near schools, violates the Equal Protection Clause under Police Department of Chicago v. Mosley. The antinoise ordinance is neither vague nor overbroad, because it prohibits only actual or imminent, willful interference with normal school activity.","reasoning":"On the antipicketing ordinance, the Court relied on its Mosley decision of the same day. On vagueness, Justice Marshall stated that “It is a basic principle of due process that an enactment is void for vagueness if its prohibitions are not clearly defined,” because vague laws fail to give fair warning, invite arbitrary enforcement, and chill protected freedoms. Although the question was close, the Court could not narrow state laws but extrapolated the ordinance's meaning from its words, its school context, and Illinois decisions construing similar laws to require an imminent threat, and concluded that it prohibits only willful noise or diversion that actually or imminently disrupts normal school activity. Unlike the ordinances in Cox and Coates, it is tied to a measurable impact, requires causation and willfulness, and does not punish unpopular views. On overbreadth, public sidewalks may be used for expression but are subject to reasonable time, place and manner rules, and the question is whether the manner of expression is basically incompatible with the normal activity of a place at a particular time. Under Tinker v. Des Moines, expression may be barred near a school only if it materially disrupts classwork or involves substantial disorder, and the ordinance goes no further, leaving quiet picketing and picketing outside school sessions untouched.","separate":"Justice Blackmun joined the judgment and Part I and concurred in the result as to Part II. Justice Douglas joined Part I but dissented as to the antinoise ordinance, arguing that the record showed Grayned marched quietly and made no noise, that the demonstration was orderly and in the best First Amendment tradition, and that the disruption came from a racial issue properly addressed through First Amendment means; he rejected the Court's view that his as-applied question was not presented.","disposition":"The judgment of the Illinois Supreme Court was affirmed in part and reversed in part: the antipicketing conviction was reversed and the antinoise ordinance upheld. Justice Marshall wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Brennan, Stewart, White, Powell and Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep408/usrep408104/usrep408104.pdf"},"1977/76-1346":{"facts":"Respondent Pons sued her former employer, Lorillard, alleging she was discharged because of her age in violation of the Age Discrimination in Employment Act of 1967 (ADEA). She sought reinstatement, lost wages, liquidated damages, attorney's fees, and costs. She demanded a jury trial on all issues of fact, and the employer moved to strike the demand.","procedure":"The District Court struck the jury demand but certified the question for interlocutory appeal. The Fourth Circuit vacated the order, holding that the ADEA and the Seventh Amendment gave respondent a jury trial right on her lost-wages claim. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict among the circuits.","question":"Is there a right to a jury trial in a private civil action for lost wages under the Age Discrimination in Employment Act?","holding":"Yes. Although the ADEA does not expressly provide for jury trials, its structure shows that Congress intended a jury to be available in private ADEA actions when a party seeks one. The Court therefore did not reach the Seventh Amendment question.","reasoning":"The Court first took up the statutory question to avoid a constitutional one. Section 7(b) directs that the ADEA be enforced under the powers, remedies, and procedures of the Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA), and before 1967 every court to consider the question had held that private FLSA actions carried a jury right. Congress is presumed to know such an interpretation when it incorporates a prior statute. Its selective changes to FLSA features, such as allowing injunctions and limiting liquidated damages to willful violations, showed detailed knowledge and suggested that what it did not change was meant to be adopted. The ADEA also authorizes “legal or equitable relief,” and “legal” is a term of art that ordinarily implies a jury under the Seventh Amendment. Comparison with Title VII did not help the petitioner, because Title VII does not use the word “legal,” makes backpay a matter of equitable discretion, and Congress chose FLSA procedures over Title VII procedures for the ADEA.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Marshall wrote for the Court; all participating Justices joined, and Justice Blackmun took no part. The Court expressed no view on a jury right for liquidated damages.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep434/usrep434575/usrep434575.pdf"},"1970/1066":{"facts":"Overton Park is a 342-acre city park in Memphis containing a zoo, golf course, nature trails, and 170 acres of forest. The Secretary of Transportation approved federal funding for a six-lane section of Interstate 40 that would cut through the park, sever the zoo from the rest, and destroy 26 acres. Section 4(f) of the Department of Transportation Act of 1966 and section 138 of the Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1968 bar such approval unless there is no feasible and prudent alternative and the project includes all possible planning to minimize harm. The Secretary announced his concurrence in 1968 and gave final route and design approval in November 1969, with no statement of factual findings explaining why alternatives were rejected.","procedure":"Citizens and conservation groups sued to block the project. The District Court granted summary judgment for the Secretary on the strength of litigation affidavits, and the Sixth Circuit affirmed, both holding that formal findings were not required and refusing to depose a former Federal Highway Administrator. The Supreme Court stayed construction and granted certiorari.","question":"Is the Secretary's approval of a highway through a public park subject to judicial review under the Administrative Procedure Act, and if so, what standard applies and what record must the court review?","holding":"The Secretary's decision is reviewable because it is not committed to agency discretion by law, and the proper standard is the arbitrary-and-capricious test of section 706(2)(A) applied through a thorough, probing inquiry rather than de novo or substantial-evidence review. Formal findings were not required, but review on litigation affidavits was inadequate, so the case was remanded to the District Court for plenary review of the full administrative record.","reasoning":"The exception for action committed to agency discretion is very narrow and covers cases where statutes are drawn so broadly that there is no law to apply. Section 4(f) and section 138 are clear directives that give protection of parkland paramount importance, so the Secretary may approve a route through a park only on finding that alternatives are infeasible or present unique problems, leaving law to apply. The substantial-evidence and de novo standards apply only in rulemaking, formal adjudication, or specific circumstances not present here. Review must nevertheless be searching: the court asks whether the Secretary acted within his authority, whether his choice rested on relevant factors without a clear error of judgment, and whether required procedures were followed, though the court may not substitute its judgment for the agency's. Post hoc affidavits prepared for litigation are not the whole record. Because the record may not reveal the Secretary's reasoning, the District Court may require explanatory testimony from the decisionmakers or new formal findings from the Secretary, viewed critically.","separate":"Justice Black, joined by Justice Brennan, agreed that the judgment should be reversed but would remand to the Secretary of Transportation rather than the District Court, because the record showed he had not complied with Congress's command. Justice Blackmun joined the Court's opinion and added a brief statement that the decisions under attack were made over more than a decade by several officials and that the statutes were enacted after the project was well developed, which explains the sketchy record.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the District Court. Justice Marshall wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Harlan, Stewart, White, and Blackmun; Justice Douglas took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep401/usrep401402/usrep401402.pdf"},"1976/74-6593":{"facts":"Gardner was convicted of first-degree murder in Florida for killing his wife with a blunt instrument. At the separate sentencing hearing, he testified to a day-long drinking spree and almost no memory of the assault, and the jury advised a life sentence because mitigating circumstances outweighed the aggravating one. The trial judge, who had ordered a presentence investigation report, rejected that advice and imposed death, finding the crime especially heinous, atrocious, or cruel and no mitigating circumstance. The judge said he relied in part on the report, but part of it was confidential and was not disclosed to counsel for either side, and counsel never asked to see it.","procedure":"The Florida Supreme Court affirmed the death sentence over two dissents, without discussing Gardner's objection to the confidential report and without having the confidential portion in the appellate record. The Supreme Court granted certiorari after upholding Florida's capital-sentencing statute on its face in Proffitt v. Florida.","question":"Does a death sentence violate due process when it is imposed in part on a presentence report whose confidential portion was never disclosed to the defendant or his counsel or made part of the record on appeal?","holding":"Yes, in the judgment of the Court. A plurality concluded that Gardner was denied due process because the death sentence rested at least in part on information he had no opportunity to deny or explain. The death sentence was vacated and the case remanded for further proceedings in the trial court.","reasoning":"The plurality distinguished Williams v. New York, where the judge described the report's facts in open court and the defendant did not challenge them. Since 1949 the Court had recognized that death is a different kind of punishment and that sentencing must satisfy due process. The State's justifications did not hold up: confidentiality invites unreliable information, claimed delay is overstated and worth any cost in a capital case, rehabilitation concerns have no force when death is imposed, and trusting judges is foreclosed after Furman and ignores the value of adversary comment. Even if some material could be withheld for good cause, the full report would have to be in the appellate record so reviewing courts can see what motivated each death sentence. Counsel's failure to ask for the report was not an effective waiver. Remand only to supplement the record would not cure the error, because full disclosure might have led the judge to accept the jury's recommendation of life.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred in the judgment. Justice White concurred in the judgment on Eighth Amendment grounds, reasoning that a death-selection procedure allowing secret information fails the reliability that Woodson v. North Carolina requires, and he did not reach due process. Justice Blackmun concurred in the judgment based on Woodson and Roberts v. Louisiana. Justice Brennan agreed with the plurality's due process reasoning but would hold the death penalty always unconstitutional and so dissented from the remand. Justice Marshall dissented in part, also viewing the death penalty as always unconstitutional, and criticized the Florida courts' cursory review as undermining the premises of Proffitt. Justice Rehnquist dissented, arguing that a procedure not unfair under due process cannot make a sentence cruel and unusual, and would affirm.","disposition":"Vacated and remanded to the Florida Supreme Court with directions to order further trial-court proceedings. Justice Stevens announced the judgment and wrote an opinion joined by Justices Stewart and Powell, and other Justices concurred in the judgment or dissented as described.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep430/usrep430349/usrep430349.pdf"},"1978/78-5283":{"facts":"Jackson was convicted after a bench trial in Virginia of the first-degree murder of Mary Cole, a jail staff member who had befriended him. He did not dispute that he shot her twice at close range with his revolver. He told police that the shooting was accidental, a result of a struggle after she attacked him with a knife, and he also claimed he was too intoxicated to premeditate. The prosecution showed that he had been drinking and shooting at targets, had said he and the victim were about to engage in sexual activity, reloaded after firing warning shots, and drove to North Carolina after the shooting.","procedure":"The Virginia courts rejected his claim that the evidence of premeditation was insufficient. In federal habeas proceedings, the District Court applied the Thompson v. Louisville no-evidence test, found no evidence of premeditation, and granted the writ. The Fourth Circuit applying the same test reversed, finding some evidence of intent, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"In a federal habeas corpus proceeding challenging a state conviction as based on insufficient evidence, must the court ask only whether there was any evidence of guilt, or whether the evidence was enough for a rational trier of fact to find guilt beyond a reasonable doubt?","holding":"The court must ask whether, viewing the evidence in the light most favorable to the prosecution, any rational trier of fact could have found the essential elements of the crime beyond a reasonable doubt. A state prisoner is entitled to habeas relief under section 2254 if no rational trier of fact could have found guilt, and on this record a rational factfinder could have found first-degree murder.","reasoning":"In re Winship established that due process forbids conviction except on proof beyond a reasonable doubt of every element, and that requirement means little unless the factfinder applies it rationally to the evidence. The Thompson no-evidence rule protects only against wholly arbitrary convictions, and a mere modicum of evidence could satisfy it without supporting a rational finding of guilt beyond a reasonable doubt. The reviewing court does not ask whether it itself believes guilt was proved, and it respects the factfinder's role in resolving conflicts, weighing evidence, and drawing inferences, presuming conflicts were resolved for the prosecution. The Court rejected arguments that such claims should be barred or limited as in Stone v. Powell, because insufficiency goes to guilt or innocence, the burden on federal courts was exaggerated, and section 2254 makes federal courts responsible for state convictions that violate the Constitution. Applying the standard, the double shooting at close range, the reloading, the drive to North Carolina inconsistent with extreme intoxication, and the prior statements permitted a finding of premeditation.","separate":"Justice Stevens, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist, concurred in the judgment. He argued that the new rule was unnecessary to decide the case because the evidence was sufficient under any standard, that Winship concerns the trial factfinder's state of mind and does not compel a particular appellate or habeas standard, and that the rule would burden federal and state judges with duplicative review for little benefit.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, White, Marshall, and Blackmun; Justice Powell took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep443/usrep443307/usrep443307.pdf"},"1969/57":{"facts":"In January 1960 three or four masked men robbed six poker players in a Missouri basement. Ashe was charged separately with robbing each player. At his first trial, for robbing Knight, the proof that a robbery occurred was unassailable, but the evidence that Ashe was one of the robbers was weak, and the defense offered no testimony. The jury returned a verdict of not guilty due to insufficient evidence. Six weeks later Ashe was tried for robbing another player, Roberts. The witnesses were largely the same, but their identification testimony was now stronger and the State dropped one unhelpful witness. This time the jury convicted, and he received a 35-year sentence.","procedure":"The Missouri Supreme Court affirmed the conviction and rejected the former-jeopardy plea, and a state collateral attack failed. The federal District Court denied habeas relief, as did the Eighth Circuit, both relying on Hoag v. New Jersey. Benton v. Maryland, decided after those rulings, applied the Fifth Amendment's Double Jeopardy Clause to the States, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Double Jeopardy Clause, applied to the States, bar a second trial for the robbery of a different victim when a jury in the first trial acquitted the defendant by determining that he was not one of the robbers?","holding":"Yes. The Fifth Amendment guarantee against double jeopardy incorporates the rule of collateral estoppel, which bars relitigation between the same parties of an issue of ultimate fact already determined by a valid and final judgment. Because the first jury necessarily decided that Ashe was not one of the robbers, Missouri could not constitutionally try him again on that issue.","reasoning":"Hoag v. New Jersey had asked only whether the second prosecution was fundamentally unfair under due process. After Benton, the question became whether collateral estoppel is part of the Double Jeopardy Clause, which is a matter of constitutional fact decided on the whole record. Collateral estoppel has long been a rule of federal criminal law and is applied with realism, not hypertechnically: where the earlier verdict was general, a court examines the pleadings, evidence, and charge and asks whether a rational jury could have based its verdict on an issue other than the one the defendant seeks to foreclose. Here the only rationally conceivable contested issue was whether Ashe was one of the robbers, and the jury resolved it in his favor. The Court reasoned that whatever else the Clause covers, it protects an acquitted person from having to run the gantlet a second time, and the identity of the second victim had no bearing on that issue. The State had conceded that it treated the first trial as a dry run for the second, which the Clause forbids.","separate":"Justice Black joined the opinion but rejected any suggestion that a fundamental-fairness test could substitute for the Bill of Rights. Justice Harlan joined, noting that he would have followed Hoag under traditional due process but accepted Benton's incorporation, and that the opinion does not embrace a same-transaction concept. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall, concurred and would also hold that the Clause requires the prosecution to join in one trial all charges arising from a single criminal episode. Chief Justice Burger dissented, arguing that the charges involved separate offenses against separate victims under the same-evidence test, that collateral estoppel is poorly suited to criminal cases and was extended on slogans, that the first jury's basis for acquittal was uncertain, and that the same-transaction idea would give later crimes free passes.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the Court of Appeals. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; Justices Black, Harlan, and Brennan (joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall) concurred, and Chief Justice Burger dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep397/usrep397436/usrep397436.pdf"},"1973/73-507":{"facts":"Petitioners, including Hamling, Kemp, Wright, Thomas and two corporations, produced and sold an illustrated version of the official report of the President's Commission on Obscenity and Pornography. To advertise it they mailed to about 55,000 people a brochure with a collage of explicit photographs of heterosexual and homosexual acts and other sexual conduct, together with an order form and text praising the book. The individual petitioners were officers of the corporate petitioners, and evidence tied each of them to the mailing or to the sale of the report.","procedure":"A federal grand jury in the Southern District of California indicted petitioners under 18 U.S.C. sections 2, 371 and 1461. The jury convicted them on twelve counts of mailing and conspiring to mail the obscene advertisement but could not agree on counts concerning the illustrated report itself. The Ninth Circuit affirmed, and after this Court decided Miller v. California it denied rehearing, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Do the obscenity standards of Miller v. California require reversal of convictions obtained before Miller under the Memoirs test, and did the indictment, instructions, and trial rulings otherwise afford petitioners a fair trial under the Constitution?","holding":"No. Section 1461, read as limited to the kind of patently offensive hard-core sexual depictions described in Miller, is not unconstitutionally vague, and the brochure was plainly within that category. The instructions, the indictment, and the trial and jury-selection rulings were not reversible error, so the convictions stood.","reasoning":"Defendants whose convictions were on direct appeal when Miller was decided are entitled to whatever benefit Miller gives them, but the Court found none that required reversal. Miller rejected a uniform national standard as a constitutional requirement without requiring any particular geographic community, so reference to the nation as a whole in the instruction was not reversible absent a probability it affected the jury. Miller's examples of proscribable conduct gave a clarifying gloss to section 1461 rather than creating new crimes, so the statute gave adequate notice, and rejecting the Memoirs social-value test did not make the statute vague. The indictment, which used the statutory term obscene, a legal term of art with a settled meaning, gave sufficient notice. It was enough on scienter that petitioners knew the contents and character of the material they mailed, whatever they believed about its legal status. The Court found no abuse of discretion in excluding allegedly comparable materials, in instructing that the jury could consider appeal to deviant groups and pandering, or in the jury-selection and voir dire rulings, and it found no prejudice from the judge's failure to follow Rule 30 on objections to instructions.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, reasoning that the First Amendment embodies the people's right to know, so that if officials may report on obscenity, nothing allows barring a glossary that factually illustrates the report. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, dissented, adhering to the view that the government may not wholly suppress sexually oriented materials, so that section 1461 as construed is unconstitutionally overbroad and invalid on its face. He also argued that the Court wrongly overruled the long-accepted national standard of decency under the statute in favor of a local one and found other grounds for setting the convictions aside.","disposition":"Affirmed, by a 5-4 vote, in an opinion by Justice Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep418/usrep418087/usrep418087.pdf"},"1976/75-636":{"facts":"The United States sued T.I.M.E.-D.C., a nationwide motor freight carrier, and the International Brotherhood of Teamsters under Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964. It alleged that the company discriminated against Black and Spanish-surnamed employees by steering them into lower-paying city driver and serviceman jobs while line-driver jobs went to whites. It also alleged that the collective-bargaining seniority system locked in the effects of this discrimination, because a city driver who transferred to a line-driver job forfeited all accumulated seniority and started at the bottom of the line drivers' board. The government sought a general injunction and make-whole relief for individual victims.","procedure":"After a trial, the district court held that the company and the union had violated Title VII, enjoined both, and defined an affected class of minority employees entitled to preference for line-driver jobs, with three subclasses receiving different seniority relief. The Fifth Circuit agreed on liability but replaced the remedial scheme with a qualification-date formula and shortened the priority of laid-off line drivers. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the company engage in a pattern or practice of discrimination, did the union violate Title VII by maintaining a seniority system that perpetuated past discrimination, and what individual relief may victims receive?","holding":"The company violated Title VII through a systemwide pattern or practice of discrimination, supported by statistical disparities and individual testimony. The seniority system was protected by section 703(h) because it was bona fide and not adopted with discriminatory intent, so the union did not violate the Act and the injunction against it was vacated. Victims of post-Act discrimination, including nonapplicants who can prove they were deterred by the discriminatory policy, may receive relief including retroactive seniority, but not to a date before the Act's effective date.","reasoning":"Where the government showed that discrimination was the company's standard operating procedure, the pervasive statistical disparities, bolstered by specific instances of discrimination, were not adequately rebutted and supported the findings below. Because a post-Act pattern was proved, retroactive seniority may be awarded as relief even if the seniority agreement does not provide for it. By the language and legislative history of section 703(h), Congress did not intend to make it unlawful for employees with vested seniority rights to keep exercising them even at the expense of pre-Act discriminatees, so a bona fide system that merely perpetuates pre-Act discrimination is lawful, and this system applied equally to all and was free from discriminatory purpose. Every post-Act minority applicant for line-driver work is presumptively entitled to relief unless the company shows its refusal was not discriminatory. A nonapplicant is not barred from relief because a consistently enforced discriminatory policy deters applications, but the government must prove at a remedial hearing which nonapplicants would have applied but for that policy. The district court must then balance the victims' interests against the legitimate expectations of innocent employees.","separate":"Justice Marshall, joined by Justice Brennan, concurred in part and dissented in part. He agreed with parts of the Court's analysis but argued that section 703(h) does not plainly and unmistakably protect seniority systems that perpetuate the effects of intentional past discrimination, since such differences result from prior discrimination, and that the legislative history and later administrative and legislative developments do not show Congress meant to legalize them.","disposition":"The Fifth Circuit's judgment was vacated and the case remanded for further proceedings, in an opinion by Justice Stewart joined by six other Justices, with Justices Marshall and Brennan concurring in part and dissenting in part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep431/usrep431324/usrep431324.pdf"},"1969/79":{"facts":"Sandra Adickes, a white teacher from New York, took six Black students from a Mississippi Freedom School to the Hattiesburg public library, where they were turned away, and then to a S. H. Kress and Company store for lunch. A waitress served the students but refused to serve her, and when she left the store a Hattiesburg police officer arrested her for vagrancy. She alleged that a policeman had been in the store watching when service was refused. She sued Kress under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 for violating her Equal Protection right not to be discriminated against because of race.","procedure":"Her first count alleged refusal of service under a community custom of segregating the races in eating places, and her second alleged a conspiracy between Kress and the police. The district court granted summary judgment on the conspiracy count for want of alleged facts, and directed a verdict on the custom count because she could not prove a specific state-enforced custom of refusing to serve whites accompanied by Black people. The Second Circuit affirmed, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the district court err in granting summary judgment on the conspiracy count, and what must a plaintiff show to establish under section 1983 that a private party refused service under color of a state custom?","holding":"Yes, summary judgment was improper because Kress did not carry its burden of showing no genuine issue of material fact about a conspiracy with the police. On the custom count, a plaintiff makes out a section 1983 equal protection claim by proving she was refused service because of a state-enforced custom of racial segregation in restaurants, and she need not prove a specific custom against serving whites with Black people or a statewide custom.","reasoning":"Section 1983 requires deprivation of a constitutional right and action under color of state law. A private party who is a willful participant in joint activity with state officials, such as a conspiracy with a policeman, acts under color of law, whether or not the officer's acts were authorized. On the record Kress did not rule out that a policeman was in the store, from which a jury could infer an understanding that she not be served, so it failed its initial burden as the movant and she was not required to produce opposing affidavits. As to custom, the statutory words and history mean that the custom or usage must have the force of law through the persistent practices of state officials, and it is not limited to customs enforced by a statute. A private person who discriminates racially knowing of and pursuant to such a state-enforced custom of segregation acts under color of that custom. The relevant custom is the longstanding, still prevailing, state-enforced segregation of public eating places, not a narrow practice about whites accompanied by Black people, and a custom with the force of law in a locality can violate the Fourteenth Amendment without being statewide.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the judgment, concluding that on this record both the conspiracy and custom claims should have gone to the jury, even assuming the trial court's reading of custom or usage was correct. Justice Douglas dissented in part, arguing that the statutory words custom or usage were seriously emasculated by requiring that a custom have the force of law and be enforced by the State. Justice Brennan concurred in part and dissented in part, joining the Court's treatment of the conspiracy count but disagreeing with its reading of the under-color-of-law element and its approach to the custom claim, and addressing the standard for punitive damages. Justice Marshall took no part.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings, in an opinion by Justice Harlan.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep398/usrep398144/usrep398144.pdf"},"1973/72-914":{"facts":"Personal representatives of the estates of three students killed at Kent State University in May 1970 sued the Governor of Ohio, the Adjutant General and his assistant, various officers and enlisted members of the Ohio National Guard, and the university president. They alleged that the defendants, acting under color of state law, “intentionally, recklessly, willfully and wantonly” caused an unnecessary Guard deployment on campus and ordered Guard members to perform illegal acts that caused the deaths. Fairly read, the complaints alleged that each defendant acted outside the scope of office or, within it, arbitrarily and in gross abuse of lawful powers.","procedure":"The complaints sought damages under 42 U.S.C. § 1983, with Ohio-law claims added through pendent or diversity jurisdiction. The district court dismissed for lack of jurisdiction, before any answer, on the theory that the suits were in effect against Ohio and barred by the Eleventh Amendment. The Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit affirmed on that ground and alternatively on the ground that executive immunity was absolute.","question":"Were the damages actions against state officials barred by the Eleventh Amendment, or by an absolute executive immunity, so that dismissal at the pleading stage was proper?","holding":"No. The Eleventh Amendment does not bar damages suits seeking to impose personal liability on state officials for deprivation of federal rights under color of state law, and state executive officers have only a qualified immunity whose scope depends on the office and the circumstances. Dismissal on the pleadings was premature.","reasoning":"On a motion to dismiss the question is whether the plaintiff may offer evidence, and the complaints are read favorably to the pleader, citing Conley v. Gibson. Under Ex parte Young, the Eleventh Amendment is no shield for an official accused of depriving another of a federal right under color of state law, and the complaints sought individual and personal liability rather than payment from the state treasury. As to immunity, Section 1983 reaches misuse of state authority, and its history shows no intent to abolish common-law immunities wholesale, but officials as a class cannot be totally exempt. Immunity rests on the injustice of liability for officers who must exercise discretion and the danger of deterring decisive action. A qualified immunity is available to executive officers, varying with the scope of discretion and responsibilities of the office and all circumstances as they reasonably appeared, together with good-faith belief. The lower courts wrongly accepted the Governor's good faith and took judicial notice of mob rule without any evidence, so further proceedings by summary judgment or trial were required.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion for a unanimous Court; Justice Douglas took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep416/usrep416232/usrep416232.pdf"},"1978/77-926":{"facts":"Geraldine Cannon, a 39-year-old woman, applied to the medical schools of the University of Chicago and Northwestern University, both private institutions that receive federal financial assistance, and was rejected by both. She alleged that she was qualified, that less qualified applicants were admitted, and that the schools' policies against admitting applicants over 30 or 35 excluded women, who more often interrupt their education, without predicting success in medicine. After HEW did not act on her administrative complaint, she sued the schools, claiming among other things sex discrimination in violation of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972. The Court took the complaints' allegations as true because the case came up on motions to dismiss.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the complaints because Title IX does not expressly authorize a private suit and the court declined to infer one. The Seventh Circuit affirmed, reasoning that Congress meant the fund-termination procedure in section 902 to be the exclusive enforcement method and that Title VI, the model for Title IX, carried no implied private action; it adhered to this view on rehearing after Congress passed the 1976 attorney's fees amendment. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on the Title IX question alone.","question":"Does Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, which prohibits sex discrimination in federally funded education programs but does not expressly say so, give a person discriminated against a private right of action against the institution?","holding":"Yes. Applying the four factors from Cort v. Ash, the Court held that Title IX implies a private cause of action for victims of the prohibited discrimination, so Cannon may maintain her suit despite the lack of express authorization in the statute.","reasoning":"First, Title IX is phrased in terms of the persons it protects, and a statute that creates a personal right to be free of discrimination is the kind from which the Court has regularly implied a remedy; Cannon belongs to the protected class. Second, the legislative history points toward a remedy: Title IX was copied from Title VI, its drafters expected it to be enforced as Title VI had been, and by 1972 lower courts had already read Title VI to permit private suits. Congress also showed its understanding in the attorney's fees provision of the 1972 Act and in later fees legislation, and it did not act to change the prevailing assumption. Third, a private action serves the statute's two aims, avoiding the use of federal money for discrimination and protecting individuals, because the fund cutoff is a severe remedy poorly suited to an isolated violation and HEW itself says it lacks the resources to police every complaint and supports the private remedy. Fourth, discrimination is not a matter traditionally left to the states, and the use of federal funds is the basis for the prohibition. The universities' argument that suits over admissions are burdensome is a policy objection that the Congresses enacting Titles VI and IX rejected, and nothing shows that private Title VI suits have overburdened schools or courts. Arguments drawn from the express remedies in other titles of the 1964 Act and from isolated remarks in the Title VI debates do not show an intent to exclude a private remedy.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred in the judgment without opinion. Justice Rehnquist, joined by Justice Stewart, concurred, saying that the question is one of statutory construction, that Congress in the era of the Civil Rights Act relied on courts to decide whether to allow private actions, and that Congress should now specify when it wants one, so the Court should be extremely reluctant to imply causes of action in future. Justice White, joined by Justice Blackmun, dissented, arguing that the history of Title VI and Title IX shows Congress meant enforcement to be carried out by federal agencies, with private suits against state actors already available under section 1983 and none intended against private recipients; he found the lower-court precedents, the fees provision and the cited Court cases inapposite and said the Court's remedy displaces the statutory scheme. Justice Powell dissented, agreeing with White that no private action was intended, and argued further that the Cort analysis violates separation of powers and Article III by letting courts create remedies that belong to Congress, and should be abandoned in favor of requiring the most compelling evidence of congressional intent.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Stevens wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart, Marshall and Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep441/usrep441677/usrep441677.pdf"},"1975/74-1124":{"facts":"Several low-income individuals and organizations representing low-income people sued the Secretary of the Treasury and the Commissioner of Internal Revenue over Revenue Ruling 69-545. That ruling dropped the earlier requirement that a nonprofit hospital serve patients unable to pay, to the extent of its financial ability, in order to qualify as charitable and tax exempt, and it held that a hospital offering only emergency care to indigents could qualify. The individual plaintiffs described occasions when they were refused hospital services or billed despite their indigency at hospitals that had been held tax exempt and received substantial private contributions. They alleged that the ruling encouraged hospitals to deny services to the poor, that it misread the Internal Revenue Code, and that it was issued without the notice and comment procedures of the Administrative Procedure Act. No hospital was a defendant.","procedure":"The District Court denied the government's motion to dismiss, which challenged standing, and on summary judgment held the ruling void as contrary to the Code. The D.C. Circuit also found jurisdiction but reversed on the merits, upholding the ruling as a permissible reading of the Code and as an interpretive rule exempt from rulemaking procedures. The Supreme Court granted both sides' petitions and consolidated them, and decided only the government's jurisdictional challenge.","question":"Did the indigent plaintiffs and the organizations representing them allege an injury fairly traceable to the Treasury officials' issuance of Revenue Ruling 69-545 and likely to be redressed by a favorable decision, as Article III standing requires?","holding":"No. The plaintiffs lacked standing because it was speculative whether the hospitals' denials of service resulted from the ruling rather than from the hospitals' own independent decisions, and speculative whether the relief sought would lead hospitals to serve the plaintiffs. The complaint should have been dismissed.","reasoning":"Standing asks whether the plaintiff has shown an injury to himself that is likely to be redressed by a favorable decision, and a federal court acting without that showing would exceed its Article III role. The organizations alleged no injury to themselves and could not rest standing on an abstract interest in the health of the poor; they could sue only as representatives of members actually injured. The plaintiffs did suffer injury in the sense of being denied hospital care, but the injury came from the hospitals, which were not before the court, and a federal court may redress only injury fairly traceable to the defendant rather than to independent third-party action. The allegation that the ruling encouraged hospitals to refuse indigents left it speculative whether the refusals stemmed from the ruling or from the hospitals' own choices. It was also speculative whether a court order would produce service for these plaintiffs, since hospitals might give up the tax benefits instead; the allegation that the hospitals received substantial deductible contributions did not show dependence on them. Linda R. S. v. Richard D. and Warth v. Seldin control, and United States v. SCRAP and Data Processing are distinguishable because the plaintiffs there alleged injury directly traceable to the challenged action. The Court did not reach whether a third party may ever challenge the IRS treatment of another or whether statutory or sovereign immunity bars applied.","separate":"Justice Stewart filed a concurring statement joining the opinion and adding that, outside the First Amendment area, he could not imagine a case in which a person whose own tax liability was unaffected could have standing to litigate someone else's tax liability. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, concurred in the judgment but rejected the Court's standing reasoning. He would have decided the case on the ground that the challenge to the ruling was unripe or unsupported because the ruling on its face applies to a narrow category of hospitals and the plaintiffs never showed that the hospitals affecting them fell within it, so the government was entitled to summary judgment. He argued that the Court wrongly imposed demanding pleading and proof requirements on the chain of causation, conflicted with Data Processing, Barlow and SCRAP, and wrongly put its holding on the Article III minimum, beyond congressional power to correct.","disposition":"Judgment of the Court of Appeals vacated and the case remanded to the District Court with instructions to dismiss the complaint. Justice Powell wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Blackmun and Rehnquist; Justice Stevens took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep426/usrep426026/usrep426026.pdf"},"1971/70-40":{"facts":"Georgia's 1968 abortion statute, modeled on the American Law Institute's Model Penal Code, made abortion a crime punishable by one to ten years in prison unless performed by a licensed Georgia physician who judged an abortion necessary because the pregnancy endangered the woman's life or seriously and permanently injured her health, the fetus would likely be born with a grave defect, or the pregnancy resulted from rape. The statute also required that the woman be a Georgia resident, that the abortion take place in a hospital accredited by the Joint Commission on Accreditation of Hospitals, that a hospital staff abortion committee approve it in advance, and that two other licensed physicians examine the woman and concur in the performing physician's judgment. Mary Doe, a pseudonym for a poor, married Georgia woman who was about eight weeks pregnant, already had three children and could not support another. Her request for an abortion was denied by a hospital committee because her situation did not fit the statutory grounds.","procedure":"Doe, other individuals including physicians, nurses, clergy and social workers, and two nonprofit corporations sued Georgia officials in federal court for a declaration that the statute was unconstitutional and for an injunction. A three-judge District Court held that all plaintiffs had standing but that only Doe presented a justiciable controversy, struck down the limit to three specified grounds and a few other provisions as infringing privacy, upheld the remaining procedural requirements, and denied an injunction. The plaintiffs appealed directly to the Supreme Court, and the case was argued and reargued alongside Roe v. Wade.","question":"Do the Georgia abortion statute's requirements of a physician's best clinical judgment, accredited-hospital performance, hospital committee approval, concurrence of two other physicians, and state residence, violate the Constitution?","holding":"The requirement that an abortion rest on the physician's best clinical judgment is not unconstitutionally vague. But the requirements of Joint Commission accreditation of the hospital, hospital committee approval, confirmation by two other physicians, and Georgia residence violate the Fourteenth Amendment. The woman's right to an abortion is not absolute, as stated in Roe v. Wade.","reasoning":"Under Roe v. Wade, Doe's case is live and she has standing, and the physician plaintiffs, who are the people the criminal statute directly threatens, may sue without first being prosecuted; the Court did not need to decide the standing of the other plaintiffs. The best-clinical-judgment standard is not vague because, as in United States v. Vuitch, physicians routinely make judgments of necessity, and the judgment may take account of physical, emotional, psychological and familial factors and age relevant to the patient's wellbeing. The accreditation requirement is unrelated to abortion as such and the State did not show that only hospitals, let alone accredited ones, can protect the patient's health; a hospital requirement that does not exclude the first trimester also fails under Roe. Committee approval is redundant because the medical judgment has already been made, it is not required for any other surgery, and it substantially limits the woman's and the physician's rights, while the hospital is already protected by its freedom to decline abortions and by conscience provisions. The two-physician confirmation has no rational connection with the patient's needs and unduly infringes the attending physician's right to practice, since a licensed physician is presumed competent to judge. The residence rule is not tied to preserving state facilities and, like the Privileges and Immunities Clause protection of those who enter a state to work, must protect persons who enter Georgia for medical services. With the three procedural requirements gone, the equal protection argument that the system discriminates against the poor largely falls away, and the Court declined to decide on injunctive relief.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred, noting some discomfort at the Court's reliance on scientific data but not exceeding judicial notice, saying he would allow a state to require certification by two physicians but not the complex Georgia scheme, and stating that the Court rejects any claim of a constitutional right to abortion on demand. Justice Douglas concurred, discussing the constitutional sources of a right of privacy, including liberty to make basic decisions about procreation and care of one's health, and arguing that the statute is overbroad, that the state may regulate abortion as a medical matter only through standards for physicians, and that the multiple-physician approval system destroys the privacy of the physician-patient relationship. Justice White, joined by Justice Rehnquist, dissented, finding no support in the Constitution's text or history for a right to abortion for reasons of convenience and arguing that the issue belongs to the people and legislatures; he would reverse the District Court. Justice Rehnquist also dissented separately, objecting to the compelling-state-interest test used to examine the Georgia provisions.","disposition":"The District Court's judgment was modified to strike the accreditation, committee approval, two-physician confirmation and residence requirements, and as modified was affirmed. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Douglas, Brennan, Stewart, Marshall and Powell.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep410/usrep410179/usrep410179.pdf"},"1975/74-1303":{"facts":"Bishop was hired in 1969 as a probationary policeman for the city of Marion, North Carolina, and became a permanent employee after six months. In 1972 the City Manager, on the Chief of Police's recommendation, dismissed him without a hearing, telling him privately that the reasons were failure to follow orders, poor attendance at training classes, causing low morale, and conduct unsuited to an officer. A city ordinance said a permanent employee who failed to meet the standards of his classification, or was negligent, inefficient or unfit, could be dismissed by the City Manager, and that a discharged employee would be given written reasons on request. Bishop and other officers filed affidavits denying the charges.","procedure":"Bishop sued under 42 U.S.C. § 1983 for reinstatement and backpay, claiming deprivation of property and liberty without due process. The District Court granted summary judgment for the defendants, concluding that under state law he held his position at the will and pleasure of the city. The Court of Appeals affirmed, the en banc court being equally divided, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did Bishop's status as a permanent employee give him a property interest in continued employment protected by the Fourteenth Amendment, and did a false explanation for his discharge, given privately, deprive him of liberty?","holding":"Under the lower courts' tenable reading of state law, Bishop held his job at the city's will and so had no property interest. Assuming the stated reasons were false, the private communication of them did not deprive him of liberty, because they were not made public and truth or falsity does not change the liberty analysis.","reasoning":"Property interests are created by independent sources such as state law, so whether the ordinance created entitlement is a question of North Carolina law. There was no authoritative state-court interpretation, and the Court said the District Court's reading, that dismissal required no hearing or review and the employee had only procedural rights that were observed, was tenable, had some support in a North Carolina Supreme Court decision, and was accepted by the Court of Appeals, so the Court would not independently re-examine it. On liberty, discharge of an at-will employee without public disclosure of reasons is like the nonretention in Board of Regents v. Roth. The private oral explanation was not published, and the later answers to interrogatories came in litigation after the injury and could not supply retroactive support. Even if the reasons were false, their truth affects only whether the decision was prudent, not whether liberty was impaired; the Court said the Due Process Clause is not a guarantee against incorrect or ill-advised personnel decisions and federal courts are not the forum for reviewing routine personnel decisions.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented: the stigmatizing reasons were disclosed and would likely reach prospective employers, so Bishop should have a hearing to clear his name; he criticized Paul v. Davis; and he argued that federal law informs the definition of property, so the question should be what the employee could reasonably expect, requiring remand for fact development. Justice White, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall and Blackmun, dissented: the ordinance conditions dismissal on cause, and the majority revived the plurality view in Arnett v. Kennedy, rejected by six Justices there, that the procedures attached to a statutory right define the process due. Justice Blackmun, joined by Justice Brennan, dissented, joining White and adding that the North Carolina decision relied on was not authoritative and the ordinance's for-cause standard created a protected expectation of continued employment.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stevens delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Powell and Rehnquist (5-4).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep426/usrep426341/usrep426341.pdf"},"1971/70-27":{"facts":"A Florida prosecuting attorney brought a state court proceeding to close Mitchum's bookstore as a public nuisance, and the state court entered a preliminary order barring the store's operation. After further inconclusive state proceedings, Mitchum sued in federal court. He alleged that state judicial and law enforcement officials, by applying Florida law unconstitutionally, were depriving him of First and Fourteenth Amendment rights and causing irreparable harm.","procedure":"Relying on 42 U.S.C. § 1983, Mitchum sought injunctive and declaratory relief against the state court proceeding. A three-judge District Court dissolved the temporary restraining orders and refused to enjoin, holding that the relief was not within any exception to the federal anti-injunction statute, 28 U.S.C. § 2283, because it was not expressly authorized by Act of Congress. Mitchum appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Is 42 U.S.C. § 1983 an Act of Congress that 'expressly authorizes' an exception to the anti-injunction statute, so that a federal court in a § 1983 suit may enjoin a pending state court proceeding?","holding":"Section 1983 falls within the 'expressly authorized' exception of 28 U.S.C. § 2283, so the anti-injunction statute is not an absolute bar to a federal injunction against state court proceedings in a § 1983 action. The Court did not question the principles of equity, comity and federalism that restrain federal courts in such cases.","reasoning":"Under Atlantic Coast Line, the anti-injunction statute is an absolute ban unless a statutory exception applies, so the question was critical, and a contrary answer would also have undercut Younger v. Harris and its companion cases. Because Congress in 1948 restored the law as understood before Toucey, the Court applied its earlier criteria: an authorizing statute need not refer to § 2283 or expressly mention injunctions against state courts, but must create a specific, uniquely federal right or remedy, enforceable in a federal court of equity, that could be given its intended scope only by staying a state proceeding. Section 1983 was enacted in 1871 to enforce the Fourteenth Amendment against state action, including judicial action, and the debates show Congress knew state courts were failing to protect federal rights. Its purpose was to place the federal courts between the States and the people as guardians of federal rights, and it expressly authorizes a 'suit in equity.' The Court therefore held that § 1983 qualifies, while leaving intact the restraints of equity, comity and federalism.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger, joined by Justices White and Blackmun, concurred. He stressed that the Court held only that § 2283 does not bar such an injunction, and said that on remand the District Court should consider whether equity or federalism principles like those in Younger v. Harris prevent an injunction against the state nuisance-abatement proceedings, since the restraint applicable to civil proceedings had not been decided.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by all participating Justices; Justices Powell and Rehnquist took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep407/usrep407225/usrep407225.pdf"},"1974/73-1908":{"facts":"In 1972, Bethlehem Steel Corporation paid from general corporate funds for advertisements and mailings built around a speech by its chairman, Stewart Cort, that responded to a presidential candidate's statements about corporate taxes and urged \"truth squads\" in the campaign. Respondent Ash, who owned 50 shares of Bethlehem stock, sued Bethlehem's directors, claiming the spending violated 18 U.S.C. § 610, a criminal statute barring corporate contributions or expenditures in connection with federal elections. He sought an injunction against future spending and damages on behalf of the corporation.","procedure":"The District Court denied a preliminary injunction, and after Ash dropped his pendent Delaware-law claim rather than post security, it granted summary judgment to the defendants. The Third Circuit reversed, holding that a private cause of action under § 610 was proper for a citizen seeking an injunction or a stockholder seeking injunctive or derivative damage relief. The Supreme Court granted certiorari; meanwhile Congress had enacted the 1974 Federal Election Campaign Act Amendments creating the Federal Election Commission.","question":"Is a private cause of action, for injunction or derivative damages, to be implied in favor of a corporate stockholder under 18 U.S.C. § 610, a criminal statute that provides no civil remedy?","holding":"No. The 1974 Amendments, an intervening law, send complaints seeking an injunction against future § 610 violations to the Federal Election Commission, and a stockholder's derivative suit for damages for the 1972 violation cannot be implied from § 610 itself, leaving any remedy to Delaware corporate law.","reasoning":"A court must apply the law in effect when it decides, and the Amendments gave the Commission primary jurisdiction over complaints of § 610 violations and a route to injunctive relief through the Attorney General, so a private complainant must use that remedy for future elections. On damages, the Court set out four factors: whether the plaintiff is one of the class for whose especial benefit the statute was enacted, whether there is legislative intent to create or deny a remedy, whether a remedy is consistent with the statute's purposes, and whether the cause of action is one traditionally relegated to state law. Protecting stockholders was at best a secondary purpose of § 610, which aimed mainly at the corrupting influence of corporate wealth on elections. Nothing in the legislative history suggested a federal damages right, and repayment to the corporation would not undo or likely deter the initial misuse of funds. Corporate internal affairs are traditionally governed by state law, which may supply an ultra vires or fiduciary duty claim.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep422/usrep422066/usrep422066.pdf"},"1971/70-5039":{"facts":"Margarita Fuentes bought a gas stove and a stereo from Firestone under conditional sales contracts and paid for over a year. After a dispute over servicing of the stove, Firestone sued for repossession and, on the same day and before she had been served, obtained a writ of replevin from a court clerk on a form and bond; a deputy sheriff then seized the goods from her home. In the companion Pennsylvania case, other buyers and one mother in a custody dispute with her ex-husband, a deputy sheriff, had household goods seized under similar writs obtained ex parte on the posting of a bond, in some cases without any court action ever being filed by the seizing party.","procedure":"The plaintiffs sued in federal court under 42 U.S.C. § 1983, challenging the Florida and Pennsylvania prejudgment replevin statutes under the Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process Clause. Three-judge District Courts in both cases upheld the statutes, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on appeal.","question":"Do state replevin statutes that let a private party obtain a writ for seizure of goods ex parte, on posting a bond and without notice or a prior hearing, deprive the possessor of property without due process of law?","holding":"Yes. The Florida and Pennsylvania prejudgment replevin provisions violate the Fourteenth Amendment insofar as they deny the possessor a prior opportunity to be heard before chattels are taken. The holding is narrow and leaves States free to design the form of prior hearing.","reasoning":"The central meaning of procedural due process is that parties whose rights are affected are entitled to be heard at a meaningful time, which here means before the seizure, when the deprivation can still be prevented rather than undone by a later hearing or damages. A bond and the applicant's conclusory allegation test only the applicant's own confidence and are no substitute for a neutral official's evaluation. The deprivation counts even though it may be temporary, even though the buyers lacked full title, and even though the household goods were not necessities, because any significant possessory interest is protected. Prior hearings may be postponed only in extraordinary situations involving an important governmental or public interest, a special need for prompt action, and strict state control through a government official under a narrowly drawn statute, and these statutes serve only private gain and leave the State acting largely in the dark. The fine-print repossession clauses were not a waiver, since they did not mention a prior hearing or the process of repossession and were part of a form contract.","separate":"Justice White, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Blackmun, dissented. He would have vacated and remanded for reconsideration under Younger v. Harris because state replevin proceedings were pending. On the merits, he argued that creditors have a protectable property interest too, that a mistaken claim of default is unlikely, that the decision calls into question the secured-transactions laws of nearly every State, and that creditors could avoid its effect through contract terms or short notice and a probable-cause hearing.","disposition":"Judgments of the District Courts vacated and cases remanded. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Douglas, Brennan and Marshall; Justices Powell and Rehnquist took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep407/usrep407067/usrep407067.pdf"},"1974/73-762":{"facts":"Carol Sosna moved from New York to Iowa in August 1972 with her three children and in September petitioned an Iowa court for dissolution of her marriage. Her husband, who remained in New York, was served when he visited Iowa and made a special appearance to contest jurisdiction. Iowa Code § 598.6 required a petitioner to have been \"for the last year a resident of the state,\" and the Iowa court dismissed her petition because she had not met that requirement.","procedure":"Rather than appeal in the Iowa courts, Sosna sued in the Northern District of Iowa under Rule 23 on behalf of a class of Iowa residents of under a year who wished to start divorce actions, seeking injunctive and declaratory relief against the State and the state judge. The parties stipulated to class treatment, the court approved it, and a three-judge court upheld the statute. By the time of appeal she had long satisfied the one-year period and had obtained a divorce in New York; the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction and asked the parties to address Younger v. Harris.","question":"Is a class action challenging a state durational residency requirement for divorce moot once the named plaintiff has satisfied the requirement, and does a one-year residency requirement for divorce violate equal protection or due process?","holding":"The case is not moot, because after certification the class acquired a legal status separate from the named plaintiff and the controversy remains live for its unnamed members. On the merits, Iowa's one-year residency requirement for divorce is constitutional.","reasoning":"Had Sosna sued only for herself, the case would be moot, but the Court held that after certification \"the class of unnamed persons described in the certification acquired a legal status separate from the interest asserted by appellant.\" As in Dunn v. Blumstein, the state would keep enforcing the statute against class members although no single challenger would stay subject to it long enough to finish the litigation, and the named plaintiff had been a class member with a real and immediate injury at certification. The Court also found that Rule 23(a) adequacy of representation was met. On the merits, domestic relations are traditionally a state province, and 48 States impose some durational requirement. Shapiro, Dunn and Maricopa County were distinguished because those plaintiffs were irretrievably denied benefits, while Sosna faced only delay, and because Iowa's justification was not mere budget or administrative convenience. Iowa may require genuine attachment given the stakes for both spouses and any children, may avoid becoming a divorce mill, and may reduce the risk of collateral attack in other States under full faith and credit. No due process violation arose, since individualized proof of domicile would not itself have entitled her to a divorce under a one-year rule, and unlike Boddie there was no total denial of access.","separate":"Justice White dissented, arguing that the case was moot: Article III requires a named plaintiff with a live personal stake, certification does not substitute for one, and the Court should at least have remanded to consider mootness and adequacy of representation. Justice Marshall, joined by Justice Brennan, dissented on the merits, contending that the right to seek divorce is fundamental so that penalizing recent interstate migrants requires a compelling interest, and that a domicile requirement would protect Iowa's interests adequately.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Douglas, Stewart, Blackmun and Powell.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep419/usrep419393/usrep419393.pdf"},"1973/72-5847":{"facts":"Harrell Alexander, a Black worker, was fired from his job as a drill operator at Gardner-Denver in September 1969 for producing too many defective parts. He grieved the discharge under the collective-bargaining agreement, which barred discrimination and just-cause discharge and provided for binding arbitration, and raised a race discrimination claim at the last pre-arbitration step. Before the arbitration hearing he filed a charge with the Colorado Civil Rights Commission, which referred it to the EEOC. The arbitrator ruled the discharge was for just cause without mentioning discrimination, and the EEOC later found no reasonable cause.","procedure":"After receiving a right-to-sue notice, Alexander sued under Title VII in the District of Colorado. The district court granted summary judgment, holding that by electing to arbitrate he was bound by the arbitral decision and barred from suing. The Tenth Circuit affirmed per curiam, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Is an employee's right to a trial de novo under Title VII foreclosed by prior submission of the claim to final arbitration under the nondiscrimination clause of a collective-bargaining agreement?","holding":"No. An employee's statutory right to a trial de novo under Title VII is not foreclosed by prior submission of the claim to arbitration under a collective-bargaining agreement. The federal court may admit the arbitral decision as evidence and give it appropriate weight.","reasoning":"Title VII gives federal courts final enforcement responsibility, treats the private suit as essential, and was meant to supplement rather than supplant other remedies, so nothing in the statute makes an arbitral decision preclusive. Election of remedies does not apply, because the contractual right to arbitrate and the statutory right to equal employment opportunity are distinct even if one event violates both. There can be no prospective waiver of Title VII rights by a union, and merely resorting to arbitration is not a waiver. An arbitrator's authority is confined to the collective-bargaining agreement and does not extend to enforcing statutory rights, so he has \"no general authority to invoke public laws that conflict with the bargain between the parties.\" The employer does not have \"two strings to his bow\" inequity argument because Title VII gives employers no cause of action, and employers will still value arbitration for the no-strike pledge. A deferral rule would also be inappropriate because arbitration is less suited to Title VII issues: arbitrators know the law of the shop, factfinding is less complete, and the union controls the grievance. Courts may weigh the arbitral award according to the circumstances, including procedural fairness and the adequacy of the record.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Powell delivered the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep415/usrep415036/usrep415036.pdf"},"1978/78-690":{"facts":"Reiter sued on behalf of herself and all people in the United States who bought hearing aids made by five manufacturers. She alleged that the manufacturers committed antitrust violations, including vertical and horizontal price fixing and restrictions on their retail dealers, so that she and the class paid illegally inflated prices for hearing aids and related services. She sought treble damages under § 4 of the Clayton Act and injunctive relief under § 16. Section 4 allows suit by \"[a]ny person who shall be injured in his business or property\" by an antitrust violation.","procedure":"The manufacturers moved to dismiss, arguing that a retail consumer buying for personal use is not injured in her \"business or property.\" The District Court held that a purchaser is injured in property if violations raised the price paid, and certified the question for interlocutory appeal. The Eighth Circuit reversed, holding that consumers alleging no injury of a commercial or business nature lack standing under § 4, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Do consumers who pay a higher price for goods bought for personal use because of antitrust violations suffer an injury to their \"business or property\" within the meaning of § 4 of the Clayton Act?","holding":"Yes. A consumer whose money has been diminished by reason of an antitrust violation is injured in her \"property\" under § 4, even if she bought for personal use and lost only money.","reasoning":"The Court began with the statutory text. The word property naturally has a broad meaning covering anything of material value, and money is property. The disjunctive or shows that business was not meant to modify property, and the respondents' reading would have turned business into an adjective and stripped property of its independent meaning. The phrase still keeps restrictive force, for example by excluding personal injuries. Chattanooga Foundry held that a city paying inflated prices for pipe was injured in its property, so monetary injury alone can qualify. Consumer standing was implicit in Goldfarb and in many other cases referring to consumers, and Hawaii v. Standard Oil, with its language about commercial interests, did not hold otherwise. The legislative history, though not specific, shows the treble-damages remedy was meant to protect consumers from overcharges, and nobody questioned a consumer's right to sue. Concerns about crowded dockets and burdens on small businesses are policy matters for Congress, because \"We must take the statute as we find it,\" and district courts can use Rule 23 to screen frivolous or cumbersome class actions.","separate":"Justice Rehnquist concurred, joining the opinion but adding that the Court of Appeals' concern about added litigation and unfair settlements is well founded, that exhortations to district courts are not a complete solution, and that it is doubtful consumers rather than class attorneys benefit most; any problem is for Congress.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion of the Court, in which all other Members joined except Justice Brennan, who took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep442/usrep442330/usrep442330.pdf"},"1975/74-1042":{"facts":"Ernst & Ernst, an accounting firm, audited the books of First Securities Company of Chicago, a small brokerage, from 1946 through 1967 and prepared reports the firm had to file with the SEC and the Midwest Stock Exchange. Respondents, customers of First Securities, invested in fictitious \"escrow\" accounts promoted by Leston Nay, the firm's president and 92% owner, who converted the money to his own use. The fraud surfaced in 1968 when Nay committed suicide and left a note calling the accounts spurious. Respondents claimed Ernst & Ernst negligently failed to audit properly and so missed Nay's rule that only he could open mail addressed to him, and they expressly disclaimed any allegation of fraud or intentional misconduct by the firm.","procedure":"Respondents sued Ernst & Ernst in the Northern District of Illinois under section 10(b) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 and SEC Rule 10b-5, alleging that it aided and abetted Nay's fraud. The District Court granted summary judgment for Ernst & Ernst, finding no genuine issue about whether its audits met generally accepted standards. The Seventh Circuit reversed, holding that a party who breaches a duty of inquiry and disclosure is liable as an aider and abettor if the fraud would have been discovered or prevented but for the breach.","question":"Does a private action for damages lie under section 10(b) and Rule 10b-5 when the defendant is not alleged to have acted with scienter, that is, with intent to deceive, manipulate, or defraud?","holding":"No. A private damages action under section 10(b) and Rule 10b-5 requires an allegation of scienter; negligence alone is not enough. The Court did not decide whether recklessness suffices or whether scienter is required in an SEC injunctive action.","reasoning":"The Court began with the statutory text. The words \"manipulative or deceptive device or contrivance,\" and especially \"manipulative,\" which is nearly a term of art in securities markets, point to intentional or willful conduct rather than negligence. It rejected the SEC's effects-based argument, noting that the same logic would impose liability for wholly faultless conduct, and held that the Acts' remedial purpose does not justify departing from the statutory words. The legislative history, though silent on the point explicitly, shows no intent to reach negligent conduct. The statement of a drafters' spokesman described the provision as a catchall against new manipulative devices, and the committee reports tied liability to bad faith. The structure of the 1933 and 1934 Acts also pointed the same way. Where Congress created express civil liability it specified the required state of mind, and the express negligence-based remedies carry procedural limits (costs bonds, short limitation periods) that would be nullified if the same claims could proceed under section 10(b). Rule 10b-5 could not be read more broadly, because its administrative history shows it was aimed at fraud and because an agency's rulemaking power cannot exceed the statute that grants it. Remand was refused because respondents had proceeded throughout on a negligence theory and disclaimed intentional misconduct.","separate":"Justice Blackmun, joined by Justice Brennan, dissented. He argued that the language of subsections (b) and (c) of Rule 10b-5 is broad enough to reach negligent omissions and misstatements, that securities laws should be construed flexibly to serve their remedial purposes, and that the SEC and the Solicitor General took the same view. He saw no principled reason why negligence would violate the Rule in an SEC suit but not in a private suit, and he stressed the importance of the auditor's role. Justice Stevens took no part.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Powell wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Marshall, and Rehnquist (6-2, with Justice Stevens not participating).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep425/usrep425185/usrep425185.pdf"},"1974/73-1543":{"facts":"Willie Johnson, Jr., a Black employee of Railway Express Agency in Memphis, filed a timely charge with the EEOC on May 31, 1967, alleging race discrimination in seniority rules and job assignments, and later added a claim that he was fired on June 20, 1967 because of his race. The EEOC issued an investigation report in December 1967 but did not find reasonable cause until March 1970 and did not issue a right-to-sue notice until January 1971. Johnson then filed in federal court in 1971, asserting claims under Title VII and 42 U.S.C. section 1981, more than three and a half years after his cause of action accrued.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the section 1981 claims as barred by Tennessee's one-year statute of limitations and dismissed the remaining claims on other grounds. The Sixth Circuit affirmed, rejecting the argument that the limitation period was suspended while the EEOC charge was pending. The Supreme Court granted certiorari limited to the limitation question.","question":"Does the timely filing of a Title VII charge with the EEOC toll the state limitation period applicable to a later action under 42 U.S.C. section 1981 based on the same facts?","holding":"No. Title VII and section 1981 provide separate, independent remedies, and filing an EEOC charge does not toll the borrowed state limitation period for a section 1981 action. Johnson's section 1981 suit was therefore time barred.","reasoning":"The Court first held that section 1981 reaches private employment discrimination and that Congress intended it to coexist with Title VII, so the remedies are \"separate, distinct, and independent,\" and a Title VII charge is not a prerequisite to a section 1981 suit. Because section 1981 has no federal limitation period, the Court borrowed the most appropriate state period, here Tennessee's one-year statute, and held that the state's tolling rules go with it, since the length of a period reflects a value judgment tied to its exceptions. Borrowed state law yields only if inconsistent with federal policy. The Court acknowledged that failing to toll may push claimants into court before EEOC conciliation ends, but answered that a plaintiff can ask for a stay and that Congress kept section 1981 independent, so the plaintiff could have sued at any time and in effect \"slept on his § 1981 rights.\" American Pipe and Burnett were distinguished because they involved federal limitation periods, a body of federal procedural law and conflicting federal policy, and prior filings of the identical cause of action.","separate":"Justice Marshall, joined by Justices Douglas and Brennan, concurred in Parts I-III (the independence of the two remedies) but dissented from Parts IV and V. He argued that federal courts apply federal equity principles to the effect of a borrowed limitation period, that federal policy favoring EEOC conciliation weighs strongly in favor of tolling, and that tolling would not revive stale claims because the respondents had notice through the EEOC charge. He would have reversed on the tolling point.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Powell, and Rehnquist in full, and by Justices Douglas, Brennan, and Marshall as to Parts I-III.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep421/usrep421454/usrep421454.pdf"},"1972/71-1369":{"facts":"Respondents Rodriguez, Katzoff, and Kritsky were New York state prisoners in a conditional-release program under which they earned good-behavior credits toward their maximum sentences. Prison officials canceled credits through disciplinary proceedings: 120 days for Rodriguez (possession of contraband), 50 days for Katzoff (derogatory diary entries), and 545 days for Kritsky (alleged leadership in a prison protest, imposed summarily). Each alleged the cancellation violated the Constitution and sought restoration of the credits, which would have entitled him to immediate release on parole.","procedure":"Each respondent filed a civil rights action under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 joined with a habeas corpus petition. The District Courts treated the habeas claim as an adjunct, so no exhaustion of state remedies was required, and ruled for the prisoners on the merits. The Second Circuit, sitting en banc, affirmed, relying on Wilwording v. Swenson, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a state prisoner who challenges the fact or duration of his confinement, by attacking the cancellation of good-conduct credits, obtain restoration of the credits through a section 1983 action without first exhausting state remedies, or is federal habeas corpus his exclusive federal remedy?","holding":"When a state prisoner challenges the very fact or duration of his physical imprisonment and seeks immediate or speedier release, his sole federal remedy is a writ of habeas corpus, with its exhaustion requirement. Section 1983 remains available for challenges to prison conditions and for damages claims.","reasoning":"The Court reasoned that \"the essence of habeas corpus is an attack by a person in custody upon the legality of that custody,\" and that respondents' claims fell within that traditional core even if restoration of credits would only shorten, rather than end, their confinement. Although section 1983 literally covers such claims, the more specific habeas statute, which Congress amended in 1948 to require exhaustion of adequate state remedies, controls over the general one, as respondents conceded it does for challenges to a conviction itself. The Court rejected the argument that exhaustion rests only on respect for state courts, finding comity equally implicated in state prison administration, which the States have a strong interest in correcting first. It addressed respondents' concerns about damages (a damages claim may proceed under section 1983 without exhaustion), delay (federal habeas is summary, while a section 1983 suit is a plenary action), and res judicata. Earlier cases such as Cooper, Houghton, Wilwording, and Haines were distinguished because those prisoners challenged conditions, not the fact or length of custody, and were reaffirmed on that understanding.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall, dissented. He argued that Wilwording controlled and that the Court had invented an unworkable \"core of habeas corpus\" distinction that would produce anomalies, such as split proceedings in state and federal court when a prisoner challenges both conditions and good-time credits. He contended that the no-exhaustion rule is an integral part of section 1983, that exhaustion in habeas protects relations between state and federal courts rather than state administrative action, and that nothing showed Congress meant habeas to displace section 1983 here.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Blackmun, Powell, and Rehnquist (6-3).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep411/usrep411475/usrep411475.pdf"},"1970/36":{"facts":"Fred Mackey was convicted in January 1964 of five counts of income tax evasion for 1956 through 1960, in violation of 26 U.S.C. section 7201. The Government proved evasion by the net-worth method and introduced 60 monthly wagering excise tax returns that Mackey, who ran a policy wheel, had filed as the statute required, plus a summary exhibit and testimony that reported gross wagers less expenses exceeded the gambling profits on his income tax returns. His counsel objected that the returns were prejudicial, inflammatory, and irrelevant, but not on Fifth Amendment grounds. The Seventh Circuit affirmed in 1965. In January 1968 the Court decided Marchetti and Grosso, holding that the Fifth Amendment privilege was a defense to prosecution for failing to register as a gambler and pay the wagering taxes.","procedure":"After Marchetti and Grosso, Mackey, who was serving his sentence, moved under 28 U.S.C. section 2255 to vacate it, arguing the Fifth Amendment barred use of the wagering tax returns at his trial. The District Court denied the motion, and the Seventh Circuit affirmed, holding that Marchetti and Grosso would not be applied retroactively to upset a conviction obtained under the then-governing law.","question":"Do the Fifth Amendment holdings of Marchetti and Grosso apply retroactively to invalidate, on collateral review, a conviction at which compelled wagering tax returns were used as evidence of unreported income in a tax evasion prosecution?","holding":"No, the judgment denying relief was affirmed, but there was no majority rationale. A four-Justice plurality held that Marchetti and Grosso are not retroactive because they raise no doubt about the reliability of the verdict. Justice Harlan would apply the law in effect when the conviction became final on collateral review, and Justices Brennan and Marshall concluded that the Fifth Amendment does not bar use of the returns in a tax evasion prosecution.","reasoning":"The plurality (Justice White) relied on the Court's retroactivity line from Linkletter through Desist, and on Tehan v. Shott and Johnson v. New Jersey, which declined to apply new Fifth Amendment rules retroactively. Marchetti and Grosso broadened the privilege's reach without being motivated by concern that wagering returns were unreliable, and Mackey did not claim his returns were inaccurate, so the returns resembled reliable physical evidence seized under a later-changed Fourth Amendment rule. Because the old rule had been expressly approved in Kahriger and Lewis and the new one did not undermine the accuracy of the verdict, prospective application was enough. Justice Harlan, in a separate opinion that also addressed the companion Williams and Elkanich cases, rejected the Court's retroactivity approach as legislative in character. He would apply new rules to all cases on direct review but, on habeas, generally apply the law in force when the conviction became final, with exceptions for new substantive due process rules and for procedures implicit in ordered liberty. Applying that approach, he could not say that reliance on the pre-Marchetti rule worked an inexcusable inequity against Mackey.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred in the judgment in this case in an opinion that also covered Nos. 81 and 82 (Williams and Elkanich). He argued that retroactivity on direct review must follow ordinary judicial principles, that on collateral review it concerns the scope and purposes of habeas corpus and finality favors applying the law as it stood when the conviction became final, with exceptions for substantive due process rules and procedures implicit in ordered liberty. He wrote that \"We sit as a court of law, not a council of revision.\" Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, concurred in the judgment on a different ground: the Government may enforce the tax laws through compelled reporting of income, so the Fifth Amendment protects against use of the information to prosecute gambling offenses but not against its use in a tax evasion prosecution. Justice Douglas, joined by Justice Black, dissented, saying he found nothing in the Constitution allowing some constitutional rules to be retroactive and others only prospective, and that Mackey should be treated like the defendants in Marchetti and Grosso.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice White announced the judgment of the Court and wrote an opinion joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart and Blackmun; Justices Harlan, Brennan, and Marshall concurred in the judgment in separate opinions, and Justices Douglas and Black dissented (7-2 on the judgment).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep401/usrep401667/usrep401667.pdf"},"1978/78-5384":{"facts":"In November 1976, 18-year-old David Sandstrom confessed to killing Annie Jessen. He was charged with deliberate homicide under a Montana statute, which required that he \"purposely or knowingly\" caused the death. At trial his lawyer conceded the killing but argued, relying on two court-appointed mental health experts, that because of a personality disorder aggravated by alcohol Sandstrom did not act purposely or knowingly and was guilty only of a lesser crime. Over defense objection, the judge instructed the jury that \"the law presumes that a person intends the ordinary consequences of his voluntary acts.\"","procedure":"The jury convicted Sandstrom of deliberate homicide and he was sentenced to 100 years. The Montana Supreme Court affirmed, accepting that a presumption may not shift the burden of proof but holding that the instruction placed on the defendant only a burden to produce some evidence, and so did not violate due process. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"In a case where intent is an element of the crime, does a jury instruction that \"the law presumes that a person intends the ordinary consequences of his voluntary acts\" violate the Fourteenth Amendment's requirement that the State prove every element of the offense beyond a reasonable doubt?","holding":"Yes. A reasonable juror could have read the instruction as either a conclusive presumption or one shifting the burden of persuasion on intent, and either reading would relieve the State of its burden under In re Winship, so the instruction is unconstitutional.","reasoning":"The Court held that the effect of a presumption depends on how a reasonable juror could have understood it, not on the state court's view of its legal import. The jury was told only that the law presumes intent, without being told it could infer intent or that the presumption could be rebutted, so it could readily have taken the instruction as mandatory. The Court rejected the State's characterizations of the instruction as a permissive inference or as a mere burden of production. A juror could have treated the presumption as conclusive, which would conflict with the presumption of innocence and invade the jury's factfinding role as in Morissette and United States Gypsum. Alternatively, the juror could have read it as shifting the burden of persuasion, which Mullaney v. Wilbur forbids; Montana's own evidence rules require a disputable presumption to be overcome by a preponderance of evidence. Because Winship holds that due process requires \"proof beyond a reasonable doubt of every fact necessary to constitute the crime,\" either reading was error. The State's argument that the jury might have convicted on knowledge alone failed because the verdict was general and the jury might have relied on the invalid instruction. The Court left harmless error to the Montana courts.","separate":"Justice Rehnquist, joined by Chief Justice Burger, concurred. He said he was reluctant for the Court to parse state jury instructions and noted that a permissive inference would raise no constitutional problem, but he deferred to the majority's view that the jury may have understood the instruction as mandatory, and that the difference between \"infer\" and \"presume\" may have mattered.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep442/usrep442510/usrep442510.pdf"},"1974/73-1977":{"facts":"The Wilderness Society, Environmental Defense Fund and Friends of the Earth sued the Secretary of the Interior in 1970 to stop permits for the trans-Alaska oil pipeline, arguing that the right-of-way widths violated the Mineral Leasing Act of 1920 and that the Secretary had not complied with the National Environmental Policy Act. Alyeska Pipeline Service Co., the consortium that would build the pipeline, later intervened. The Court of Appeals ruled for the groups on the Mineral Leasing Act ground alone, and Congress then amended that Act to authorize the permits, ending the merits dispute. The groups claimed 4,455 hours of attorneys' time.","procedure":"The District Court had dissolved the preliminary injunction and dismissed the complaint, but the en banc D.C. Circuit reversed on the Mineral Leasing Act ground. After Congress changed the law, the en banc Court of Appeals awarded respondents attorneys' fees against Alyeska, for half the full award, under the “private attorney general” theory, and remanded for the amount; it had rejected the bad-faith and common-benefit exceptions as inapplicable. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a federal court, in the exercise of its equitable powers and without statutory authorization, award attorneys' fees to a prevailing party that acted as a “private attorney general” vindicating important public policies?","holding":"No. Under the American Rule, the prevailing litigant ordinarily cannot recover attorneys' fees absent statute or enforceable contract, and the federal courts may not create a private-attorney-general exception; any such departure must come from Congress.","reasoning":"The Court traced the history of fee practice in federal courts: from early reliance on state practice, through Arcambel v. Wiseman, to the 1853 fee-bill act and its successors, now 28 U.S.C. sections 1920 and 1923, which limit the fees taxable as costs. The existing judicial exceptions (common fund or common benefit, willful disobedience of a court order, and bad faith) are recognized assertions of inherent equitable power, but none was relied on by the Court of Appeals. Congress has made specific fee-shifting provisions in many statutes, which differ in whether awards are mandatory or discretionary and who may recover, showing that the circumstances and range of discretion are matters for Congress. A judge-made rule would require courts to decide which statutes are important enough, and who may be awarded fees and against whom, without standards. It would also collide with 28 U.S.C. section 2412, which generally bars fee awards against the United States, so the rule would operate only against private parties. Congress's use of the private-attorney-general idea in particular statutes is not a grant of authority to the courts to adopt it generally. The Court said it would not “invade the legislature's province” by redistributing litigation costs as the Court of Appeals had.","separate":"Justice Brennan dissented in a short opinion, agreeing with Justice Marshall that federal equity courts have power to award fees on a private-attorney-general rationale and, for the reasons given by Judge Wright below, that this was a proper case. Justice Marshall dissented, arguing that the availability of fees rests on independent equitable power, not just statute, as shown by Sprague, Mills v. Electric Auto-Lite and Hall v. Cole, and that section 1923 is not a bar. He thought workable standards could be drawn from prior cases (an important right shared by the public, a plaintiff interest too small to justify counsel, and fees that would fall on a benefiting class), found them met here, and would have affirmed the half-fee award against Alyeska. Justices Douglas and Powell took no part.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas and Powell took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep421/usrep421240/usrep421240.pdf"},"1974/74-214":{"facts":"Appellee Salfi married wage earner Londo Salfi in May 1972; he had a heart attack less than a month later and died in November 1972, less than six months after the marriage. She applied for mother's insurance benefits for herself and child's insurance benefits for her daughter by a previous marriage. The Social Security Administration denied both applications, initially and on reconsideration, solely because the Social Security Act defines “widow” and “child” to exclude surviving wives and stepchildren whose relationship to the deceased wage earner lasted less than nine months before his death.","procedure":"The appellees filed a class action in federal district court, invoking 28 U.S.C. section 1331 and seeking declaratory and injunctive relief and retroactive benefits. A three-judge District Court held that the nine-month requirements were unconstitutional irrebuttable presumptions under Stanley v. Illinois, Vlandis v. Kline and Cleveland Board of Education v. LaFleur, certified a class, and enjoined the denial of benefits on that basis. The government appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Did the District Court have jurisdiction over the suit given 42 U.S.C. section 405(h), and, if so, are the Act's nine-month duration-of-relationship requirements for widows and stepchildren unconstitutional as conclusive presumptions?","holding":"Section 405(h) bars federal-question jurisdiction under section 1331, so jurisdiction existed only under section 405(g), and only over the named appellees, not unnamed class members who had not alleged any application or decision. On the merits, the nine-month requirements are constitutional because they are rationally related to the legitimate goal of preventing sham marriages entered to obtain benefits.","reasoning":"The third sentence of section 405(h) is sweeping and not a mere codification of exhaustion, and the claim, though raising a constitutional issue, also arises under the Act, which supplies standing and the substantive basis; Johnson v. Robison was distinguished because the statute there reached only decisions of the Administrator and would have left no judicial review. Section 405(g) supplied jurisdiction for the named appellees because the Secretary, by not challenging their allegations of exhaustion, in effect treated the reconsideration decision as final and a hearing would have been futile. On the merits, social welfare classifications need only be rationally based and free from invidious discrimination, and the Court said “a noncontractual claim to receive funds from the public treasury enjoys no constitutionally protected status.” Stanley, Vlandis and LaFleur were distinguished because the Act sets an objective eligibility criterion that claimants can try to meet rather than speaking of good faith and then excluding evidence of it. Congress had long been concerned about marriages entered to obtain benefits, and could rationally adopt an imprecise prophylactic rule, like a life insurance suicide clause, given the many claimants and the difficulty of reliably determining intent or knowledge of terminal illness through individual hearings.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, agreeing with Justice Brennan that jurisdiction was clear and the Court's discussion unwarranted; on the merits he objected to legislatively created presumptions as invading the right to jury trial, and would have remanded to let Mrs. Salfi show her marriage was not a sham. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented. He argued that the jurisdictional question had not been briefed, that section 405(h) only codifies exhaustion and does not reach a claim arising under the Constitution or a suit for declaratory and injunctive relief (relying on Johnson v. Robison), that the Secretary had not actually waived exhaustion, and that on the merits the conclusive presumption was irrational and contrary to Vlandis, Murry and Jimenez, so that individualized proof should have been allowed.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Blackmun and Powell.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep422/usrep422749/usrep422749.pdf"},"1970/5586":{"facts":"Bell, a clergyman who traveled by car to serve three rural Georgia communities, was involved in a 1968 accident when a five-year-old girl rode her bicycle into the side of his car. Her parents reported injuries and claimed $5,000 in damages. Under Georgia's Motor Vehicle Safety Responsibility Act, the Director of Public Safety told Bell that, since he was uninsured, he must post $5,000 in security or have his license and registration suspended. At the administrative hearing the Director refused to consider Bell's evidence that he was not at fault, because the only matters the Department could consider were involvement in the accident, compliance with the Act, and statutory exceptions.","procedure":"Bell appealed de novo to the Superior Court, which heard his evidence, found him free from fault, and ordered that his license not be suspended pending a damages suit. The Georgia Court of Appeals reversed, holding fault and innocence completely irrelevant under the Act and rejecting his due process claim; the Georgia Supreme Court denied review and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process Clause allow Georgia to suspend an uninsured motorist's driver's license and registration after an accident, without a prior hearing on whether he was at fault or liable?","holding":"No. Because the licenses are important interests that cannot be taken without procedural due process, and because the Georgia scheme makes liability a significant factor, the State must, before suspension, provide a forum to determine whether there is a reasonable possibility of a judgment being rendered against the licensee as a result of the accident.","reasoning":"Once issued, a driver's license may become essential to a livelihood, so its suspension is state action adjudicating important interests, and the same constraints apply whether the entitlement is called a right or a privilege, as in Goldberg v. Kelly and Sniadach. The required inquiry need not be a full adjudication of liability, which can occur only in litigation between the parties, but only a determination of a reasonable possibility of a judgment in the amounts claimed. The State's interest in security for claimants does not justify denying the hearing where no judgment is reasonably possible, and added expense does not outweigh due process. Georgia's argument that fault is irrelevant failed because, looking to substance, the Act lifts or avoids suspension on a release from liability or an adjudication of nonliability, so it is not a no-fault scheme. A hearing that excludes an element essential to the decision is not meaningful, and except in emergencies the hearing must come before the termination takes effect. Georgia may choose among several ways to comply.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger and Justices Black and Blackmun concurred in the result; the opinion states no separate reasons.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Douglas, Harlan, Stewart, White and Marshall; Chief Justice Burger and Justices Black and Blackmun concurred in the result.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep402/usrep402535/usrep402535.pdf"},"1972/72-634":{"facts":"The National Association of Letter Carriers, six individual federal employees, and local Democratic and Republican committees challenged section 9(a) of the Hatch Act, now 5 U.S.C. section 7324(a)(2), which bars federal employees from taking “an active part in political management or in political campaigns,” defined as the acts prohibited for competitive-service employees by Civil Service Commission determinations before July 19, 1940. The plaintiffs said they wanted to run for local offices, serve as convention delegates and party officers, campaign for candidates, work at polls, and write political letters, and that the Commission was enforcing or threatening to enforce the ban. One plaintiff had resigned from federal employment to run for state office and then faced delayed reemployment.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court for the District of Columbia held the provision unconstitutional on its face as vague and overbroad, reading United Public Workers v. Mitchell as leaving the definition open, and enjoined enforcement. The government appealed directly, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on the single question of facial invalidity.","question":"Is the Hatch Act's prohibition on federal employees taking an active part in political management or political campaigns unconstitutional on its face for vagueness or overbreadth under the First Amendment and Fifth Amendment?","holding":"No. Congress may constitutionally bar federal employees from plainly identifiable partisan political management and campaign activity, as Mitchell held, and the provision, read with the Civil Service Commission's regulations, is neither unconstitutionally vague nor substantially overbroad.","reasoning":"The Court reaffirmed Mitchell and described a century of history, from the Pendleton Act and Civil Service Rule I to the Hatch Act, in which the country judged that federal service should rest on merit rather than political service and that employee influence on elections should be limited. Applying the Pickering balance between employee speech and the government's interest as employer, the Court found the interests substantial: impartial execution of the laws, avoiding the appearance of political justice, preventing a government work force from being built into a political machine, and protecting employees from pressure. The restrictions are not aimed at any party or viewpoint. On vagueness, Congress's 1940 substitute for section 15 meant that the statute incorporates the Commission's pre-1940 adjudications as summarized in Form 1236 and refined in the 1970 regulations, and the Court said “our task is not to destroy the Act if we can, but to construe it.” The regulations list the prohibited acts in terms an ordinary person can understand, and the Commission offers advisory opinions. Any overbreadth in the restrictions on endorsements and speeches at party gatherings is not substantial and would not invalidate the whole statute.","separate":"Justice Douglas, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented. He argued that the Act, which incorporates thousands of Commission rulings, lacks precision and chills First Amendment speech, petition and assembly by employees on their own time; that Mitchell's rational-basis approach is outdated in light of Pickering, Wood v. Georgia and Perry v. Sindermann; that the Commission's open-ended list of prohibited acts gives it unbridled discretion (illustrated by a ruling against a worker who placed a newspaper ad for his father's candidacy); and that the provision should be struck down so Congress could start anew.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas, Brennan and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413548/usrep413548.pdf"},"1969/301":{"facts":"Bruce Church, Inc. grew high-quality cantaloupes on about 6,400 leased acres at Parker, Arizona, in which it had invested over $3,000,000. Because it had no packing sheds there, and the perishable melons had to be packed immediately, it trucked them in bulk to its packing facilities at Blythe, California, 31 miles away, where they were packed and shipped in containers bearing the California packer's name. In 1968 Arizona's fruit and vegetable supervisor, enforcing the state Fruit and Vegetable Standardization Act, ordered that the cantaloupes not be shipped out of state unless packed in approved containers, which could be done only in packing sheds. The parties stipulated that compliance would require building packing facilities near Parker at a cost of about $200,000, and the company faced loss of a $700,000 crop.","procedure":"The company sued in federal court to enjoin the order. A three-judge District Court granted temporary relief and then a permanent injunction, holding that the order was an unlawful burden on interstate commerce. The state official appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does an Arizona order that requires cantaloupes grown in the State to be packed in Arizona before interstate shipment, in order to identify them as Arizona produce, unconstitutionally burden interstate commerce?","holding":"Yes. The order burdens interstate commerce, and Arizona's minimal interest in having the company's high-quality cantaloupes identified as Arizona-grown cannot justify forcing the company to build and operate an unneeded $200,000 packing plant in the State.","reasoning":"The order does burden interstate commerce, because the melons were headed to an ascertainable place in California and the order would require an operation done out of state to be done in state; Federal Compress and Chassaniol, involving taxes on goods at rest in a State, were distinguished. The Court stated the general rule that where a statute regulates evenhandedly to effectuate a legitimate local interest and has only incidental effects on interstate commerce, it will be upheld unless the burden is clearly excessive in relation to the local benefits, with the extent of tolerable burden depending on the nature of the local interest and on whether it could be promoted with less impact. The Act's purpose, protecting the reputation of Arizona growers against deceptive packaging, is legitimate, but applied here it served only to enhance Arizona's reputation through the company's superior produce, and the company was not misusing the State's name. The Court said “The nature of that burden is, constitutionally, more significant than its extent,” since requirements that business operations be performed in the home State have been viewed with particular suspicion (Foster-Fountain Packing, Toomer v. Witsell). The State's interest, assumed legitimate, was minimal and less than the interest in local employment, which the Commerce Clause also does not permit to justify such a requirement.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court; the opinion text does not state a vote count and notes no separate opinions.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep397/usrep397137/usrep397137.pdf"},"1976/75-1721":{"facts":"In May 1973 railroad officials in San Diego noticed that a heavy footlocker loaded by Machado and Leary was leaking talcum powder, and federal agents were alerted. When the train reached Boston, agents with a trained dog saw the dog signal the presence of a controlled substance in the footlocker. After Chadwick joined the others and the footlocker was placed in the trunk of his car, agents arrested all three. They took the footlocker to the Federal Building, where it stayed under their exclusive control, and opened it about an hour and a half after the arrests without consent or a warrant, finding large amounts of marihuana.","procedure":"Respondents were indicted for possession of marihuana with intent to distribute and conspiracy, and moved to suppress the marihuana. The District Court granted the motion, rejecting both the automobile exception and search incident to arrest, and a divided Court of Appeals for the First Circuit affirmed. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Is a search warrant required before federal agents may open a locked footlocker that they have lawfully seized at the time of its owners' arrest, when they have probable cause to believe it contains contraband?","holding":"Yes. Once the agents had the footlocker under their exclusive control and no exigency existed, the Warrant Clause required a warrant before the search, and the search was justified neither by the automobile exception nor as incident to arrest.","reasoning":"The Court rejected the Government's argument that the Warrant Clause protects only homes and similar places, since the Fourth Amendment “protects people, not places” and a warrant's neutral scrutiny is as valuable outside the home. By locking personal effects in a double-locked footlocker, respondents manifested an expectation of privacy that is due the Warrant Clause's protection. The automobile exception rests on the diminished privacy expectation in vehicles, which does not apply to luggage that serves as a repository of personal effects, and the footlocker's mobility did not justify dispensing with a warrant once it was immobilized under police control. A search incident to arrest is justified by the danger that the arrestee will use a weapon or destroy evidence, so it cannot cover luggage that is remote in time or place from the arrest or already in exclusive police control. The Court drew the line where the property comes under the exclusive dominion of police authority, even though a warrant would likely have been issued.","separate":"Justice Brennan concurred, joining the opinion in full, to criticize the Government's “extreme view” of the Fourth Amendment and to note that the dissent's alternative courses of action were supported only by court of appeals decisions and are not clearly lawful. Justice Blackmun, joined by Justice Rehnquist, dissented, agreeing the Warrant Clause is not limited to the home but arguing that police should be allowed to search without a warrant any movable property seized with a person properly arrested in a public place, that the majority's rule makes outcomes turn on fortuitous circumstances, and that other doctrines would often allow such searches anyway.","disposition":"Affirmed. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart, White, Marshall, Powell and Stevens (7-2).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep433/usrep433001/usrep433001.pdf"},"1973/72-953":{"facts":"Nineteen residents of Cairo, Illinois (17 black, two white) sued as a class, alleging that officials in Alexander County engaged in discriminatory criminal-justice practices to deter their civil-rights protests and boycott of merchants. As to the petitioners, a magistrate and a circuit judge, they alleged an unofficial bond schedule applied without regard to individual circumstances, harsher sentences and conditions for black residents, and charging for jury trials in city-ordinance cases. They sought only an injunction against these practices, not damages. The complaint described these practices in general terms and did not identify any named plaintiff as having suffered them.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed for want of jurisdiction to grant the injunction and on judicial immunity grounds. The Seventh Circuit reversed, holding that injunctions against judicial officers are not barred if they knowingly engage in intentional racial discrimination, and that the District Court should fashion appropriate relief if the allegations were proved. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does a complaint seeking to enjoin a magistrate's and judge's alleged discriminatory bond, sentencing, and jury-fee practices present an actual case or controversy under Article III, and does it state a basis for federal equitable relief against state criminal proceedings?","holding":"The complaint failed to allege an actual case or controversy, because none of the named plaintiffs was shown to have suffered or to face a real and immediate threat of injury from the challenged practices. Even if there were a controversy, the injunction sought would intrude on state criminal proceedings contrary to principles of equity, comity, and federalism, and the plaintiffs showed no irreparable injury or inadequate legal remedy.","reasoning":"Plaintiffs must allege a \"personal stake in the outcome,\" and a real and immediate injury rather than a conjectural one; if no named class representative meets that requirement, none may seek relief for the class. The complaint alleged injury only in general terms, and past exposure to illegal conduct does not show a present controversy for injunctive relief absent continuing effects. The prospect of future injury depended on respondents again being arrested, charged, and brought before petitioners, which would require speculation, especially since no unconstitutional statute was challenged and the Court assumed respondents would stay within the law. On the alternative ground, the requested injunction would amount to \"an ongoing federal audit of state criminal proceedings,\" requiring continuing federal supervision and contempt proceedings, in conflict with Younger v. Harris. The Court also found irreparable injury and inadequacy of legal remedies unshown, pointing to state remedies (change of judge, appeal, collateral review, judicial discipline), federal habeas, and criminal liability under 18 U.S.C. section 242.","separate":"Justice Blackmun concurred in the judgment and in Part I, but argued that once the Court found no case or controversy, its discussion of equitable relief in Part II was an advisory opinion it could not properly give. Justice Douglas, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented, arguing that the complaint's allegations of a continuing pattern of discriminatory bail and sentencing were specific enough to state a case or controversy and warranted a trial, that remedies should be decided after trial, and that Part II was advisory if there was no controversy.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Powell, and Rehnquist; Justice Blackmun concurred in the judgment and in Part I; Justices Douglas, Brennan, and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep414/usrep414488/usrep414488.pdf"},"1975/75-252":{"facts":"After nine serious fires over about two and a half months in 1974 at the medium-security Massachusetts Correctional Institution at Norfolk, six inmates were removed from the general population based mainly on informant reports. The prison's Classification Board held hearings, with counsel present, on whether to transfer them to other institutions, possibly maximum-security ones where conditions were substantially less favorable. The Board heard the superintendent's account of informant information outside the inmates' presence, and the inmates never received transcripts or summaries of it. The inmates could present their own evidence and denied involvement. The Board's recommendations, as reviewed and modified by the Commissioner of Corrections, resulted in transfers to Walpole and Bridgewater, none involving loss of good time or disciplinary confinement.","procedure":"The inmates sued under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 for an injunction, declaratory relief, and damages. The District Court, reading Wolff v. McDonnell, held the notice and hearing inadequate and ordered the inmates returned to the general population until transferred after proper procedures. A divided First Circuit affirmed, holding the transfers significantly changed confinement conditions and triggered due process protections. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment entitle a state prisoner to a factfinding hearing before transfer to a prison with substantially less favorable conditions, where state law does not condition transfers on specific misconduct or other events?","holding":"No. A transfer to a less favorable prison does not infringe or implicate a liberty interest within the Due Process Clause, either by its own force or through state law, where state officials have discretion to transfer prisoners for any reason or no reason, so no hearing is constitutionally required.","reasoning":"The Court said that not every grievous loss triggers due process; the nature of the interest, not its weight, controls. Given a valid conviction, the State may confine the prisoner in any of its institutions and subject him to its prison rules, so confinement in any state prison is within the range of custody the conviction authorized, even if one is far more disagreeable. Treating any substantial deprivation as requiring a hearing would subject a wide range of discretionary prison administration to federal judicial review. Wolff v. McDonnell was distinguished because the liberty interest there came from state law, which made good-time credits forfeitable only for serious misbehavior. Massachusetts law gave no right to remain in a particular prison and vested transfer authority in officials in many circumstances. That misconduct often prompts a transfer does not change this, and any expectation of staying put is \"too ephemeral and insubstantial to trigger procedural due process protections.\" States remain free to require hearings by statute or rule.","separate":"Justice Stevens, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented. He objected most to the Court's view that liberty protected by the Due Process Clause comes only from the Constitution or state law, arguing instead that liberty is a basic freedom that persists in some measure during lawful custody. He would hold that a sufficiently grievous change in a prisoner's status may not be imposed arbitrarily, and agreed with the Court of Appeals that these transfers were serious enough to require due process.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, Powell, and Rehnquist; Justices Stevens, Brennan, and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep427/usrep427215/usrep427215.pdf"},"1969/153":{"facts":"Respondents Dash, Richardson and Williams were each convicted in a New York state court of a felony (robbery, murder, robbery) on a plea of guilty entered on the advice of counsel. Each later claimed in a habeas corpus petition that the plea was the product of a coerced confession. Dash also alleged that the trial judge threatened a 60-year sentence if he went to trial. Richardson alleged his assigned lawyer advised him to plead and raise the confession later by habeas corpus. Williams alleged his lawyer ignored his alibi and misdescribed the plea as to a misdemeanor.","procedure":"State courts denied collateral relief without hearings, and the federal District Courts denied the habeas petitions without hearings. The Second Circuit reversed in each case and ordered hearings, holding that a plea is not voluntary if it results from an involuntary confession, at least where entered in New York before Jackson v. Denno. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a counseled, otherwise valid guilty plea be attacked in collateral proceedings, and a hearing obtained, merely by alleging that the plea was motivated by a prior coerced confession, including where the plea was entered before Jackson v. Denno?","holding":"A defendant who alleges that he pleaded guilty because of a prior coerced confession is not, without more, entitled to a hearing on a habeas corpus petition. A plea based on reasonably competent advice is an intelligent plea, and the pre-Jackson timing of the plea does not change that result.","reasoning":"The Court reasoned that a defendant who deems his confession inadmissible and would otherwise go to trial is, by pleading guilty, bypassing his chance to raise the confession claim in state court, and the more plausible explanation is that he predicted the confession would be admitted. Nothing in that sequence makes the plea itself involuntary; at most it shows a misjudgment of admissibility. Plea advice necessarily involves uncertain predictions, and the question is whether counsel's advice was within the range of competence demanded of attorneys in criminal cases, not whether it proved correct. As to Jackson v. Denno, a plea is judged by whether it was voluntary and intelligent under the law then existing, and counsel could not be faulted for relying on Stein v. New York or for not anticipating Jackson. A guilty plea rests on the defendant's own admission in open court, not on the confession, so Jackson's retroactivity for trial convictions does not extend to pleas. The Court expressed no disagreement with the Court of Appeals on the other claims (the judge's threat, inadequacy of counsel).","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the opinion and judgment while adhering to his separate opinion in Jackson v. Denno. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall, dissented. He argued that a plea induced by a coerced confession is invalid under Pennsylvania ex rel. Herman v. Claudy and Chambers v. Florida, that the presence of counsel should not be given talismanic effect, and that the plea should be treated as the fruit of the State's illegal conduct as in Harrison v. United States. Because New York's pre-Jackson procedure was constitutionally defective, counsel's advice could not cure it. He criticized the Court for making Jackson only partially retroactive and would have affirmed the order for hearings on the coerced-confession claims.","disposition":"The judgments of the Court of Appeals were vacated and the case remanded for further proceedings consistent with the opinion. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court. Justice Black concurred; Justices Brennan, Douglas and Marshall dissented (the text does not state a vote count).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep397/usrep397759/usrep397759.pdf"},"1970/95":{"facts":"A Wisconsin statute let designated persons, including a wife, mayor, police chief, sheriff or district attorney, forbid in writing all sales or gifts of liquor to a person who by excessive drinking exposed himself or family to want or became dangerous to community peace. Selling or giving liquor to such a person was a misdemeanor. The Hartford police chief, without notice or hearing, had notices posted in all retail liquor outlets in Hartford forbidding sales or gifts of liquor to appellee, a Hartford resident, for one year.","procedure":"Appellee sued the chief for damages and injunctive relief in federal district court. Wisconsin intervened on the injunction issue, and a three-judge court, by a divided vote, held the Act unconstitutional on its face and enjoined its enforcement. The State appealed and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Does a state statute that permits an official to post a person as forbidden to buy liquor, without notice or a hearing, violate procedural due process? And should the federal court have abstained to let state courts construe the statute first?","holding":"Where a statute attaches a stigma to a person by official posting, procedural due process requires notice and an opportunity to be heard, and the Wisconsin Act, which provides none, is unconstitutional. Abstention was not required because the statute is unambiguous and presents no uncertain question of state law.","reasoning":"The Court acknowledged the State's broad police power over intoxicating liquors, so the only issue was whether the label imposed by posting is a stigma requiring procedural protection. Drawing on earlier decisions, it stated that where the State attaches a \"badge of infamy\" to a citizen, due process comes into play, and that where a person's good name or reputation is at stake because of government action, \"notice and an opportunity to be heard are essential.\" Posting may be a mark of illness to some but is an official branding to others, and the appellee had no chance to defend herself. On abstention, the doctrine applies only where the issue of state law is uncertain. Here the Act contains no provision for notice and hearing and is unambiguous, so the naked question was its facial constitutionality, and under Zwickler v. Koota the federal court should decide it rather than await state court proceedings.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger, joined by Justice Blackmun, dissented. He thought the result probably correct on the merits, but would have required the parties to resort to the state courts first, citing Reetz v. Bozanich and the abstention policy even for a plain statute, and noted that counsel had deliberately avoided state court to obtain a three-judge court and direct appeal. Justice Black, joined by Justice Blackmun, also dissented. He would have vacated and remanded so state courts could construe the Act, suggested that other state law might supply notice and hearing, and compared the Act's unbounded ex parte power to a bill of attainder.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Douglas delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Harlan, Brennan, Stewart, White and Marshall. Chief Justice Burger and Justice Blackmun dissented, as did Justice Black (joined by Justice Blackmun).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep400/usrep400433/usrep400433.pdf"},"1973/72-6476":{"facts":"Petitioners were recipients of Aid to Families with Dependent Children (AFDC), a cooperative federal-state program. Each had been unable to pay rent and, facing eviction, received emergency rent payments from the county social services department. New York characterized these payments as advances and, under a state regulation, deducted them from later monthly grants. Petitioners sued under 42 U.S.C. section 1983, alleging that the recoupment regulation violated the Equal Protection Clause and conflicted with the Social Security Act and HEW regulations.","procedure":"The District Court found the equal protection claim substantial, took pendent jurisdiction of the statutory claim, and declared the regulation invalid under the Social Security Act and HEW regulations and enjoined it. The Court of Appeals reversed, holding that because no substantial constitutional claim was presented, the District Court lacked jurisdiction over either claim. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on the jurisdictional question.","question":"Did petitioners' equal protection claim satisfy the substantiality requirement for jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. section 1343(3), and if so, could the District Court decide the pendent statutory claim of conflict with federal law before the constitutional claim?","holding":"The equal protection claim was not so insubstantial as to defeat jurisdiction, so the District Court had jurisdiction under section 1343(3). With that jurisdiction, it could also decide the pendent statutory claim first, without convening a three-judge court, and should not have dismissed it.","reasoning":"The Court accepted the rule that a federal claim must be substantial to support jurisdiction, but said it is insubstantial only if obviously frivolous or if prior decisions clearly foreclose it. Dandridge v. Williams applied rational-basis review to welfare regulations but did not suspend the Equal Protection Clause, and no decision of the Court addressed a regulation like this one, whose rationality was not immediately obvious. Under Bell v. Hood, a claim that may fail on the merits is not for that reason beyond jurisdiction. The statutory claim was properly decided first, because a nonconstitutional ground that may dispose of the case is preferred, and a single judge may decide it; requiring a three-judge court only to send it back would be inefficient. Mine Workers v. Gibbs concerns discretion over pendent state claims and did not displace this practice, and there is special reason to hear a claim that is in reality a Supremacy Clause claim, since \"federal courts are particularly appropriate bodies for the application of pre-emption principles.\" The Court left open whether other grounds also supported jurisdiction.","separate":"Justice Powell, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist, dissented, joining Justice Rehnquist's dissent and adding that the majority misread Gibbs and Siler, and that the federal claim must have more than a glimmer of merit before a district court decides a pendent claim. Justice Rehnquist, joined by the Chief Justice and Justice Powell, dissented. He argued that the equal protection claim was insubstantial under Dandridge and was pleaded mainly to obtain jurisdiction over the stronger Supremacy Clause claim, which Congress had left to state courts through the amount-in-controversy requirement of section 1331. He added that, even if jurisdiction existed, the District Court abused its discretion under Gibbs by exercising pendent jurisdiction, and should have referred the constitutional claim to a three-judge court.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was reversed and the case remanded for further proceedings consistent with the opinion. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Douglas, Brennan, Stewart, Marshall and Blackmun. Justices Powell, Rehnquist and Chief Justice Burger dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep415/usrep415528/usrep415528.pdf"},"1970/203":{"facts":"McGautha was convicted in California of first-degree murder (and two armed robberies) after he and a codefendant held up two stores on the same day and a store owner was fatally shot; the evidence was inconclusive on who fired the shot. Crampton was convicted in Ohio of first-degree murder of his wife, whom he had threatened, after stealing guns and returning to her home; she was found shot in the face. In both states the jury had absolute discretion to choose death or life imprisonment, and the jurors received no standards for the choice. California tried guilt and punishment in two stages before the same jury, and McGautha and his codefendant testified at the penalty stage; the jury sentenced McGautha to death and his codefendant to life. Ohio tried guilt and punishment together in one proceeding and one verdict; Crampton did not testify, and the jury returned a guilty verdict with no recommendation of mercy, which carried a death sentence.","procedure":"The California Supreme Court unanimously affirmed McGautha's conviction and rejected his challenge to standardless jury sentencing, relying on an earlier narrowly divided decision. The Ohio Supreme Court affirmed Crampton's conviction and sentence. The Supreme Court granted certiorari in McGautha limited to the standards question, and in Crampton on that question plus the constitutionality of a single guilt-and-punishment proceeding.","question":"Does the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment forbid a State from leaving the choice between death and life imprisonment to the jury's absolute discretion, without governing standards? And is a single trial and verdict on both guilt and punishment in a capital case unconstitutional because it forces a defendant to choose between his privilege against self-incrimination on guilt and his opportunity to be heard on punishment?","holding":"No to both. Giving a capital jury \"untrammeled discretion of the jury\" over life or death does not violate the Constitution, and the Constitution does not require bifurcated capital trials. A State may decide that issues of guilt and punishment are better resolved in a single proceeding.","reasoning":"Justice Harlan began by stressing that the Court's role was to ask what the Constitution prohibits, not to impose a preferred system on the States. Tracing the history of capital punishment, he found a long record of failed attempts to define in advance which homicides deserve death, which led legislatures to hand the decision to juries. Every American jurisdiction that has used jury sentencing has done so without standards, and no court had held that unconstitutional. The Court reasoned that the proposed Model Penal Code criteria give only minimal control and show how intractable it is to write standards, so it found it impossible to say that standardless discretion offends the Constitution. On bifurcation, the Court distinguished Simmons v. United States and said the Constitution does not always forbid making a defendant choose, citing \"the making of difficult judgments\". It reasoned that the self-incrimination privilege is not offended when a defendant testifies on punishment at the risk of hurting his case on guilt, comparing cross-examination, impeachment, and other routine choices. As for the right to be heard on sentence, Ohio allowed counsel to argue for mercy, admitted broad background evidence, and kept allocution, so Crampton was not denied any constitutionally required opportunity.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the judgments and substantially all of the opinion, but said the Court's task is not to decide whether trials were fairly conducted; he found no due process violation and no Eighth Amendment bar to capital punishment, since it was common when the Amendment was adopted. Justice Douglas, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented in No. 204: Ohio's unitary trial violates procedural due process because a defendant can be heard on insanity or mercy only by giving up his privilege against self-incrimination, and Ohio bars evidence directed solely at mercy. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall, dissented in both cases: due process requires States to make explicit their basic policy choices and to use procedures that control arbitrary action and permit meaningful review, and he called the California and Ohio schemes \"stark legislative abdication\" that nothing in the nature of capital sentencing makes impossible to cure.","disposition":"Affirmed in both No. 203 (McGautha v. California) and No. 204 (Crampton v. Ohio). Justice Harlan delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, and Blackmun; Justice Black filed a separate opinion concurring; Justices Douglas, Brennan, and Marshall dissented (Douglas only in No. 204).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep402/usrep402183/usrep402183.pdf"},"1972/71-6278":{"facts":"Almeida-Sanchez, a Mexican citizen with a valid U.S. work permit, was stopped by a Border Patrol roving patrol on a California state highway about 25 air miles north of the Mexican border, and his car was thoroughly searched. The agents had no warrant, no probable cause, no consent, and not even reasonable suspicion. The search turned up a large quantity of marihuana, and he was convicted of receiving, concealing, and facilitating the transportation of illegally imported marihuana. The Government relied on section 287(a)(3) of the Immigration and Nationality Act, which allows warrantless searches of vehicles within a reasonable distance of the border, and a regulation defining that as 100 air miles.","procedure":"The Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit recognized the search was not a border search but upheld it on the basis of the statute and regulation. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to consider the search's constitutionality.","question":"Does the Fourth Amendment permit the Border Patrol to search a vehicle, without a warrant, probable cause, or consent, on a road well inside the country some distance from the border, under a statute authorizing warrantless searches within a reasonable distance of the border?","holding":"No. A warrantless vehicle search by a roving patrol, made without probable cause or consent at a place that is neither the border nor its functional equivalent, violates the Fourth Amendment, and the statute cannot authorize it.","reasoning":"The Court first found no support in the automobile search cases, which require probable cause even without a warrant. It then rejected the analogy to administrative inspections: Camara required a warrant or consent to limit the discretion of the official in the field, and Colonnade and Biswell involved businesses in closely regulated, licensed industries whose owners accept inspection as a condition of the trade. The petitioner was not in any such business, and there was no reason to think he or his car had crossed the border. That left only the statute, but the Court stated that \"no Act of Congress can authorize a violation of the Constitution.\" Border searches may occur at the border or its functional equivalents, such as an established station where roads from the border meet or an arriving nonstop international flight, but this roving patrol search was of a wholly different sort. The seriousness of illegal immigration did not justify dispensing with constitutional safeguards, and the Court noted Carroll's statement that people lawfully within the country have a right to free passage absent probable cause.","separate":"Justice Powell concurred, joining the Court's opinion but adding that he would accept a functional equivalent of probable cause for roving border-area searches based on area-wide factors such as the frequency of alien transport, proximity to the border, and the road's character, while still requiring an area warrant from a judicial officer; since none was obtained here, he agreed the judgment must be reversed. Justice White, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Blackmun and Rehnquist, dissented. He would have held that the Fourth Amendment's standard is reasonableness, that Congress's judgment in the statute and the consistent views of the courts of appeals deserve weight, that the border is better treated as a zone than a line, and that the stop and limited search for aliens here was reasonable, making the discovery of marihuana lawful.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Douglas, Brennan, Marshall, and Powell (5-4), with Justice Powell also concurring.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413266/usrep413266.pdf"},"1976/75-1861":{"facts":"Gordon Patterson, estranged from his wife Roberta, borrowed a rifle, went to his father-in-law's home, and saw his wife in a state of semiundress with John Northrup, a neighbor to whom she had once been engaged. He entered and shot Northrup twice in the head, killing him. Patterson confessed before trial. He was charged with second-degree murder, which in New York requires intent to cause death and causing death, and at trial he raised the affirmative defense of extreme emotional disturbance, which reduces murder to manslaughter.","procedure":"The jury was instructed that the prosecution must prove each element beyond a reasonable doubt and that Patterson had to prove extreme emotional disturbance by a preponderance of the evidence. It convicted him of murder, and the Appellate Division affirmed. The New York Court of Appeals rejected his argument, based on Mullaney v. Wilbur, that the statute unconstitutionally shifted the burden of proof, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on appeal.","question":"Does the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment bar New York from requiring a murder defendant to prove by a preponderance of the evidence the affirmative defense of extreme emotional disturbance that reduces the crime to manslaughter?","holding":"No. Due process requires the prosecution to prove beyond a reasonable doubt every element of the offense as the State defines it, but a State need not disprove an affirmative defense that does not negate any element of the crime, and it may place the burden of proving such a defense on the defendant.","reasoning":"The Court noted that the State proved beyond a reasonable doubt each fact it made necessary for murder: the death, the intent to kill, and causation. Nothing further was presumed against Patterson, and extreme emotional disturbance did not negate any of those facts but was a separate issue on which the defendant carries the burden. That follows Leland v. Oregon and Rivera v. Delaware, which allowed placing the burden of proving insanity on the defendant, and common law placed the burden of proving heat of passion and similar defenses on the defendant. The Court said that \"Proof of the nonexistence of all affirmative defenses has never been constitutionally required\" and that a State that chooses to recognize a mitigating factor may assure itself the fact is established with reasonable certainty. It acknowledged that legislatures cannot simply relabel elements as affirmative defenses to evade Winship, and that there are constitutional limits. It distinguished Mullaney, where Maine's law presumed malice, an ingredient of murder as defined there, and required the defendant to rebut it; Mullaney forbids shifting the burden on an ingredient of the offense by presuming it, but did not reach this situation.","separate":"Justice Powell, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented. He argued that the decision drains Winship and Mullaney of vitality by drawing a formalistic line based on how a statute is worded, since extreme emotional disturbance is the modern equivalent of the heat of passion at issue in Mullaney and the effect on the defendant is the same. He would hold that the prosecution must bear the burden of persuasion on a factor that makes a substantial difference in punishment and stigma and has historically held that importance, and he noted the State may still shift the burden of production.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, and Stevens; Justices Powell, Brennan, and Marshall dissented. Justice Rehnquist took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep432/usrep432197/usrep432197.pdf"},"1969/830":{"facts":"Shortly after a Gulf service station in North Braddock, Pennsylvania was robbed by two armed men, police stopped a light blue station wagon matching the description given by teenage witnesses and the attendant. It carried four men, one in a green sweater and with a trench coat in the car. Petitioner was one of them. The occupants were arrested and the car was driven to the police station, where a thorough warrantless search found two revolvers, a glove of coins, and cards bearing the name of an attendant at another station robbed a week earlier. The next day a warrant-authorized search of petitioner's home turned up ammunition. Both attendants identified petitioner, and he was convicted of both robberies at a second trial; a Legal Aid attorney who first met him just before that trial represented him.","procedure":"Petitioner did not take a direct appeal. He sought habeas corpus in the Pennsylvania courts, which denied relief, and then in federal district court, which denied the petition without a hearing; the Third Circuit affirmed, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the warrantless search of the automobile at the police station, after the occupants' arrest, violate the Fourth Amendment so that the items found had to be excluded? Also, were the admission of the ammunition seized from petitioner's home and the late substitution of counsel grounds for reversal?","holding":"The warrantless station-house search of the car was valid. Where police have probable cause to search an automobile, they may either search it immediately without a warrant or seize and hold it until a warrant is obtained; the Fourth Amendment treats the two courses alike because of the difference between cars and houses. Any error in admitting the home-seized ammunition was harmless, and the Court would not require a hearing or reversal for tardy appointment of counsel on this record.","reasoning":"The Court agreed the police had probable cause to stop the car and arrest its occupants, but held the later station-house search could not be justified as incident to arrest, because a search at another place after the accused is in custody is not incident to the arrest. It found another ground: the police had probable cause to believe the robbers, with guns and the fruits of the crime, were in this very station wagon, and under Carroll v. United States and later cases an automobile may be searched without a warrant on probable cause in circumstances that would not justify a warrantless search of a house or office, because the car is mobile and the chance to search is fleeting. Because the circumstances giving probable cause are usually unforeseeable, either the car must be searched at once or seized and held until a warrant issues, and the Court saw no constitutional difference between the two, since probable cause and mobility continued at the station house. The Court noted that \"there is a constitutional difference between houses and cars.\" On the ammunition, it accepted the lower courts' finding of harmless error. On counsel, it declined to adopt a per se rule against tardy appointment, noting that the Court of Appeals found no prejudice because the car search was valid and the bullet error harmless.","separate":"Justice Stewart concurred, adhering to his view that admitting evidence obtained in violation of the Fourth Amendment is not by itself a ground for collateral attack on a final conviction, but joining the Court because the Court had not adopted that view. Justice Harlan concurred in part and dissented in part. He would have required an evidentiary hearing on the ineffective-assistance claim, given counsel's last-minute entry and his handling of the suppression issue and cross-examination. He also dissented on the car search, arguing that the exception to the warrant requirement should be no broader than the exigency, so that police should only immobilize the car while seeking a warrant, which he thought the lesser intrusion; he agreed that admitting the bullets was harmless.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Blackmun took no part. The text does not state the vote count; Justice Stewart concurred and Justice Harlan concurred in part and dissented in part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep399/usrep399042/usrep399042.pdf"},"1975/74-728":{"facts":"Franks brought a class action under Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 against Bowman Transportation Co. and its unions, alleging racially discriminatory employment practices. Lee intervened for himself and others, alleging discriminatory hiring and discharge policies limited to over-the-road (OTR) truck drivers. The District Court found a pattern of racial discrimination in hiring, transfer and discharge. It defined a class of black nonemployee applicants for OTR jobs before January 1, 1972 (class 3), whose applications were in evidence, and Lee had been hired later and then properly discharged for cause.","procedure":"The District Court enjoined the discriminatory practices and ordered Bowman to notify class members of their right to priority consideration, but denied the unnamed members backpay and retroactive seniority. The Court of Appeals vacated as to backpay but affirmed denial of any seniority relief to class 3, holding it barred as a matter of law by § 703(h) of Title VII. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on the seniority issue only.","question":"Is the case moot because the named class representative no longer has a personal stake? Does § 703(h) bar retroactive seniority relief to identifiable applicants refused employment because of race after Title VII took effect, and is such relief otherwise appropriate under § 706(g)?","holding":"The case is not moot, § 703(h) does not bar seniority relief for post-Act victims of discriminatory refusal to hire, and retroactive seniority dating from the job application is a form of relief generally appropriate under § 706(g). Courts should start from a presumption in favor of such relief and may deny it only for reasons that would not frustrate Title VII's purposes if applied generally.","reasoning":"A live controversy remained because, in a certified class action, an adversary relationship continued between Bowman and the unnamed class members, who were identifiable, still wanted seniority, and some were already working as OTR drivers. Section 703(h) appears to be a definitional provision aimed at attacks on seniority systems that perpetuate pre-Act discrimination, and its legislative history shows no intent to limit remedies once a post-Act discriminatory practice is proved. Section 706(g) gives courts broad equitable power to serve Title VII's objective \"to make persons whole for injuries suffered on account of unlawful employment discrimination\". Hiring without seniority falls short, because the victim would stay subordinate in layoffs, job bidding, vacations and pensions, and NLRA practice, the model for § 706(g), routinely pairs hiring relief with retroactive seniority. The District Court's reasons failed: lack of EEOC charges by class members was rejected in Albemarle Paper, concerns about vacancy and qualification arise only when individuals reapply and Bowman bears the burden then, and harm to other employees' expectations cannot alone justify denial. The Court noted that district courts keep discretion to modify relief for circumstances peculiar to a case.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred in part and dissented in part, agreeing generally with Justice Powell but stressing that competitive-type seniority relief at the expense of innocent employees can rarely be equitable, and that a monetary award such as front pay would be better. Justice Powell, joined by Justice Rehnquist, concurred in Part I and in the holding of Part II, but dissented from Parts III and IV: benefit-type seniority is like backpay, but competitive-type seniority falls on innocent incumbents rather than the employer, so § 706(g)'s grant of equitable discretion requires district courts to weigh the equities case by case, and he would remand for that weighing.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the District Court for further proceedings. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Stewart, White, Marshall and Blackmun, and in Part I by Justice Powell. Justice Stevens took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep424/usrep424747/usrep424747.pdf"},"1973/72-1598":{"facts":"A union petitioned the NLRB for an election among 25 buyers in the purchasing department of Bell Aerospace's Wheatfield, New York plant. Buyers had broad discretion to select vendors, negotiate terms and sign purchase orders up to $50,000, subject to approval above $5,000 and to team input on some projects. The company argued the buyers were \"managerial employees\" and so not covered by the National Labor Relations Act.","procedure":"The Board, relying on its North Arkansas decision, held that managerial employees are covered unless union membership would create a conflict of interest in labor relations, found no such conflict shown, directed an election, certified the union, and ordered the company to bargain after it refused. The Second Circuit denied enforcement, holding that Congress excluded all true managerial employees and that, since the Board had long held buyers managerial, it could change position only through rulemaking, and remanded. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the NLRA exclude all \"managerial employees\" from its protections, or only those whose union membership would create a conflict of interest in labor relations? And must the Board use rulemaking rather than adjudication to decide whether buyers are managerial employees?","holding":"Congress intended to exclude all employees properly classified as managerial from the Act, not just those with labor-relations conflicts of interest. The Board is not required to proceed by rulemaking rather than adjudication in deciding whether these buyers are managerial.","reasoning":"The Court relied on the Board's early decisions treating managerial employees as outside bargaining units and never certifying a unit of them, and on the Taft-Hartley legislative history. The 1947 reports show concern about unionizing management, and the conference committee treated labor-relations and confidential employees as impliedly excluded, which the Court read to include executives and other managerial employees. The Board consistently held managerial employees outside the Act for over two decades, Congress left that reading untouched in the 1959 amendments, and the courts of appeals uniformly approved it, so the Board could not adopt a narrower exclusion. On rulemaking, SEC v. Chenery II and NLRB v. Wyman-Gordon establish that an agency may announce new principles in adjudication and that the choice lies in its informed discretion. Adjudication suits this context because of the very large number of buyers and the wide variation in their duties, and an agency should not \"exalt form over necessity.\" The Court expressed no view on whether these buyers are in fact managerial.","separate":"Justice White, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart and Marshall, concurred in Part III (no rulemaking required) but dissented from Part II. He argued that the Act covers \"any employee\" except those expressly excluded, that supervisors and professionals were addressed specifically while managerial employees were not, that before 1947 the Board excluded managerial employees only from rank-and-file units and had never held them outside the Act, and that congressional silence does not show approval of the Board's later Swift decision, so the Board's narrower exclusion was permissible.","disposition":"Affirmed in part, reversed in part, and remanded (to the Court of Appeals with directions to remand to the Board). Justice Powell delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Douglas, Blackmun and Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep416/usrep416267/usrep416267.pdf"},"1969/540":{"facts":"New York administered the federally supported Aid to Families With Dependent Children (AFDC) program. After Congress added a requirement in 42 U.S.C. 602(a)(23) that States adjust their standards of need for changes in living costs by July 1, 1969, New York enacted section 131-a of its Social Services Law. The new law replaced individualized grants and special-needs grants with maximum family allowances based on family size and the average age of the oldest child. The change cut benefits to many recipients, and cut New York City benefits by almost $40,000,000. Welfare recipients sued, first on an equal protection claim about unequal Nassau County payments, with a pendent claim that the new state law conflicted with the federal statute.","procedure":"A three-judge court found the equal protection claim moot after the State amended the law, dissolved itself, and remanded to the single District Judge. The District Judge decided the statutory claim for the recipients and issued an injunction. The Court of Appeals reversed, holding that the District Judge should not have reached the merits and that the federal statute did not affect New York. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Could the District Court hear the federal statutory claim as a pendent claim after the original constitutional claim became moot, and if so, is New York's new welfare program consistent with section 402(a)(23) of the Social Security Act?","holding":"The District Court had jurisdiction and properly exercised its discretion to reach the merits; neither exhaustion of administrative remedies nor primary jurisdiction required deferral to HEW. Section 402(a)(23) invalidates a state program that substantially reduces the content of the standard of need unless the State shows the dropped items no longer formed part of the reality of existence for most recipients, and New York's elimination of special grants failed that test.","reasoning":"On jurisdiction, the Court said mootness of the primary claim goes to the court's discretion, not its power, and that judicial energy already spent and the federal character of the statutory claim favored deciding it. It found the administrative doctrines inapplicable because recipients seek no review of an agency order and HEW had no procedure by which they could participate. On the merits, the Court read the statute as having two aims: to make States face the real magnitude of need and to push them to apportion payments more equitably. A State may reduce the percentage of need it pays, but it “may not obscure the actual standard of need.” It rejected both the District Court's broad reading and the Court of Appeals' view that the statute did not affect New York. The record, including the State's own regulations and its 1968 pilot project, showed that special grants covered essentials such as laundry and telephones, so their elimination significantly cut the content of the standard. Averaging of items remained permissible. Because compliance would be very costly and no single provision could be severed, the Court remanded so New York could revise its program, with an injunction against use of federal funds if it did not.","separate":"Justice Douglas concurred, adding that the federal statutory claim was within the pendent jurisdiction of the three-judge court, that the discretionary factors of economy, convenience and fairness favored exercising it, and that recipients cannot be required to exhaust remedies before HEW because HEW gives them no way to participate. Justice Black, joined by the Chief Justice, dissented. He argued that Congress gave HEW primary responsibility for deciding whether state plans conform, that HEW was already reviewing the New York law, and that federal courts should not rule on such conflicts before a final HEW decision.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the Court of Appeals for further proceedings consistent with the opinion, with directions that the District Court set a date for New York to revise its program. Justice Harlan delivered the opinion of the Court; the text shown does not state a vote count, but Justice Douglas concurred and Justice Black, joined by the Chief Justice, dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep397/usrep397397/usrep397397.pdf"},"1970/338":{"facts":"The University of Illinois Foundation owned the Isbell patent on a television antenna. In an earlier suit against another manufacturer, Winegard, a federal district court held the patent invalid as obvious, and the Eighth Circuit affirmed. The Foundation separately sued a customer of Blonder-Tongue Laboratories in the Northern District of Illinois, and Blonder-Tongue defended and counterclaimed. That court disagreed with the Winegard result and held the patent valid and infringed.","procedure":"The Seventh Circuit affirmed the finding that the Isbell patent was valid and infringed, creating a conflict with the Eighth Circuit. The Supreme Court granted certiorari and asked the parties to address whether Triplett v. Lowell should be adhered to and, if not, whether the Winegard invalidity ruling bound the Foundation.","question":"Should the Triplett rule, that a judgment of patent invalidity is not binding on the patentee in later suits against different defendants, be maintained, or may an accused infringer plead estoppel based on an earlier declaration of invalidity?","holding":"Triplett is overruled to the extent that it forecloses an estoppel plea by one charged with infringing a patent already declared invalid. The patentee may show that he lacked a full and fair chance to litigate validity in the first case, and trial courts decide on the basis of justice and equity.","reasoning":"The Court traced Triplett to the doctrine of mutuality of estoppel and described how many courts had abandoned that doctrine, especially since the California decision in Bernhard. It asked whether it is still tenable to give a litigant more than one full and fair opportunity to litigate the same issue. It rejected the argument that patent cases are so complex that repeated litigation is needed as a safeguard, noting that the patentee chose the first forum and could present all its evidence. It stressed the economic costs of Triplett: patent suits are expensive, patents are presumed valid, and accused infringers often settle or take licenses rather than relitigate, which hurts competitors and consumers. The burden on the courts was a lesser consideration. The patentee must be allowed to show it lacked “a fair opportunity procedurally, substantively and evidentially to pursue his claim the first time.” Because Triplett had discouraged Blonder-Tongue from pleading estoppel, the Court remanded so the pleadings could be amended and the record supplemented.","disposition":"Judgment of the Court of Appeals vacated and the case remanded to the District Court for further proceedings. Justice White delivered the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep402/usrep402313/usrep402313.pdf"},"1976/75-1153":{"facts":"Michigan law allowed a union and a local government employer to agree to an agency shop, under which every represented employee, whether or not a union member, must pay the union a service charge equal to dues. The Detroit Federation of Teachers and the Detroit Board of Education agreed to such a clause, and a teacher who did not pay could be discharged. Teachers who were unwilling to pay, who opposed public-sector collective bargaining, or who objected to union political and ideological activities sued in state court. They alleged that the union spent part of the fees on economic, political, professional, scientific and religious activities they did not approve, unrelated to collective bargaining.","procedure":"The trial court dismissed the complaints for failure to state a claim. The Michigan Court of Appeals upheld the agency-shop clause on its face under Railway Employes' Dept. v. Hanson, but held that the teachers could get no restitution because they had not told the union which causes and candidates they objected to. The teachers appealed to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does the First and Fourteenth Amendments' protection of freedom of association bar a public employer from requiring non-member employees to pay an agency-shop service charge to a union, either at all or to the extent the money funds union activities unrelated to collective bargaining?","holding":"A public-sector agency shop is constitutional insofar as the fees finance collective bargaining, contract administration and grievance adjustment. The Constitution does not allow a public employee to be compelled, as a condition of employment, to support union ideological or political causes unrelated to those duties.","reasoning":"The Court treated Hanson and Street, which upheld union shops under the Railway Labor Act, as controlling for bargaining-related expenses, because the same interests in exclusive representation, labor peace and preventing free riders apply in the public sector. It rejected the arguments that government employment calls for a different result and that public-sector bargaining is so political that any compelled support is ideological conformity, finding that public employees' First Amendment interests are no weightier and that they remain free to express their views and take part in politics. On the second question, it held that compelling contributions for political purposes infringes First Amendment freedom of belief and association just as prohibiting them would. The Constitution requires only that ideological expenditures not germane to collective bargaining be financed by those who do not object. The Court did not draw the line between the two categories on this bare pleading record. As to remedy, it held that the state court erred in requiring objectors to name specific expenditures, following Railway Clerks v. Allen, and that remedies such as a refund or reduction of fees proportional to ideological spending might be available. It suggested that the parties might first use the union's newly adopted internal refund procedure.","separate":"Justice Rehnquist concurred, joining the Court only because he believes the Elrod v. Burns dissent states the governing principles, and agreeing that public-sector bargaining positions touch political concerns. Justice Stevens concurred, saying that by joining he did not imply that the Street and Allen remedies would necessarily be adequate, and that the union may be required to adopt procedures that avoid even temporary use of objectors' funds for ideological activities. Justice Powell, joined by the Chief Justice and Justice Blackmun, concurred in the judgment. He agreed that the complaints state a cause of action but argued that Hanson and Street do not control because here the government itself is the employer, that any compelled support of a public-sector union implicates the First Amendment, and that the State should bear the burden of showing that each compelled contribution serves paramount governmental interests.","disposition":"Judgment of the Michigan Court of Appeals vacated and the case remanded for further proceedings not inconsistent with the opinion. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, White, Marshall, Rehnquist and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep431/usrep431209/usrep431209.pdf"},"1976/75-6521":{"facts":"Donald Abney, Larry Starks and Alonzo Robinson were indicted with two others in a single count charging conspiracy and attempt to obstruct interstate commerce by extortion in violation of the Hobbs Act. The Government's case rested on a tavern owner who said the defendants had extorted weekly payments from him and who recorded the last payment. Petitioners argued that the single count was duplicitous. The trial court did not dismiss it or make the prosecutor choose, but required proof of all elements of both offenses and instructed the jury so. The jury convicted each petitioner.","procedure":"The Third Circuit reversed the convictions because the tape recording had not been properly authenticated, agreed that the indictment was duplicitous, and ordered the Government to elect between conspiracy and attempt on retrial. The Government elected conspiracy, and petitioners moved to dismiss on double jeopardy grounds and for failure to charge an offense. The District Court denied the motion, and the Court of Appeals affirmed on an immediate appeal without addressing its jurisdiction. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Is a pretrial order denying a motion to dismiss an indictment on double jeopardy grounds a “final decision” under 28 U.S.C. 1291 that may be appealed immediately, and if so, does double jeopardy bar this retrial and may the court of appeals also review petitioners' other challenge to the indictment?","holding":"A pretrial order rejecting a double jeopardy claim is a final decision under section 1291 and may be appealed immediately, under the collateral order exception. Other claims in the same motion, such as the sufficiency of the indictment, are not immediately appealable. On the merits, double jeopardy did not bar retrial on the conspiracy charge.","reasoning":"The Court began with the principle that appeals are creatures of statute and that Congress has a firm policy against piecemeal appeals. It then applied the Cohen collateral order test. The denial of a double jeopardy claim is a complete and final rejection in the trial court, and the claim is collateral to and separable from guilt or innocence. The Court also reasoned that the Double Jeopardy Clause protects against being tried twice, not only against being punished twice, so the right would be significantly undermined if review waited until after a second trial. Petitioners' attack on the sufficiency of the indictment is not collateral, goes to the heart of the trial, and can be reviewed after final judgment, so the Court of Appeals lacked jurisdiction over it at this stage. On the merits, the trial court had instructed the jury that it must find all elements of both conspiracy and attempt, and the Court would not assume the jury disregarded that instruction. The jury therefore did not acquit petitioners of conspiracy, and retrial on that charge is not barred.","separate":"Justice White concurred in the judgment without a written opinion in the text shown.","disposition":"Affirmed in part, reversed in part, and remanded. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart, Marshall, Blackmun, Powell, Rehnquist and Stevens; Justice White concurred in the judgment.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep431/usrep431651/usrep431651.pdf"},"1969/768":{"facts":"Boys Markets and Retail Clerks Union Local 770 were parties to a collective-bargaining agreement that required all disputes over its interpretation or application to go through grievance and arbitration procedures and barred any work stoppage, lockout, picketing or boycott during the contract. A dispute arose when the company's frozen foods supervisor and non-unit crew members began rearranging merchandise in a supermarket's frozen food cases, and a union representative demanded that union personnel restock the cases. When the company refused, the union struck and picketed. The company demanded an end to the stoppage and tried to invoke the contract's arbitration procedures.","procedure":"The company sued in California Superior Court and obtained a temporary restraining order. The union removed the case to federal district court, which ordered arbitration and enjoined the strike and picketing. The Ninth Circuit reversed, considering itself bound by Sinclair Refining Co. v. Atkinson, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Norris-LaGuardia Act's ban on federal injunctions in labor disputes bar a federal court from enjoining a strike that breaches a no-strike clause when the underlying dispute is subject to binding arbitration under a collective-bargaining agreement, and should Sinclair v. Atkinson be overruled?","holding":"The Court overruled Sinclair and held that the Norris-LaGuardia Act does not bar an injunction against a strike over an arbitrable grievance in these circumstances. The holding is narrow and applies where the contract has a mandatory grievance or arbitration procedure; the district court must first order arbitration and must find that ordinary principles of equity support an injunction.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan said stare decisis is a policy rather than a mechanical formula and that congressional silence after Sinclair is not a reason to refuse reconsideration. He reasoned that Avco, which allows removal of Section 301(a) suits from state to federal court, combined with Sinclair to strip state courts of injunction power in such suits, contrary to Congress's purpose to supplement rather than displace state jurisdiction. Extending Sinclair to the States would instead remove the principal enforcement tool for no-strike pledges, which are the quid pro quo for an employer's agreement to arbitrate, and damages are no substitute for an immediate halt to a strike. Statutes must be read together, and the Norris-LaGuardia Act was aimed at abuses of injunctions against organizing, not at enforcing a union's own contractual promise to arbitrate rather than strike, as in Chicago River. The Court adopted the Sinclair dissent's guidelines: arbitration must be ordered as a condition of the injunction, and the court must weigh irreparable injury and the balance of hardships. It stated that \"Our holding in the present case is a narrow one.\"","separate":"Justice Stewart concurred, explaining that he had joined Sinclair in 1962 but later concluded it should be reconsidered, and that he now joins the Court's opinion. Justice Black dissented, arguing that Sinclair was correctly decided, that Congress had been invited to change the law and declined, and that changing a settled statutory interpretation because the Court's membership changed is a legislative act. Justice White dissented for the reasons stated in the majority opinion in Sinclair. Justice Marshall took no part.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded with directions to enter a judgment affirming the District Court's order. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court; Justice Marshall took no part, Justice Stewart concurred, and Justices Black and White dissented. The text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep398/usrep398235/usrep398235.pdf"},"1971/70-69":{"facts":"Orito was indicted for knowingly transporting obscene film material by common carrier in interstate commerce, from San Francisco to Milwaukee by two airlines, in violation of 18 U.S.C. section 1462. The materials included about 83 reels of film, with several copies of some. He moved to dismiss, arguing the statute violated his First and Ninth Amendment rights.","procedure":"The District Court granted the motion and held the statute unconstitutionally overbroad because it did not distinguish between public and nonpublic transportation of obscene material, relying on Griswold, Redrup and Stanley v. Georgia. The United States appealed directly to the Supreme Court, and the case was argued and then reargued.","question":"Does the First Amendment, through the privacy right recognized in Stanley v. Georgia, bar Congress from prohibiting the interstate transportation of obscene material by common carrier, even when the transport is private or the material is for the transporter's own use?","holding":"No. Congress may regulate the interstate transportation of obscene material, which the First Amendment does not protect, and the privacy zone of Stanley v. Georgia does not extend beyond the home. The District Court's dismissal on privacy grounds was error, and the case was remanded to reconsider the sufficiency of the indictment in light of Miller v. California and related decisions.","reasoning":"Chief Justice Burger rejected the argument that Stanley's right to possess obscene material at home creates a correlative right to receive, transport or distribute it, noting that Thirty-seven Photographs and Reidel negate any zone of privacy that follows such material outside the home. He wrote that \"The Constitution extends special safeguards to the privacy of the home,\" but that viewing obscene films in a commercial theater or shipping them on common carriers has no claim to such consideration. Three points carried the result: \"obscene material is not protected under the First Amendment,\" the government has a legitimate interest in keeping such material out of the stream of commerce, and no constitutionally protected privacy is involved. A transporter's private use or private carriage is not controlling, because Congress may rely on the legislatively determined risk of eventual exposure to juveniles or the public. Because the case came up on a summary dismissal, the obscenity of the films was never determined, and the Court noted that for the first time since Roth it had majority-accepted standards for obscenity in Miller and 12 200-ft. Reels.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, reasoning that under Stanley a person who reads or carries an obscene book on a journey for personal enjoyment is protected, so section 1462 is overbroad, and he would affirm unless Stanley is overruled. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, dissented, relying on his dissent in Paris Adult Theatre I v. Slaton and Miller to conclude that the statute is overbroad and unconstitutional on its face, and he would affirm.","disposition":"Vacated and remanded. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices White, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas, Brennan, Stewart and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413139/usrep413139.pdf"},"1974/74-165":{"facts":"Eight major railroads in the northeast and midwest entered reorganization under section 77 of the Bankruptcy Act, creating a rail transportation crisis. Congress responded with the Regional Rail Reorganization Act of 1973, which created the United States Railway Association (USRA) to draft a Final System Plan and required designated rail properties to be conveyed to a new private corporation, Conrail, in exchange for Conrail securities, up to $500 million of federally guaranteed USRA obligations, and other benefits. Section 304(f) restricted railroads from discontinuing service until the plan took effect. Creditors and the sole stockholder of Penn Central, along with trustees, challenged the Act, arguing it took property without just compensation through interim losses (the \"erosion taking\") and through the final conveyance (the \"conveyance taking\"), and that it violated the Bankruptcy Clause's uniformity requirement.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court for the Eastern District of Pennsylvania held the conveyance taking claim premature, but held the erosion claim ripe, found that the Rail Act precluded any Tucker Act remedy, and declared section 304(f) and section 303 invalid to the extent they required continued loss operations without compensation. It enjoined USRA from certifying the plan and held part of section 207(b) nonuniform under the Bankruptcy Clause. The United States, USRA and the Penn Central Trustees appealed directly, and the New Haven Trustee cross-appealed.","question":"Is a Tucker Act suit in the Court of Claims available, and adequate, to compensate any unconstitutional taking effected by the Rail Act through interim erosion or the final conveyance, which of the conveyance issues are ripe, and does the Act, which applies to railroads in one defined region, satisfy the uniformity requirement of the Bankruptcy Clause?","holding":"The Court reversed. The Rail Act does not withdraw the Tucker Act remedy, which is available to cover any compensation shortfall from either the erosion or the conveyance, and that remedy makes the Act valid as a reorganization statute that satisfies due process. The basic conveyance issues are ripe, though valuation questions are not, and the Act satisfies the Bankruptcy Clause's uniformity requirement.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan held the erosion issue ripe because Penn Central was not reorganizable on an income basis and compelled loss operations could erode creditors' interests with no assurance of compensation. He found that the District Court asked the wrong question, since the issue is whether Congress withdrew the Tucker Act's jurisdiction over claims founded on the Constitution, and the Act's text, funding provisions, section 601 and legislative history show no such withdrawal. Disfavored implied repeals and the canon of construing statutes to avoid constitutional doubt, with Lynch v. United States, point to the same result. On the conveyance, he held the basic issues ripe because the conveyance is virtually certain and irreversible, with no better time to decide, but held valuation methods premature. He rejected the claim that the Act is an eminent domain statute, treating it as an exercise of the bankruptcy power under which compensation in securities backed by a Tucker Act remedy is adequate. On uniformity, he held that Congress may address geographically isolated problems and that the Act applies to all railroads in reorganization, so that \"The uniformity clause requires that the Rail Act apply equally to all creditors and all debtors.\"","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, concluding that the Act effects a taking without assured just compensation and amounts to a fraudulent conveyance, that the Rail Act's expedited structure and limits on federal funds show Congress meant to displace a Tucker Act remedy, that the Court's saving construction rewrites the statute, and that the Act lacks the geographic uniformity the Bankruptcy Clause requires. Justice Stewart dissented from the opinion and judgment substantially for the reasons in Part II of Douglas's dissent.","disposition":"Reversed (judgment of the three-judge District Court, 383 F. Supp. 510). Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Marshall, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas and Stewart dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep419/usrep419102/usrep419102.pdf"},"1969/85":{"facts":"Petitioners sell data processing services to businesses generally. They sued to challenge a ruling of the Comptroller of the Currency that national banks may, as an incident to their banking services, make data processing services available to other banks and bank customers. They alleged that competition from national banks would cost them profits and that respondent American National Bank & Trust Company was performing or preparing to perform such services for two customers with whom petitioner Data Systems, Inc., had agreed or negotiated.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the complaint because petitioners lacked standing to sue. The Court of Appeals affirmed, applying a test that required a legal interest from a public charter, contract, or statute, or a public interest recognized by Congress. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Do competitors of national banks have standing under Article III and the Administrative Procedure Act to challenge the Comptroller's ruling, and has Congress precluded judicial review of that ruling?","holding":"Yes to both parts. The petitioners have standing: they allege injury in fact, and their interest is arguably within the zone of interests protected or regulated by the relevant statutes. Congress did not preclude judicial review of the Comptroller's ruling.","reasoning":"The Court separated the Article III inquiry from the merits. The first question is whether the challenged action caused the plaintiff injury in fact, and petitioners' alleged economic harm met that test. The Court of Appeals's \"legal interest\" test went to the merits and was not a proper standing test. The further question is whether the interest is \"arguably within the zone of interests to be protected or regulated by the statute,\" which fits the Administrative Procedure Act's grant of review to persons aggrieved by agency action. Section 4 of the Bank Service Corporation Act, which limits bank service corporations to bank services for banks, arguably brings a competitor within that zone. On preclusion, the Court read the APA as broadly remedial, saying there is \"no presumption against judicial review and in favor of administrative absolutism\" unless a contrary purpose is fairly discernible, and it found none in the Bank Service Corporation Act or the National Bank Act. Whether petitioners have a legal interest and whether the Comptroller violated the statutes are merits questions left for the lower courts.","separate":"The text notes that Justice Brennan filed a separate opinion (post, p. 167), but that opinion is not included in the provided text, so its content cannot be described.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for a hearing on the merits. Justice Douglas delivered the opinion of the Court; the opinion text does not state a vote count.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep397/usrep397150/usrep397150.pdf"},"1970/133":{"facts":"Milton Luros returned from Europe on October 24, 1969, with 37 photographs in his luggage. Customs agents seized them as obscene under 19 U.S.C. § 1305(a), which bars importing obscene material and directs seizure and a forfeiture suit by the district attorney. The U.S. Attorney filed a forfeiture action on November 6. Luros denied obscenity and counterclaimed that the statute was unconstitutional, and the parties stipulated that the photographs were meant for a hard cover edition of The Kama Sutra of Vatsyayana.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court declared § 1305(a) unconstitutional on two grounds: it lacked the time limits required by Freedman v. Maryland, and under Stanley v. Georgia it was overbroad because it reached importation for private use. The Government appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does § 1305(a) satisfy the procedural requirements of Freedman v. Maryland, and does Stanley v. Georgia bar its application to obscene material that a traveler brings in from abroad?","holding":"The statute can be construed to require suit within 14 days of seizure and a final district court decision within 60 days of filing (excluding claimant-caused delay), which avoids the Freedman problem. Congress may bar importation of obscene material, including material intended for private use, and Stanley does not protect that importation.","reasoning":"Justice White noted that Freedman, Teitel and Blount struck down censorship schemes lacking prompt judicial review, but that the Court should first look for a fairly possible construction that avoids a constitutional question. Unlike the state laws in Freedman and Teitel, a federal statute can be construed authoritatively by this Court, and unlike the Blount statute it needed no wholesale rewriting. The Senate debates showed concern that courts decide promptly, so reading in time limits fits congressional purpose. Reviewing lower-court cases, the Court chose 14 and 60 days as workable, and the Government's filing 13 days after seizure met them. On Stanley, a port of entry is not a home, and since \"obscenity is not within the scope of First Amendment protection,\" Congress may declare it contraband and exclude it, as the Court also held in United States v. Reidel.","separate":"Justice Harlan concurred in the judgment and in Part I, and concurred only in the judgment on Part II. He would have held that Luros, who stipulated to commercial importation, lacked standing to raise the overbreadth claim for private importers. Justice Stewart concurred in the judgment and in Part I, agreed that border seizure of obscenity for commercial dissemination is permissible, but would not decide that the Government may seize material meant for the importer's purely private use. Justice Black, joined by Justice Douglas, dissented: he would deny Congress any power to censor, argued that the plurality's reasoning cannot be squared with Stanley, and said the Court improperly rewrote the statute instead of affirming. Justice Marshall's dissent appears in the companion case Reidel and is not included in this text.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice White announced the judgment and wrote the opinion; the Chief Justice and Justices Brennan and Blackmun joined all of it, and Justices Harlan and Stewart joined Part I only and concurred in the judgment.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep402/usrep402363/usrep402363.pdf"},"1975/75-251":{"facts":"Present and retired male employees of Connecticut sued state officials on behalf of a class, alleging that the state retirement plan discriminated on the basis of sex in violation of Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964. Congress had amended Title VII in 1972 to cover states as employers. The employees sought retroactive retirement benefits and attorneys' fees.","procedure":"The District Court found a Title VII violation and granted prospective injunctive relief, but denied retroactive benefits and fees as barred by the Eleventh Amendment under Edelman v. Jordan. The Court of Appeals affirmed on benefits but reversed on attorneys' fees, treating them as only an ancillary effect on the state treasury. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on both petitions.","question":"May Congress, acting under § 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment, authorize federal courts to award money damages against a state government despite the Eleventh Amendment?","holding":"Yes. The Eleventh Amendment and the state sovereignty it embodies are limited by § 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment, so Congress may provide for suits against states that would be impermissible in other contexts. The backpay award is therefore not barred, and neither is the fee award authorized by Title VII.","reasoning":"The Court began where Edelman ended: here the \"threshold fact of congressional authorization\" to sue the state as employer is present, since the parties agreed Congress acted under § 5. The Fourteenth Amendment's substantive provisions are directed at the States, and Ex parte Virginia and later cases recognized that Congress's enforcement power can intrude on spheres once reserved to the States. The Court concluded that the Eleventh Amendment and state sovereignty are \"necessarily limited by the enforcement provisions of § 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment.\" Because Title VII expressly authorizes fees, the same holding means Congress's exercise of power there is also not barred, so the Court did not decide the Edelman ancillary-effect question.","separate":"Justice Brennan concurred in the judgment, reasoning that the Eleventh Amendment does not literally apply to a suit by a state's own citizens and that the States surrendered sovereign immunity in the plan of the Convention as to Congress's enumerated powers. Justice Stevens concurred in the judgment, relying on the commerce power and Ex parte Young and doubting that § 5 adequately answers the defense; he thought Edelman's holding did not cover this award and would treat fees like other litigation costs.","disposition":"No. 75-251 reversed; No. 75-283 affirmed. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Stewart, White, Marshall, Blackmun and Powell.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep427/usrep427445/usrep427445.pdf"},"1976/75-1053":{"facts":"Jones, a Riverside County weights and measures director, ordered Rath Packing Co.'s bacon and flour from three millers (General Mills, Pillsbury, Seaboard) off sale. He found, by the statistical sampling method of California's Article 5, that lot average net weights fell below the stated weights, in violation of Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 12211. California's method allows for manufacturing variation but not for moisture loss during good distribution practice. Federal law permits reasonable variations from stated weight, including moisture loss.","procedure":"Rath and the millers sued in federal district court for declarations that the California provisions were pre-empted and for injunctions. The District Court granted relief and the Court of Appeals affirmed in relevant part; the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Do the Federal Meat Inspection Act, the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act and the Fair Packaging and Labeling Act pre-empt California's net-weight enforcement scheme as applied to bacon and flour?","holding":"As to bacon, California's law is expressly pre-empted by the FMIA's ban on labeling requirements \"different than\" federal ones. As to flour, the FPLA's express clause does not pre-empt the state law, but enforcement would obstruct the FPLA's purposes and so it must yield under the Supremacy Clause.","reasoning":"The Court started from the presumption that historic state police powers are not displaced absent a clear congressional purpose, and noted that conflict pre-emption asks whether state law obstructs congressional objectives. For bacon, FMIA § 408 bars labeling requirements different than the federal ones, and California's refusal to allow moisture-loss variation differs from the federal rule; Jones's argument that accuracy requirements are not labeling requirements was rejected. For flour, the Court concluded that the FDCA and FPLA together also permit reasonable variations, and that the FPLA's \"less stringent\" and \"different information\" clauses did not reach the California law. It then held that a major FPLA purpose is to \"facilitate value comparisons among similar products,\" and that California's standard would force national millers to overpack and make identically labeled packages contain different amounts of flour solids, which would mislead consumers.","separate":"Justice Rehnquist, joined by Justice Stewart, concurred in part and dissented in part. He agreed with express pre-emption as to bacon and the absence of express pre-emption as to flour, but dissented from implied pre-emption of the flour claims, arguing that compliance with both laws is possible and that the majority relied on unproved factual speculation about milling and distribution.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Marshall wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Brennan, White, Blackmun, Powell and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep430/usrep430519/usrep430519.pdf"},"1977/76-419":{"facts":"Vermont Yankee sought an operating license for a nuclear plant, and Consumers Power a construction permit for two reactors. In the Vermont Yankee matter the Atomic Energy Commission excluded the environmental effects of fuel reprocessing and waste disposal from the licensing hearing, then held rulemaking proceedings without discovery or cross-examination and adopted a fuel cycle rule. In the Consumers Power matter, an intervenor group, Saginaw, raised energy conservation contentions but largely declined to participate, and the Commission refused to reopen the proceeding; intervenors also challenged the Advisory Committee on Reactor Safeguards report.","procedure":"The D.C. Circuit overturned the fuel cycle rule as procedurally inadequate and remanded the Vermont Yankee license. In the Consumers Power case it held the environmental impact statement defective for not examining energy conservation, held the ACRS report inadequate, and remanded. The Supreme Court granted certiorari in both cases.","question":"May a reviewing court require an agency to use rulemaking procedures beyond those in the Administrative Procedure Act, and did the Court of Appeals properly overturn the Commission's handling of energy conservation and the ACRS report?","holding":"Section 553 of the APA generally sets the maximum procedural requirements courts may impose on agency rulemaking, so the Court of Appeals could not overturn the rule for the procedures used. The court also erred in rejecting the Commission's energy conservation threshold test and in requiring the ACRS report to be returned for elaboration.","reasoning":"The Court said that APA § 553 \"established the maximum procedural requirements\" Congress was willing to have courts impose, that circumstances justifying more are \"extremely rare,\" and that agencies have long been left to design their own procedures. It found the Court of Appeals had in effect faulted the procedures, and that neither NEPA, the nature of the issues nor past practice justified more. Judicial insistence on the perceived best procedures would make review unpredictable and push agencies toward full adjudicatory hearings, and the adequacy of a rulemaking record turns on following the statutes, not on particular procedural devices. On energy conservation, the concept of alternatives is evolving and bounded by feasibility, and intervenors must make their contentions meaningful. The ACRS report remand was an unjustified intrusion. The Court remanded the fuel cycle rule for ordinary arbitrary-and-capricious review.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, in which all participating Members joined; Justices Blackmun and Powell took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep435/usrep435519/usrep435519.pdf"},"1978/77-752":{"facts":"Unions won Board-supervised elections as bargaining representatives of lay teachers at two groups of Catholic high schools: the Quigley schools run by the Catholic Bishop of Chicago and five schools of the Diocese of Fort Wayne-South Bend. The schools combine religious instruction with a secular curriculum. They refused to recognize or bargain with the unions.","procedure":"The NLRB asserted jurisdiction because the schools were not \"completely religious\" and ordered them to bargain. The Seventh Circuit denied enforcement, holding the Board's standard unworkable and that the Religion Clauses barred jurisdiction. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the National Labor Relations Act give the NLRB jurisdiction over lay teachers in church-operated schools that teach both religious and secular subjects, and if so, does exercising it violate the Religion Clauses?","holding":"The Act should not be construed to cover lay teachers in church-operated schools. Because jurisdiction would raise serious First Amendment questions and Congress expressed no affirmative intention to include them, the Board lacked authority to issue the orders.","reasoning":"Chief Justice Burger first addressed statutory coverage, applying the rule that an Act of Congress should not be read to violate the Constitution if another construction is available. Where a reading would raise serious constitutional questions, the Court requires \"the affirmative intention of the Congress clearly expressed.\" He found that exercising jurisdiction would present \"a significant risk that the First Amendment will be infringed,\" because the Board's inquiry into good faith and religious mission, and the scope of mandatory bargaining over terms and conditions of employment, would entangle it with church schools, as in Lemon, Meek and Wolman. The text and history of the 1935, 1947 and 1974 enactments show no such intention, and Associated Press v. NLRB was distinguished because no First Amendment problem appeared there. The Court therefore declined to construe the Act to reach these teachers and did not decide the constitutional claims.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices White, Marshall and Blackmun, dissented. He argued that the Court's \"affirmative intention\" rule departs from the settled canon requiring only a fairly possible avoiding construction; that the Act covers all employers not expressly excepted; and that Congress rejected similar exemptions in 1947 and again in 1974, so the Court amended rather than construed the statute.","disposition":"Affirmed (denial of enforcement). Chief Justice Burger wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Stewart, Powell, Rehnquist and Stevens; Justice Brennan's dissent was joined by Justices White, Marshall and Blackmun.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep440/usrep440490/usrep440490.pdf"},"1978/77-5781":{"facts":"Police received a radio report of a clothing-store robbery and stopped a car they thought was the getaway vehicle. The owner was driving, and petitioners were passengers. After ordering the occupants out, officers searched the car and found rifle shells in the locked glove compartment and a sawed-off rifle under the front passenger seat. Petitioners were arrested and later convicted of armed robbery, with the rifle and shells admitted at trial. Petitioners did not own the car and never claimed to own the rifle or shells.","procedure":"The Illinois trial court denied petitioners' motion to suppress because they lacked standing to challenge the search of the car, and the Illinois Appellate Court affirmed on the ground that a mere passenger without a proprietary or similar interest in a car lacks standing. The Illinois Supreme Court denied leave to appeal, and the U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a criminal defendant who is a passenger in a car, and who claims neither ownership of the car nor of the items seized, invoke the Fourth Amendment and the exclusionary rule to challenge a search of the car, either because the search was 'directed' at him or because he was legitimately on the premises?","holding":"No. Fourth Amendment rights are personal and cannot be asserted vicariously, so the Court rejected the 'target' theory and held that the proper inquiry is whether the defendant's own Fourth Amendment rights were infringed, which turns on whether he had a legitimate expectation of privacy in the place searched. Petitioners, who showed no property or possessory interest in the car or the items seized, had not shown a legitimate expectation of privacy in the glove compartment or the area under the seat.","reasoning":"The Court began from the rule that \"Fourth Amendment rights are personal rights\", and declined to extend standing to anyone against whom a search was directed, citing the administrative difficulties of that approach and the social cost of applying the exclusionary rule more widely. It then concluded that the separate label of standing adds nothing, and that the question is better treated as part of substantive Fourth Amendment doctrine: whether the challenged search infringed an interest of the defendant that the Amendment protects. The Court held that the phrase from Jones v. United States, \"legitimately on premises\", is \"too broad a gauge for measurement of Fourth Amendment rights\", because it would let a casual visitor challenge a search of areas in which he had no legitimate expectation of privacy. Relying on Katz, it explained that Jones is best understood as a case in which the defendant had a legitimate expectation of privacy in the apartment he was using, and it stressed that property law concepts are relevant but not controlling. Applying this test, petitioners asserted no property or possessory interest in the car or the seized items, and as mere passengers they did not show a legitimate expectation of privacy in the glove compartment or under the seat. The Court distinguished Jones and Katz on their facts and said it had not held that only a common-law property interest suffices.","separate":"Justice Powell, joined by Chief Justice Burger, concurred. He read the Court's opinion as turning on legitimate expectations of privacy rather than property concepts, stressed that expectations of privacy in an automobile differ from those in a home, and concluded that these passengers, who had no control of the car or keys, had no reasonable expectation that the car would not be searched after they were stopped and made to leave it. Justice White, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall, and Stevens, dissented. He argued that the Court in effect ties the Fourth Amendment to property law, that Jones and later cases protect a person legitimately present in a private place with the owner's permission, that the Court's rule is not an easily applied bright line, and that it invites police to search vehicles carrying more than one occupant without fear of suppression. He agreed with the Court's rejection of target standing.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Powell, and Blackmun (5-4).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep439/usrep439128/usrep439128.pdf"},"1970/92":{"facts":"Residents of Marion and Lake Counties, Indiana, sued over state statutes that made Marion County (including Indianapolis) a multi-member district electing eight state senators and fifteen representatives at large. They claimed the arrangement diluted the votes of Black and poor residents of the Center Township \"ghetto,\" and that voters in multi-member districts were overrepresented compared with single-member districts because they have a greater theoretical chance to cast tie-breaking votes. The District Court found a ghetto area with distinct legislative interests whose residents had few legislators from their area, and that party control and bloc voting cancelled out their voting strength.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court held the statutes unconstitutional, concluded that population variances required redistricting the whole State, and, when the legislature did not act, imposed its own single-member plan. The Supreme Court stayed the judgment, so the 1970 elections used the old statutes, and later learned that Indiana had enacted new single-member legislation, but held the case not moot.","question":"Did Marion County's multi-member legislative district unconstitutionally dilute the voting strength of the ghetto's racial minority under the Equal Protection Clause, and was statewide redistricting properly ordered?","holding":"No. Multi-member districts are not inherently invalid, and on this record the challengers did not prove that the district denied ghetto residents equal access to the political process, so the District Court misread the Equal Protection Clause. A plurality also held that statewide redistricting could properly rest on the excessive population variances shown.","reasoning":"Prior cases held multi-member districts not per se illegal, but open to challenge if they operate to minimize or cancel out the voting strength of racial or political elements, with the challenger bearing the burden of proof. The mathematical theory that multi-member voters are overrepresented remained theoretical, and nothing showed that Marion County's delegation actually had more influence. There was no claim that the districts were designed to discriminate, and the ghetto's lack of seats proportional to population showed no denial of opportunity to register, vote, join a party or be slated, so the shortfall resulted more from the ghetto's preferred party losing elections, which the Court called a \"mere euphemism for political defeat at the polls.\" The findings also failed to show poor performance or unresponsiveness toward ghetto interests, and the District Court's logic would invite claims by any interest group and endless litigation. The Court further said the District Court had not justified abolishing every multi-member district in the State instead of narrower remedies. Part VII, in a plurality, upheld the statewide order because the population variances exceeded what the Court had found excessive in Swann v. Adams.","separate":"Justice Stewart joined Parts I-VI and dissented from Part VII for the reasons in his dissent in Lucas v. Colorado General Assembly. Justice Harlan filed a separate opinion criticizing the reapportionment decisions as resting on a majoritarian philosophy and as having led the Court into a mathematical and political morass, and he would have reversed with directions to dismiss the complaint. Justice Douglas, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented in part and concurred in the result in part. He would have affirmed, finding that the record showed invidious effects on an identifiable racial group, that no showing of racial motivation was needed, and that statewide redistricting was justified by population variances; he agreed the case was not moot.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings. Justice White announced the judgment and wrote the opinion of the Court as to Parts I-VI, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Black, Stewart and Blackmun, with Part VII joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Black and Blackmun.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep403/usrep403124/usrep403124.pdf"},"1973/72-812":{"facts":"California's Elections Code denied ballot position to an independent candidate who had been registered with a qualified party within one year before the preceding primary, or who had voted in that primary. It also required nominating petitions signed by 5% to 6% of the prior general-election vote, gathered in a 24-day window after the primary, with no signers who voted in the primary. Storer and Frommhagen, who had been registered Democrats until January and March 1972, sought independent ballot status for Congress. Hall and Tyner, Communist Party members whose party was not qualified, sought ballot status as independent candidates for President and Vice President.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court held that the statutes served a sufficiently important state interest and dismissed the complaints. The candidates appealed, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction and consolidated the cases.","question":"Does California's one-year party disaffiliation requirement for independent candidates violate the First and Fourteenth Amendments, and do the petition requirements (the percentage, the 24-day period, and the bar on primary voters signing) unconstitutionally burden independent presidential candidates' access to the ballot?","holding":"The disaffiliation rule is constitutional, so Storer and Frommhagen were properly barred. As to Hall and Tyner, the record was inadequate to decide whether the combined signature requirements unconstitutionally restrict ballot access, so the case was remanded for further findings.","reasoning":"The Court said the Williams v. Rhodes line of cases does not automatically invalidate every substantial election restriction; the result is a matter of degree that weighs the State's interests against the burden. It compared the rule to the waiting period upheld in Rosario v. Rockefeller and to California's parallel one-year rule for party candidates. It found that the rule protects the direct primary, which it called \"an integral part of the entire election process\", discourages candidacies driven by pique or short-range goals, and furthers the State's compelling interest in political stability. Because Storer and Frommhagen were validly barred, they could not challenge the other provisions. For Hall and Tyner, the Court stressed that key facts were unknown: the size of the pool of eligible signers after excluding primary voters, how the roughly 325,000 required signatures compare to that pool, and how past independents fared. It also said the State offered no justification for disqualifying nonpartisan primary voters, and that the new-party route is no substitute for independent candidacy.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall, dissented. He accepted that the State's interests are compelling but argued the State never showed that less burdensome means (a shorter disaffiliation period, or limiting the rule to those who actually ran in a party primary) would fail. He would also have found the petition requirements unconstitutional as applied to Hall and Tyner without a remand, because the data showed the signatures needed were about 9.5% of the available pool, to be gathered in only 24 days.","disposition":"Affirmed in part as to Storer and Frommhagen; vacated and remanded in part as to Hall and Tyner. Justice White wrote for the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, Powell, and Rehnquist; Justices Brennan, Douglas, and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep415/usrep415724/usrep415724.pdf"},"1973/72-1188":{"facts":"The Reservists Committee to Stop the War, an association of present and former Reserve members opposing U.S. involvement in Vietnam, and five members who were citizens and taxpayers sued the Secretary of Defense and the three Service Secretaries. They claimed that Members of Congress holding Reserve commissions violated the Incompatibility Clause of Art. I, § 6, cl. 2. They sought to represent several classes, including all citizens and all taxpayers, and alleged that Reserve membership exposed Members to executive influence and conflicting duties.","procedure":"The District Court held that respondents lacked standing as taxpayers, reservists, or war opponents, but had standing as citizens, and it granted partial summary judgment declaring that Members of Congress may not hold Reserve commissions. The Court of Appeals affirmed, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Do respondents have standing under Article III to challenge, as citizens or as federal taxpayers, the Reserve membership of Members of Congress under the Incompatibility Clause?","holding":"Respondents lack standing as citizens or as taxpayers. A generalized interest shared by all citizens in having the government follow the Constitution is an abstract injury, and respondents did not satisfy the Flast nexus requirement for taxpayer standing.","reasoning":"The Court said the Art. III case-or-controversy requirement demands a personal stake and concrete injury in fact. Respondents' only interest as citizens was the generalized interest of all citizens in constitutional governance, which is an abstract injury; the Court cited its recent statement that \"Abstract injury is not enough.\" It reaffirmed Ex parte Levitt, where a citizen's general interest was held insufficient, and said concrete injury also keeps courts from deciding constitutional questions unnecessarily and from producing sweeping relief against coordinate branches. It rejected the District Court's reasons for finding standing: the Clause's purpose of preventing potential injury, its precision, the sincerity and good briefing of the parties, and the idea that otherwise no one could sue. Several of those reasons improperly looked to the merits first. On taxpayer standing, Flast requires a challenge to an exercise of the taxing and spending power under Art. I, § 8; respondents instead challenged Executive Branch action in letting Members keep Reserve status.","separate":"Justice Stewart concurred, noting that respondents allege no refusal of an affirmative constitutional duty, that there is no challenge to the taxing and spending power, and that the result fits United States v. SCRAP. Justice Douglas, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented, arguing that respondents have standing as taxpayers and as citizens, because the Clause was meant to protect citizens' interest in keeping the Executive and Legislative Branches separate. Justice Brennan dissented, arguing that good-faith allegations of injury in fact suffice for standing and that the Flast nexus test should not be applied outside its context. Justice Marshall dissented, arguing that respondents alleged a concrete injury to their ability to persuade Congress effectively.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Chief Justice Burger wrote for the Court, joined by Justices Stewart, White, Blackmun, Powell, and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas, Brennan, and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep418/usrep418208/usrep418208.pdf"},"1976/75-708":{"facts":"Petitioners were charged with transporting obscene materials in interstate commerce under 18 U.S.C. § 1465 and with conspiracy. Their conduct ran through February 27, 1973, and trial began that October. In the interim, on June 21, 1973, the Court decided Miller v. California, announcing new standards for obscenity.","procedure":"Petitioners argued that they were entitled to jury instructions under the more favorable Memoirs v. Massachusetts test. The District Court instructed the jury under Miller, petitioners were convicted, and a divided Sixth Circuit affirmed.","question":"May the Miller obscenity standards be applied retroactively, to the potential detriment of defendants, to conduct that occurred before Miller was decided, consistent with the Due Process Clause of the Fifth Amendment?","holding":"The Due Process Clause bars applying Miller to petitioners to the extent that Miller would impose criminal liability for conduct not punishable under Memoirs. Petitioners are entitled to instructions requiring acquittal unless the materials are \"utterly without redeeming social value\", and any principle announced in Miller that benefits them must also be applied.","reasoning":"The Ex Post Facto Clause limits only legislatures, but the principle of fair warning behind it is protected against judicial action by due process, as in Bouie v. City of Columbia. The Court rejected the Court of Appeals' view that Memoirs, a plurality opinion, never became law, reasoning that where no rationale has five Justices, the holding is the position of those concurring on the narrowest grounds; the Memoirs plurality therefore governed. Miller's third part, whether the work lacks serious literary, artistic, political, or scientific value, expanded criminal liability beyond the Memoirs test, which Miller itself called a near-impossible burden for prosecutors. Although the statute was sweeping, it had been confined by Memoirs, so Miller had the same effect as the unforeseeable construction in Bouie. Because the statute regulates expression, the Court took special care to insist on fair warning, and distinguished Hamling, which involved only part (b) of Miller.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, concurred in part and dissented in part: he joined the due process holding but would simply reverse rather than remand for a new trial, because he considers § 1465 overbroad and unconstitutional on its face. Justice Stevens concurred in part and dissented in part, agreeing with the Court's opinion but not the remand, because the prosecution implicates First Amendment values, punishes providing material a person may constitutionally possess, and rests on intolerably vague standards.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Powell wrote for the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Blackmun, and Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep430/usrep430188/usrep430188.pdf"},"1977/76-1836":{"facts":"Respondents bought securities in reliance on a prospectus containing financial statements certified by petitioner, an accounting firm. The issuer later restated its earnings downward and respondents sold at a loss of $2,650. They sued for themselves and a class of similarly situated purchasers under the federal securities laws.","procedure":"The District Court first certified the class and later decertified it. Respondents appealed under 28 U.S.C. § 1291 without seeking certification for interlocutory review under § 1292(b). The Court of Appeals found, under the \"death knell\" doctrine, that respondents would not pursue their individual claims, took jurisdiction, and reversed the decertification.","question":"Is a district court's order denying or revoking class certification a \"final decision\" under 28 U.S.C. § 1291 that is appealable as of right, either under the collateral-order doctrine or under the \"death knell\" doctrine?","holding":"An order refusing or revoking class certification is not appealable under § 1291. The fact that an interlocutory order may induce a party to abandon his claim before final judgment is not a sufficient reason for treating it as a final decision.","reasoning":"An order denying class status does not end the litigation because the plaintiff may pursue his individual claim, so it is appealable only under an exception to the final-judgment rule. The collateral-order exception fails because such an order is subject to revision in the district court under Rule 23(c)(1), is enmeshed in the factual and legal issues of the cause of action, and can be reviewed effectively after final judgment. The death knell doctrine fails as well: class actions have no special appeal rules, so the doctrine would apply to other consequential interlocutory rulings. An appealability rule keyed to the amount of the claim is a legislative function and arbitrary, while case-by-case study of the order's impact would waste judicial resources. The doctrine also circumvents the Interlocutory Appeals Act's screening requirements, favors only plaintiffs, and thrusts appellate courts into the trial process.","disposition":"Reversed, with directions to dismiss the appeal. Justice Stevens delivered the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep437/usrep437463/usrep437463.pdf"},"1971/70-2":{"facts":"Claimant Paladini tried to carry movie films, color slides, photographs, and other printed and graphic material into the United States from Mexico. Customs officers at Los Angeles Airport seized the items as obscene, and the Government began a forfeiture action under 19 U.S.C. § 1305(a), which bars importing obscene matter. Paladini filed an affidavit saying the materials were for personal use and possession only, and the Government conceded it had no evidence to the contrary.","procedure":"The District Court for the Central District of California dismissed the Government's complaint, holding § 1305(a) \"unconstitutional on its face\" on the strength of a three-judge court decision that the Supreme Court had since reversed. The United States appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"May Congress constitutionally prohibit the importation of obscene material that the importer claims is for private, personal use and possession only, despite Stanley v. Georgia?","holding":"Yes. Congress has broad power over foreign commerce, and Stanley's protection of private possession of obscene material in the home does not create a right to import it from abroad. The District Court therefore erred in holding § 1305(a) unconstitutional, and the case was sent back for reconsideration under the obscenity standards announced in Miller v. California.","reasoning":"The Court said import restrictions and border searches rest on different considerations from domestic regulation, because the Commerce Clause gives Congress plenary power over foreign commerce, including power to bar entry of prohibited articles. Obscene material is not protected by the First Amendment, and Stanley rested on the right to privacy in the home, not on a right to acquire or possess obscene matter. The Court declined to extend Stanley's narrow holding, noting it had already held that the right to possess obscene material at home creates no correlative right to sell, give, or transport it, and that a port of entry is not a traveler's home. It said \"The Constitution does not compel, and Congress has not authorized, an exception for private use of obscene material,\" though Congress could create one. It added that private-use imports are hard to control because single copies can be cheaply duplicated. Because no obscenity finding had been made, the Miller standards, which apply to federal legislation, are to be applied on remand.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, arguing that the Constitution gives the Federal Government no power to make a book or film contraband because of its contents, that the history of the First Amendment does not support an obscenity exception, and that obscenity is a matter of taste that the Court cannot define. He also found it ironic that a person may read what he wishes at home under Stanley but may not bring it home from abroad. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, dissented, adhering to his view in Paris Adult Theatre I v. Slaton that the earlier reasoning is no longer viable and finding the statute clearly overbroad and unconstitutional on its face; he would affirm.","disposition":"Vacated and remanded. Chief Justice Burger wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices White, Blackmun, Powell, and Rehnquist (5-4).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413123/usrep413123.pdf"},"1973/73-482":{"facts":"A woman in Pontiac, Michigan, was found raped and beaten. Police followed a dog from the scene to respondent Tucker's house and arrested him. Before questioning, police asked whether he wanted an attorney and told him his statements could be used against him, but did not tell him he would be given free counsel if he could not pay. Tucker said he was with Robert Henderson and then home alone asleep. Police contacted Henderson, whose account undercut Tucker's alibi. The interrogation occurred before Miranda v. Arizona was decided, but the trial came after.","procedure":"Before trial Tucker moved to exclude Henderson's testimony as the fruit of a statement taken without full Miranda warnings. The trial judge excluded Tucker's own statements but admitted Henderson's testimony, and Tucker was convicted of rape; Michigan's appellate courts affirmed. A federal District Court granted habeas corpus on the ground that Henderson's testimony was inadmissible, and the Sixth Circuit affirmed. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Must the testimony of a witness whose identity police learned from a suspect's pre-Miranda custodial statements, given without being told of the right to appointed counsel, be excluded from the suspect's later trial?","holding":"No. The police conduct did not violate Tucker's privilege against compulsory self-incrimination itself but only the prophylactic rules later set out in Miranda, and excluding the third-party witness's testimony was not required, since the police acted in good faith under pre-Miranda standards and the exclusion rationales were weak here.","reasoning":"The Court first held the interrogation did not infringe the privilege itself: Tucker was told he could remain silent and that his words could be used against him, was asked whether he wanted a lawyer, and faced no legal sanctions, so he was not exposed to \"the cruel trilemma of self-accusation, perjury or contempt.\" The failure to give the full Miranda warnings therefore departed only from prophylactic standards, and Wong Sun's fruits rule for actual constitutional violations did not control. Examining the question as one of principle, the Court said the exclusionary rule's deterrent purpose assumes willful or negligent police conduct, and here the officers acted in good faith under Escobedo before Miranda was decided; Tucker's own statements had already been excluded. Exclusion to protect trustworthiness was unneeded because Henderson was a third party under no custodial pressure and was cross-examined at trial. The adversary-system rationale also did not require exclusion. Against this, society's interest in having relevant, trustworthy evidence available weighed strongly, and Harris v. New York supported the same approach.","separate":"Justice Stewart concurred, saying he could also join Justice Brennan's concurrence because the two opinions proceed along nearly parallel lines. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, concurred in the judgment, arguing the Court need not reach the Miranda question and that, under the Linkletter retroactivity criteria, Johnson v. New Jersey should not be read to apply Miranda to fruits of pre-Miranda interrogations. Justice White concurred in the judgment, adhering to his view that Miranda was ill-conceived, and would not extend Miranda's prophylactic scope to bar testimony of third persons identified through inadmissible admissions. Justice Douglas dissented, arguing the interrogation was unconstitutional under Miranda, the witness's testimony was a fruit of it under Silverthorne and Wong Sun, and the date of interrogation or trial cannot justify denying Tucker Miranda's protection; he would affirm.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, and Powell; Justices Brennan and Marshall, and Justice White, concurred in the judgment, and Justice Douglas dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep417/usrep417433/usrep417433.pdf"},"1974/73-1452":{"facts":"Bicycles were taken from two garages in Klamath Falls, Oregon, and respondent Hass was arrested and given full Miranda warnings. He admitted taking two bicycles. On the way in a patrol car to find them, he said he would like to telephone his attorney, but the officer said he could do so when they got to the office. Hass then pointed out where a bicycle was. At trial the court ruled his statements after asking for a lawyer inadmissible in the prosecution's case in chief. Hass then testified to a version of events that contradicted those statements.","procedure":"The prosecution recalled the officer in rebuttal to give the inconsistent statements, and the jury was instructed to consider them only on credibility. Hass was convicted of burglary. The Oregon Court of Appeals reversed, and the Oregon Supreme Court, 4 to 3, affirmed, reasoning that Harris v. New York was distinguishable because police had no deterrence-based reason for restraint once warnings were given. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"When a suspect has been given full Miranda warnings and then, after asking to call a lawyer, is questioned further and gives inculpatory information, may that information be used to impeach his contrary trial testimony?","holding":"Yes. Under Harris v. New York, statements inadmissible in the prosecution's case in chief because of a Miranda violation may be used to impeach the defendant's inconsistent trial testimony, as long as they are trustworthy and not involuntary. The Fifth and Fourteenth Amendments do not bar that use here.","reasoning":"The Court first rejected preliminary arguments: the Oregon decision rested on federal grounds, and the State was an aggrieved party entitled to seek review. On the merits, it saw no valid distinction from Harris: the impeaching statements give the jury valuable aid in judging credibility, and sufficient deterrence flows when the evidence is kept out of the case in chief. It said \"the shield provided by Miranda is not to be perverted to a license to testify inconsistently,\" free of confrontation with prior inconsistent statements. The only factual difference, that Hass's warnings were proper while Harris's were defective, did not matter, since inadmissibility would still convert the right into a right to falsify. The Court found no evidence that Hass's statements were involuntary or coerced, said the balance was struck in Harris, and noted that police abuse could be handled under traditional voluntariness standards.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented, adhering to his Harris dissent that an incriminating statement is as incriminating when used to impeach as in the case in chief. He argued the holding removes police incentive to stop questioning once a suspect asks for a lawyer. Justice Marshall, joined by Justice Brennan, also dissented, agreeing on the merits but arguing the Court should not review state-court decisions upholding constitutional claims where the judgment might rest on independent state grounds, and should at least remand so the Oregon court could consider state law. Justice Douglas took no part.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Powell, and Rehnquist (6-2); Justice Douglas took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep420/usrep420714/usrep420714.pdf"},"1975/74-1393":{"facts":"Two Missouri-licensed physicians sued state officials, challenging a Missouri statute that excluded abortions not \"medically indicated\" from the family planning services eligible for Medicaid funding. Each stated by affidavit that he had provided and expected to provide abortions to Medicaid-eligible patients and that the responsible state official, relying on the statute, had refused all Medicaid applications for such abortions. They argued the statute violated their own rights and their patients' rights, including equal protection.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court dismissed the count for lack of standing, seeing no logical nexus between the plaintiffs' status and the claim. The Eighth Circuit reversed, finding injury in fact and an interest within the relevant zone, and then went on to decide the merits, holding the statute a clear equal protection violation. The Supreme Court granted certiorari limited to standing and whether the Court of Appeals properly reached the merits.","question":"Do physicians who perform nonmedically indicated abortions have standing to challenge the Missouri Medicaid limitation, including by asserting their patients' rights, and did the Court of Appeals properly decide the merits when the case reached it on a dismissal for lack of standing?","holding":"The physicians have standing: they allege concrete injury in fact, and, in the plurality's view, they may assert their patients' abortion rights as a prudential matter. The Court of Appeals should not have decided the merits because the State had never had a chance to defend the statute, so the case is remanded to the District Court.","reasoning":"On Article III standing, the Court said the physicians would be paid if they won and the State would be out of pocket, so the relationship is classically adverse. On third-party rights (Part II-B, for four Justices), the plurality said the general bar on asserting others' rights is not applied where its justifications are absent, and looked to the closeness of the litigant's relationship to the rightholder and the ability of the third party to sue. A woman cannot safely obtain an abortion without a physician, and the physician is \"uniquely qualified to litigate the constitutionality of the State's interference with, or discrimination against, that decision.\" The women face obstacles, including privacy concerns and imminent mootness, so little is lost by allowing the physician to assert their rights. The Court unanimously held the Court of Appeals erred in reaching the merits, because the State filed only a pre-answer motion to dismiss and was never heard on the merits, and an appellate court generally does not decide issues not passed on below.","separate":"Justice Stevens concurred in part, saying the physicians clearly have standing because they have a financial stake and claim impairment of their own rights; he joined only Parts I, II-A, and III, being unsure Part II-B alone would suffice. Justice Powell, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart and Rehnquist, concurred in part and dissented in part. He agreed with the holdings on Article III standing and on the merits question but dissented from allowing the physicians to assert patients' rights, arguing third-party assertion is proper only when the rightholders' own suit is practically impossible or the State directly interdicts the relationship, neither of which was shown, and that the holding would be hard to cabin.","disposition":"Judgment of the Court of Appeals reversed and the case remanded to the District Court so petitioner could answer the complaint. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion of the Court as to Parts I, II-A, and III, joined by all Members; Part II-B was joined by Justices Brennan, White, and Marshall.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep428/usrep428106/usrep428106.pdf"},"1977/76-1767":{"facts":"The National Society of Professional Engineers, an association of about 69,000 members, had a Code of Ethics provision forbidding members to solicit or submit competitive bids for engineering services, defined as price proposals that let a client compare engineers on price before one engineer has been selected for negotiations. If a client required price information, the engineer had to withdraw from consideration. The United States sued under § 1 of the Sherman Act. The Society admitted the essential facts and defended on the ground that competition among engineers would lead to inferior work and endanger public safety.","procedure":"The District Court held the canon unlawful on its face, rejected the Society's justification without making findings on whether competition would produce inferior work, and enjoined the canon. The Court of Appeals affirmed, modifying the injunction in some respects. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to decide whether the District Court should have considered the factual basis for the justification before rejecting it.","question":"Can a professional society's ban on competitive bidding be justified under the Sherman Act's Rule of Reason on the ground that competition would endanger public safety and professional ethics, and does the resulting injunction violate the First Amendment?","holding":"No. The Rule of Reason asks whether a restraint promotes or suppresses competition, so it does not support a defense based on the assumption that competition itself is unreasonable. The injunction, as modified, does not violate the First Amendment because it is a reasonable method of eliminating the consequences of the illegal conduct.","reasoning":"The Court explained that the Rule of Reason focuses on a restraint's impact on competitive conditions and does not let parties argue that a restraint is socially beneficial in general, which is a matter for Congress. The ban is an agreement among competitors to refuse to discuss price until after an engineer is selected; it is not price fixing as such, but it operates as an absolute ban on competitive bidding and is anticompetitive on its face. The Society's defense confirms this, since it assumes the ban will maintain price levels, and its justification based on the threat competition poses to safety and ethics is \"nothing less than a frontal assault on the basic policy of the Sherman Act.\" Public-safety concerns do not create an exception, and Goldfarb's footnote does not create a broad exemption for learned professions. On the First Amendment, the Court said an antitrust remedy may restrict expression, that the standard is whether relief is a reasonable method of eliminating the consequences of the violation, and that the Society may ask the District Court to modify the decree to allow a narrower ethical guideline.","separate":"Justice Blackmun, joined by Justice Rehnquist, concurred in part and in the judgment. He joined Parts I and III but not Part II, because he would not at this point say that any professional ethical rule with an overall anticompetitive effect is forbidden, though he found this rule grossly overbroad. Chief Justice Burger concurred in part and dissented in part: he agreed the Sherman Act was violated but dissented from the part of the judgment barring the Society from stating in its ethics standards that competitive bidding is unethical, which he said the First Amendment protects.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stevens wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Stewart, White, Marshall, and Powell, and in Parts I and III by Justices Blackmun and Rehnquist; Justice Brennan took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep435/usrep435679/usrep435679.pdf"},"1971/70-128":{"facts":"Texas required candidates in party primary elections to pay filing fees. The party committee estimated the total cost of the primary and apportioned it among candidates as it judged “just and equitable”, with local candidates generally paying far more than statewide ones. Fees reached as high as $8,900. Appellees Pate and Wischkaemper were qualified to run for county offices but could not pay assessments of $1,424.60 and $6,300, and Carter was also kept off the ballot. There was no petition alternative and no write-in option in primaries, and voters who wanted to support the excluded candidates intervened.","procedure":"The candidates and voters sued in federal court, and a three-judge District Court declared the Texas filing-fee scheme unconstitutional and enjoined its enforcement. Texas officials appealed directly to the Supreme Court, which noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Does a state filing-fee system for primary candidates, with no alternative means of access to the ballot, deny equal protection under the Fourteenth Amendment to candidates who cannot pay and to the voters who would support them?","holding":"Yes. Because the fees were so large that they had a real and appreciable effect on voters according to their wealth, the system must be closely scrutinized and justified as reasonably necessary to a legitimate state objective, and Texas failed to make that showing. Requiring candidates to pay the costs of primaries through fees, with no reasonable alternative means of ballot access, denies equal protection.","reasoning":"The Court first treated the party primary as state action created by state law. It then asked whether a rational-basis test or stricter review applied, and held that the size of the fees gave the system a “patently exclusionary character” that fell with unequal weight on less affluent voters and candidates, so the law had to be “closely scrutinized,” as in Harper. Texas had a legitimate interest in limiting ballot crowding and screening out frivolous candidates, but fees that exclude candidates who are unable, not merely unwilling, to pay are an ill-fitted means, and other methods are available. Offering a petition route in the general election was not a reasonable alternative, since it required voters and candidates to give up their party affiliation. Shifting the cost of the primaries to the State was not shown to be impractical, because Texas already distinguishes among parties and pays the cost of general elections. The Court stressed that it was not casting doubt on reasonable filing or licensing fees in other settings.","disposition":"Affirmed. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion of the Court, in which all Members joined except Justices Powell and Rehnquist, who took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep405/usrep405134/usrep405134.pdf"},"1973/72-1297":{"facts":"Robison was a draftee classified I-O as a conscientious objector who satisfactorily completed two years of required alternative civilian service at a hospital. He applied for educational benefits under the Veterans' Readjustment Benefits Act of 1966. The Veterans' Administration denied the application because the Act gave benefits only to veterans who served on active duty, defined as full-time duty in the Armed Forces, and alternative civilian service did not qualify.","procedure":"Robison brought a class action in federal district court claiming the Act violated the First Amendment's free exercise guarantee and the equal protection component of the Fifth Amendment. The District Court denied the Government's motion to dismiss under 38 U.S.C. § 211(a), rejected the First Amendment claim, but held the Act unconstitutional on the equal protection claim. The Government appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does 38 U.S.C. § 211(a), barring review of the Administrator's decisions, prevent a court from hearing a constitutional challenge to veterans' benefits legislation? If not, does limiting educational benefits to those who served on active duty, and excluding conscientious objectors who performed civilian service, violate equal protection or the Free Exercise Clause?","holding":"Section 211(a) bars review only of decisions made in administering the benefits statutes, not challenges to the constitutionality of the legislation itself. On the merits, the Act's classification has a rational basis and does not violate equal protection, and the denial of benefits to conscientious objectors who performed civilian service does not violate the Free Exercise Clause.","reasoning":"On jurisdiction, the Court read § 211(a) to cover only decisions of law or fact the Administrator makes in applying the statute, noted that avoiding a serious constitutional question favors that reading, and found nothing in the legislative history showing intent to bar constitutional challenges, which neither burden courts by volume nor involve technical agency policy. On equal protection, the Court applied rational-basis review, finding that conscientious objectors were not a suspect class and that no fundamental right was infringed. It read the Act's purpose as helping those who served on active duty readjust to civilian life, and found that military service differs from alternative service both in degree (a six-year commitment including reserve obligations versus two years) and in kind (a far greater loss of personal freedom and hazardous duty). It also found the classification rationally related to the stated aim of “enhancing and making more attractive service in the Armed Forces.” On free exercise, relying on Gillette v. United States, the Court held that withholding benefits imposes at most an incidental burden, not a choice like the one in Gillette, and that the Government's interest in raising and supporting armies is sufficient.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented. He argued that denying educational benefits to conscientious objectors who performed alternative service places a price on the exercise of religious scruples, as in Sherbert v. Verner, and is an invidious penalty on them. He said the Act gave benefits to many veterans who had no hazardous or disruptive service and that Gillette did not control, because this classification singles out religious objectors and is not neutral.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court, in which Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Marshall, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist joined. Justice Douglas dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep415/usrep415361/usrep415361.pdf"},"1973/73-918":{"facts":"California Department of Corrections Manual § 415.071 provided that press and other media interviews with specific individual inmates would not be permitted. Four inmates and three journalists challenged it after requests by journalists to interview particular inmates at San Quentin were denied. The rule was adopted in 1971 after officials concluded that free face-to-face press interviews had given a few inmates disproportionate notoriety and influence, contributing to disciplinary problems, and after a violent escape attempt. Under existing policy, newsmen could tour prisons, speak with any inmates they encountered, and interview inmates chosen at random by prison officials. Inmates could write letters and receive visits from family, clergy, attorneys and friends of prior acquaintance.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court granted summary judgment to the inmates, holding the rule infringed their First and Fourteenth Amendment freedoms, but dismissed the journalists' claims, finding their rights were not infringed. The officials appealed in No. 73-754 and the journalists in No. 73-918, and the cases were consolidated in the Supreme Court. This brief addresses No. 73-918 together with the companion appeal, which the opinion decides jointly.","question":"Does a state ban on face-to-face press interviews with specifically named inmates violate the inmates' First Amendment free speech rights, or the freedom of the press of journalists seeking to interview them?","holding":"No. Given the alternative channels of communication open to inmates, the ban does not violate the inmates' free speech rights, and because it denies the press no access available to the public generally, it does not abridge freedom of the press. The Constitution does not require the government to give the press special access to information not shared with the public.","reasoning":"The Court held that a prisoner keeps those First Amendment rights not inconsistent with prisoner status or the legitimate aims of the corrections system, which include deterrence, rehabilitation and internal security. It viewed the interview rule in light of alternatives: inmates may correspond by mail, see family, clergy, attorneys and friends, and communicate with the press through those visitors. It said that “So long as reasonable and effective means of communication remain open and no discrimination in terms of content is involved,” prison officials are owed latitude, and that courts should defer to officials' expert judgment absent substantial evidence of exaggerated response. On the press claim, the Court noted the State was not hiding prison conditions and gave journalists more access than the public. Relying on Branzburg v. Hayes, it concluded that “newsmen have no constitutional right of access to prisons or their inmates beyond that afforded the general public,” and that the rule only ended a special privilege the press formerly had.","separate":"Justice Powell, who joined Part I (the inmates' claim) of the Court's opinion, concurred in part and dissented in part: he agreed the inmates have no personal right to demand interviews, but for the reasons in his dissent in Saxbe v. Washington Post Co. would hold the absolute ban on press interviews an impermissible restraint on the press. Justice Douglas, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented, arguing that an absolute ban is overbroad for the inmates' speech and that the press's right protects the public's right to know, so that excluding the press cannot be justified by also excluding the public.","disposition":"The District Court's judgment was reversed as to the inmates' free speech claim and affirmed as to the press claim; the judgment was vacated and the cases remanded for further proceedings. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court, in which Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Blackmun and Rehnquist joined, and in Part I of which Justice Powell joined.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep417/usrep417817/usrep417817.pdf"},"1974/73-296":{"facts":"The sheriff and prosecuting attorney of Allen County, Ohio sued in state court under Ohio's public nuisance statute against the operator of a Lima theater that showed pornographic films. The statute declares a place exhibiting obscene films a nuisance and requires closing it for up to a year and selling the personal property used there. The state trial court found the earlier operator had displayed obscene films and ordered the theater closed for a year and its property seized and sold. Appellee Pursue, Ltd., which had succeeded to the leasehold before judgment, did not appeal within the Ohio courts.","procedure":"Pursue instead sued under 42 U.S.C. § 1983 in federal district court. A three-judge court held the nuisance statute an overbroad prior restraint on First Amendment rights and enjoined execution of the state judgment insofar as it closed the theater to films not previously adjudged obscene, without considering Younger v. Harris. The officials appealed to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does the Younger v. Harris doctrine of federal restraint apply to a state civil nuisance proceeding of this kind, so that a federal court should not intervene before the losing party has exhausted state appellate remedies?","holding":"Yes. In these circumstances Younger applies even though the state proceeding is civil, and Younger standards must be met to justify federal intervention in a state judicial proceeding where the losing litigant has not exhausted his state appellate remedies. The District Court should not have entertained the action unless early intervention was justified under a Younger exception.","reasoning":"The Court explained that Younger rests on comity and federalism, and that interference with state judicial proceedings is, if anything, more intrusive than interference with executive officers, because it prevents the state from using its own courts to resolve constitutional objections and produces duplicative proceedings. It found the Ohio nuisance action, brought by the State and closely related to its criminal obscenity laws, more akin to a criminal prosecution than most civil cases. It rejected the argument that the lack of a civil counterpart to habeas corpus justified a federal forum, saying appellee should not be permitted “the luxury of federal litigation of issues presented by ongoing state proceedings.” It held that this restraint continues after trial court judgment, because federal intervention then would annul a state trial result and displace the state appellate courts, and that appellee's belief that an appeal would fail did not excuse exhaustion. The exceptions for bad-faith or harassing prosecutions and flagrantly unconstitutional statutes remain available, but the District Court had not considered them, and a later Ohio Supreme Court decision narrowed the statute, so the case was remanded.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall, dissented. He argued that Younger rests on a tradition of noninterference with state criminal prosecutions that does not extend to civil proceedings, that the extension undercuts Mitchum v. Foster and the 1948 amendment to the anti-injunction statute, and that requiring exhaustion in a § 1983 action conflicts with Monroe v. Pape and the purpose of the Civil Rights Act of 1871. He would have reached the merits. Justice Douglas filed a separate dissenting statement, joining Justice Brennan but adhering to his Younger dissent that abstention is inconsistent with federalism where important civil rights such as First Amendment rights are about to be sacrificed.","disposition":"Vacated and remanded to the District Court for further proceedings consistent with the opinion. Justice Rehnquist delivered the opinion of the Court, in which Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Blackmun and Powell joined. Justices Brennan, Douglas and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep420/usrep420592/usrep420592.pdf"},"1978/78-309":{"facts":"Touche Ross, an accounting firm, served as independent accountant for Weis Securities, a registered broker-dealer, and audited its books and prepared the annual financial reports that § 17(a) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 and SEC Rule 17a-5 required it to file. Weis became insolvent and was liquidated under the Securities Investor Protection Act with Redington as trustee. Weis' remaining assets and a $14 million advance from the Securities Investor Protection Corporation (SIPC) did not fully cover customer claims. SIPC and the trustee sued Touche Ross for damages, alleging that its improper audit and certification of the 1972 statements kept Weis' true condition hidden until too late, and relied on § 17(a) and common-law theories.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the § 17(a) claim because no private cause of action could be implied from that section. A divided Second Circuit panel reversed, holding that § 17(a) imposes a duty on accountants, that breach supports an implied damages action for customers, and that SIPC and the trustee could sue on the customers' behalf. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does § 17(a) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 give brokerage customers (and SIPC and a trustee suing for them) an implied private cause of action for damages against accountants who audit the reports the section requires?","holding":"No. Whether a statute carries a private right of action is a question of congressional intent, and nothing in § 17(a)'s language, the silent legislative history, or the statutory scheme shows that Congress meant to create a private damages remedy under that section.","reasoning":"The Court began with the statute's language: § 17(a) simply requires broker-dealers to keep records and file reports the SEC prescribes, and it \"confers rights on private parties nor proscribes any conduct as unlawful.\" Its purpose is an early-warning, regulatory one, so it is forward-looking and offers no remedy after insolvency. The legislative history is silent, and silence does not justify implying a remedy where the text weighs against one. Other sections of the 1934 Act, including § 18(a), expressly grant private actions, showing Congress knew how to do so, and § 18(a) limits its remedy to purchasers and sellers, so the Court was reluctant to imply a significantly broader one in § 17(a). The Court said the Cort v. Ash factors are not equally weighted, and once the language and history show no intent to create a remedy, the inquiry ends. Section 27 only confers jurisdiction, and generalized references to the Act's remedial purposes cannot support reading Borak broadly; the Court applies a stricter standard than Borak did.","separate":"Justice Brennan concurred, joining the opinion and adding that where a statute clearly creates no federal right in the plaintiff and the legislative history shows no intent to create a remedy, the remaining Cort factors cannot alone justify implying a right of action. Justice Marshall dissented, applying the four Cort factors: customers are the intended beneficiaries of § 17, § 18 and other express remedies do not show intent to deny a remedy under § 17, a private action would support SEC enforcement and deter careless certification, and the area is not one of traditional state concern. Justice Powell took no part.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Stewart, White, Blackmun, and Stevens; Justice Powell took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep442/usrep442560/usrep442560.pdf"},"1971/70-5112":{"facts":"Henry Stokes died in Louisiana of injuries received on the job. He lived with Willie Mae Weber, to whom he was not married, and with four legitimate minor children by his wife, who was committed to a mental hospital, and one unacknowledged illegitimate child of Stokes and Weber; a second illegitimate child was born after his death. The legitimate children claimed workmen's compensation and also settled a tort suit against a third party for more than the maximum workmen's compensation benefits. Weber claimed compensation for the two illegitimate children.","procedure":"The trial judge treated the legitimate children's entitlement as satisfied and gave the illegitimate children only what remained of the maximum, which was nothing. The Louisiana Court of Appeal and a divided Louisiana Supreme Court upheld the statutory scheme, holding that Levy v. Louisiana was not controlling. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does Louisiana violate the Equal Protection Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment by denying dependent unacknowledged illegitimate children equal recovery rights under its workmen's compensation law with the dependent legitimate children of a deceased worker?","holding":"Yes. Louisiana's denial of equal recovery rights to dependent unacknowledged illegitimate children violates the Equal Protection Clause, because the inferior classification of these children bears no significant relationship to the recognized purposes of workmen's compensation.","reasoning":"The Court held that Levy and Glona controlled, rejecting the state courts' distinction that unacknowledged illegitimates were merely relegated to the position of other dependents rather than excluded; the children were dependent children living in the home and equally dependent on the father. It distinguished Labine v. Vincent as resting on the State's interest in regulating property at death and on the fact that the intestate there could easily have improved his daughter's position, whereas Stokes could not legally have acknowledged his children because he remained married to another woman. Applying equal protection review, the Court asked what legitimate state interest the classification promotes and what fundamental personal rights it might endanger. It found that the State's interest in protecting legitimate family relationships is not served, since people will not shun illicit relations because the offspring may not receive compensation. It also found that the interest in avoiding problems of proof is not significantly disturbed, because dependency is already required for any recovery. The Court added that \"visiting this condemnation on the head of an infant is illogical and unjust\" and that legal burdens should bear some relationship to individual responsibility or wrongdoing.","separate":"Justice Blackmun concurred in the result, giving primary emphasis to Louisiana Civil Code Art. 204, which made it impossible for Stokes to acknowledge his children, and declining to join what he read as the Court's broader holding of full equality even where a father could acknowledge but did not. Justice Rehnquist dissented, arguing that the case was distinguishable from Levy and could be decided the other way under Labine, that he regarded Levy as wrongly decided, and that the Court's test of legitimate state interests and fundamental personal rights has no textual basis and invites judges to impose their own values, whereas Louisiana's distinction was rational given problems of proof and its policy of discouraging illicit family relationships.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Powell delivered the opinion of the Court, in which Chief Justice Burger and Justices Douglas, Brennan, Stewart, White and Marshall joined; Justice Blackmun concurred in the result and Justice Rehnquist dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep406/usrep406164/usrep406164.pdf"},"1972/71-6078":{"facts":"Linda R. S., the mother of an illegitimate child, alleged that Richard D. was the child's father and had refused to support the child. Texas Penal Code Art. 602 makes it a misdemeanor for a parent to willfully refuse to support a child under eighteen, but the Texas courts had consistently construed it to cover only parents of legitimate children. When she asked the local district attorney to enforce Art. 602 against Richard D., he refused because fathers of illegitimate children were outside the statute as construed. She sued for herself, her child and others similarly situated, claiming the construction denied equal protection.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court dismissed the action for want of standing, and the mother appealed. The Supreme Court postponed the question of jurisdiction until argument on the merits.","question":"Does the mother of an illegitimate child have standing to challenge, as a denial of equal protection, a state prosecutor's refusal to enforce a criminal child-support statute against the child's father because the statute is construed to apply only to parents of legitimate children?","holding":"No. In the context of a challenge to a criminal statute, she failed to show a sufficient nexus between her injury and the challenged government action, and \"a private citizen lacks a judicially cognizable interest in the prosecution or nonprosecution of another.\"","reasoning":"The Court first stated that a plaintiff must allege a personal stake in the outcome and some threatened or actual injury resulting from the putatively illegal action, even though standing law has broadened in recent years. It acknowledged that the mother suffered an injury from the father's failure to pay support, but said that an abstract injury is only half of the requirement; she must also show a logical nexus between that injury and the claim to be adjudicated, as in Flast v. Cohen. The Texas statute creates a completed offense with a fixed penalty and does not follow the civil contempt model in which a defendant can end his own jailing by complying. Thus the requested relief would result only in the father's jailing, and the prospect that prosecution would lead to support payments was \"only speculative.\" The Court therefore found the direct relationship between injury and claim absent, and noted that a parent of a legitimate child who had been prosecuted would be a proper party to raise the discrimination. It expressed no view on the merits.","separate":"Justice White, joined by Justice Douglas, dissented. He argued that difficulties with the relief sought go to the remedy, not to standing, and that the mother and children were intentionally excluded from the protection of a criminal law that Texas treats as useful in coercing support, so they have as much interest in challenging that exclusion as a criminal defendant has. He would reverse and remand. Justice Blackmun, joined by Justice Brennan, dissented separately. He viewed the standing question as difficult, thought Gomez v. Perez, decided after argument, had clouded the state precedents and might have altered the controversy, and would have remanded to the District Court to clarify the status of the litigation rather than decide standing.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Marshall delivered the opinion of the Court, in which Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Powell and Rehnquist joined; Justices White and Douglas dissented in one opinion, and Justices Blackmun and Brennan dissented in another.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep410/usrep410614/usrep410614.pdf"},"1972/71-863":{"facts":"The Democratic National Committee (DNC) asked the Federal Communications Commission for a ruling that a broadcaster may not, as a general policy, refuse to sell time to responsible entities to solicit funds and comment on public issues. The Business Executives' Move for Vietnam Peace (BEM) complained that radio station WTOP in Washington refused to sell it time for one-minute spot announcements on the Vietnam conflict, as part of a policy of refusing to sell time to individuals and groups for views on controversial issues. WTOP said it gave full and fair coverage of public questions, including Vietnam, and BEM presented no evidence that the coverage was unfair.","procedure":"The FCC ruled that a broadcaster who meets its obligation to provide full and fair coverage of public issues need not accept editorial advertisements; it did, however, uphold DNC's claim to buy time for fund solicitation, which was not before the Supreme Court. A divided Court of Appeals for the D.C. Circuit reversed, holding that a flat ban on paid public issue announcements violates the First Amendment, at least when other paid announcements are accepted, and remanded to the FCC to develop procedures for administering a right of access. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does a broadcast licensee's general policy of refusing to sell advertising time to individuals or groups wishing to speak on public issues violate the Communications Act's public interest standard or the First Amendment?","holding":"No. Neither the Communications Act nor the First Amendment requires broadcasters to accept paid editorial advertisements. The Chief Justice, joined by Justices Stewart and Rehnquist, further concluded that a licensee's refusal to accept such advertisements is not governmental action for First Amendment purposes.","reasoning":"Chief Justice Burger, for the Court in Parts I, II and IV, stressed that Congress and the FCC deserve great weight in balancing First Amendment interests in broadcasting, and traced Congress's repeated rejection of a common-carrier right of access, 47 U.S.C. § 153(h), and its choice to leave broad journalistic discretion with licensees under the Fairness Doctrine. Part III, joined by Justices Stewart and Rehnquist, concluded that the licensee's policy is not governmental action: the FCC had not fostered the policy but only declined to command a change, Congress had left such journalistic decisions to licensees, and treating those decisions as governmental action would undermine private broadcast journalism. In Part IV the Court held that the FCC was justified in rejecting a right of access: it would favor the affluent, could undermine the Fairness Doctrine, would dilute the public accountability of licensees, and would draw the Government into case-by-case decisions about who should be heard and when. It added that listeners are a captive audience and that \"editing is what editors are for.\" The Court said Congress or the Commission might later devise a limited right of access, and that courts should not freeze this process into a constitutional holding.","separate":"Justice Stewart concurred, joining Parts I, II and III; he argued that treating private broadcasters as the Government would strip them of their own First Amendment rights and push them toward common-carrier status, that the statutory public interest standard and the First Amendment are not coextensive, and that the FCC had not erred in its statutory judgment. Justice White concurred in Parts I, II and IV and the judgment, but would not hold the First Amendment inapplicable for lack of governmental action, and found the statutory and regulatory preservation of broadcaster discretion consistent with the First Amendment. Justice Blackmun, joined by Justice Powell, concurred in Parts I, II and IV, saying that because the First Amendment does not compel the Court of Appeals' result, the governmental action issue does not affect the outcome and he would not decide it. Justice Douglas concurred in the judgment on different grounds: television and radio have the same First Amendment protection as newspapers, and the Fairness Doctrine \"has no place in our First Amendment regime.\" Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented, finding governmental involvement in the broadcasters' policy (public airwaves, licensing, pervasive regulation and FCC approval of the ban) and concluding that an absolute ban on editorial advertising, while commercial advertising is sold, is inconsistent with the public's and individuals' First Amendment interests, with the Fairness Doctrine not an adequate substitute.","disposition":"Reversed. Chief Justice Burger announced the judgment and delivered the opinion of the Court as to Parts I, II and IV (in which Justices White, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist joined, with Justice Stewart not joining Part IV) and an opinion on Part III joined by Justices Stewart and Rehnquist; Justice Douglas concurred in the judgment, and Justices Brennan and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep412/usrep412094/usrep412094.pdf"},"1973/73-206":{"facts":"Howard Levy, a physician and Army captain, was Chief of the Dermatological Service at the Army hospital at Fort Jackson, South Carolina. His duties included conducting a clinic to train Special Forces aide men. After the hospital commander found the training unsatisfactory, he handed Levy a written order to conduct it, and Levy said he would not obey because of his medical ethics. During the same period Levy made public statements to enlisted personnel that he would refuse to go to Vietnam, that Black soldiers should refuse to go and to fight, and that Special Forces personnel were liars, thieves and killers of peasants and murderers of women and children.","procedure":"A general court-martial convicted Levy under Art. 90 of the Uniform Code of Military Justice (disobeying an order) and Arts. 133 and 134 (conduct unbecoming an officer and a gentleman, and disorders prejudicial to good order and discipline), and the conviction was sustained within the military. On federal habeas corpus the District Court denied relief, but the Third Circuit held Arts. 133 and 134 void for vagueness, gave Levy standing to challenge them on their face, and ordered a new trial. The Government appealed to the Supreme Court under 28 U.S.C. § 1252.","question":"Are Articles 133 and 134 of the Uniform Code of Military Justice unconstitutionally vague under the Fifth Amendment's Due Process Clause or overbroad under the First Amendment, so that Levy's conviction cannot stand?","holding":"No. Arts. 133 and 134 are not unconstitutionally vague or facially overbroad. Because of the differences between military and civilian society, vagueness challenges to the Code are judged by the standard for criminal statutes regulating economic affairs, and Levy's own conduct was plainly covered by both articles.","reasoning":"The Court first held that \"any court of the United States\" in § 1252 includes courts of appeals, so the appeal was proper. It then described the military as a specialized society with its own law and traditions, noted that forebears of Arts. 133 and 134 date to British and early American articles of war, and explained that the Code regulates a far broader range of conduct than a civilian criminal code. It reasoned that the Court of Military Appeals and the Manual for Courts-Martial had narrowed the literal breadth of the articles and supplied examples, and that custom and usage supply further content, so the articles are not standardless but apply clearly to some conduct and uncertainly to other conduct. Levy had fair notice that publicly urging enlisted men to refuse orders to Vietnam was covered, and \"One to whose conduct a statute clearly applies may not successfully challenge it for vagueness.\" On overbreadth, the Court said the military's need for obedience and discipline \"may render permissible within the military that which would be constitutionally impermissible outside it,\" so that facial challenges deserve less weight there. There was a wide range of conduct the articles may validly reach, and Levy's own conduct was unprotected. It also held that his Art. 90 defenses were factual determinations resolved against him and not of constitutional significance, and left his other attacks on the sentence for the Court of Appeals to address in the first instance.","separate":"Justice Blackmun, joined by Chief Justice Burger, concurred, emphasizing that concepts of right and wrong have not changed, that the general articles are needed to maintain a disciplined fighting force, and that arbitrary enforcement is checked by the military justice system. Justice Douglas dissented, arguing that the First Amendment has no exception for members of the armed forces, that Levy's statements were an expression of belief on a major public issue, and that they cannot be punished as disorders prejudicial to discipline or as conduct bringing discredit on the armed forces. Justice Stewart, joined by Justices Douglas and Brennan, dissented, arguing that the general articles are vague on their face, that the military's narrowing constructions and the Manual add little specificity, that earlier cases such as Dynes v. Hoover should no longer be followed in the changed historic environment of a conscript army, and that no military necessity justifies such vagueness in criminal statutes.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Rehnquist delivered the opinion of the Court, in which Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Blackmun and Powell joined; Justice Marshall took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep417/usrep417733/usrep417733.pdf"},"1976/76-120":{"facts":"Two commonly owned linen supply companies and their president were charged with criminal contempt for violating a 1969 antitrust consent decree. At the 1975 trial in the Western District of Texas the jury acquitted the president but announced it was hopelessly deadlocked as to the two corporations and was discharged. Six days later the corporations moved for judgments of acquittal under Federal Rule of Criminal Procedure 29(c), which allows such a motion within 7 days after the jury is discharged. The District Judge, who had called the Government's case the weakest contempt case he had seen, granted the motions two months later.","procedure":"The Government appealed under 18 U.S.C. § 3731, which permits Government appeals in criminal cases except where the Double Jeopardy Clause bars further prosecution. The Fifth Circuit dismissed the appeal, reasoning that reversal would allow a second trial barred by double jeopardy. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Double Jeopardy Clause bar the Government from appealing a judgment of acquittal entered by the trial judge under Rule 29(c) after a jury was discharged without reaching a verdict?","holding":"Yes. A Rule 29(c) judgment of acquittal that rests on a finding that the evidence was insufficient is a true acquittal, and the Double Jeopardy Clause bars appellate review and retrial just as it does for acquittals entered under Rule 29(a) or (b). Judgments under Rule 29 are to be treated uniformly.","reasoning":"Section 3731 authorizes Government appeals whenever the Constitution allows, so the question turns on the Double Jeopardy Clause, whose central concern is multiple trials. Here jeopardy had long attached, and reversing the acquittals would require another trial or further factual proceedings, so the usual rule allowing retrial after a mistrial does not apply because the judgments were entered \"on the express authority of, and strictly in compliance with, Rule 29 (c)\". What counts as an acquittal depends on substance, not label, and the judge plainly found the Government's proof legally insufficient. Fong Foo and Sisson show that a judge's acquittal binds the Government just as a jury's does, and the Rule's timing provisions were meant to give judges time to consider such motions, not to create different constitutional consequences. Sanford is distinguishable because it involved a pretrial dismissal after a mistrial, not a judgment of acquittal after trial evidence.","separate":"Justice Stevens concurred in the judgment. He argued that § 3731 never authorized appeals from acquittals, as its text and legislative history show, so the Court's contrary statements in Wilson, Jenkins and Serfass were dicta, and that the ruling here was an acquittal on the merits. Chief Justice Burger dissented, arguing that after the deadlock the defendants were no longer in jeopardy in that proceeding and could be retried, that the case cannot be distinguished from Sanford, that the judge's ruling was one of legal sufficiency rather than a factual acquittal, and that the majority elevates form and timing over substance.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Stewart, White, Marshall, Blackmun and Powell. Justice Rehnquist took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep430/usrep430564/usrep430564.pdf"},"1973/72-1148":{"facts":"Naughten was tried in an Oregon state court for armed robbery. The State relied on the store owner's testimony, a second eyewitness, police testimony that he was found near the scene with the stolen money nearby, and items of his clothing. He neither testified nor called witnesses. The trial judge instructed that he was presumed innocent until guilt was proved beyond a reasonable doubt, and also gave an instruction, based on an Oregon statute, that every witness is presumed to speak the truth and that this presumption may be overcome in several ways.","procedure":"The Oregon Court of Appeals affirmed the conviction and the Oregon Supreme Court denied review. Naughten then sought federal habeas corpus; the District Court denied relief, but the Ninth Circuit reversed, holding that the instruction placed the burden on him to prove his innocence in violation of due process. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did giving the presumption-of-truthfulness instruction in a state criminal trial so offend due process as to make Naughten's conviction constitutionally invalid?","holding":"No. A single instruction must be viewed in the context of the overall charge, and this one neither shifted the burden of proof nor negated the presumption of innocence. The fact that federal appellate courts have disapproved such instructions does not make its use a Fourteenth Amendment violation.","reasoning":"The Court explained that federal courts of appeals disapproving the instruction were exercising supervisory power over their district courts, and that before overturning a state conviction it must be shown the instruction violated a right guaranteed by the Fourteenth Amendment. A single instruction is not judged in artificial isolation, and the question is whether it \"so infected the entire trial\" that the conviction violates due process, not whether a specific cure was given. The judge twice affirmed the presumption of innocence and the State's burden of proof beyond a reasonable doubt. In re Winship was distinguished because there the judge expressly applied a lower standard. The jury was also told it could consider the witness's manner, testimony, and motives, so it remained free to reject untrustworthy testimony, and any undercutting of the reasonable-doubt requirement was only theoretical.","separate":"Justice Brennan dissented, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall. He argued that, together with the earlier instruction on disputable presumptions, the charge required jurors to believe witnesses until their untruthfulness was shown as likely as not, effectively reducing the State's burden to a preponderance in violation of Winship, as in Cool v. United States. He also argued the presumption itself is defective and that the error could not be treated as harmless.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Rehnquist delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Blackmun, and Powell; Justices Brennan, Douglas, and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep414/usrep414141/usrep414141.pdf"},"1974/73-1395":{"facts":"Wilson, a union business manager, was charged with converting union funds to pay for his daughter's wedding reception. The indictment came about 16 months after the FBI finished its investigation, three days before the statute of limitations ran. One of two union officers who signed the payment check had died and the other was terminally ill. Wilson moved before trial to dismiss for preindictment delay, and the motion was denied.","procedure":"The jury convicted Wilson, but the District Court then reversed its earlier ruling and dismissed the indictment because the delay prejudiced his right to a fair trial. The Court of Appeals held that the Double Jeopardy Clause barred the Government's appeal because the ruling rested on facts from the trial and was in effect an acquittal. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Double Jeopardy Clause bar the Government from appealing a trial judge's postverdict ruling in the defendant's favor after the trier of fact has returned a guilty verdict?","holding":"No. The Double Jeopardy Clause protects against Government appeals only where there is danger of subjecting the defendant to a second trial for the same offense. When a judge rules for the defendant after a guilty verdict, the Government may appeal without violating the Clause.","reasoning":"The Court found that Congress, in the 1970 Criminal Appeals Act, intended to remove nonconstitutional barriers and allow appeals whenever the Constitution permits. Looking at the Clause's history and common-law background, it concluded the Clause was aimed at multiple prosecutions rather than Government appeals where no new trial would result. Its cases, including Ball, Kepner, and Fong Foo, turned on avoiding successive trials, and appeals from orders arresting judgment after verdict have long been allowed. The Court read the passage in United States v. Sisson as addressing a hypothetical jury acquittal, not as a constitutional bar to appeal from postverdict rulings. Correcting a legal error after a guilty verdict would simply reinstate the verdict, so it neither gives the prosecutor a second chance nor offends the defendant's interest in the finality of an acquittal. It was therefore unnecessary to decide whether the ruling was an acquittal.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, joined by Justice Brennan. He argued that the District Court's ruling rested on its review of the trial evidence and was a postverdict acquittal like that in Sisson, that Ball and Kepner treat acquittals as unreviewable, and that the judgment below should be affirmed.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the Court of Appeals to consider the merits of the Government's appeal. Justice Marshall delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Blackmun, Powell, and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas and Brennan dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep420/usrep420332/usrep420332.pdf"},"1975/75-491":{"facts":"Respondent was convicted of second-degree murder for stabbing James Sewell, with whom she had registered at a motel, with his own bowie knife. Motel employees found Sewell on top of her struggling for the knife; he had deep stab wounds and she had no cuts or bruises. Her only defense was that Sewell attacked her first. Afterward her counsel learned that Sewell had prior guilty pleas to assault and carrying a deadly weapon, which the prosecutor had not disclosed, and the defense had made no request for it.","procedure":"Counsel moved for a new trial. The District Court denied it, assuming the record was admissible but holding it not sufficiently material because it added little to the undisputed evidence that Sewell carried two knives. The Court of Appeals reversed, holding the evidence material and that the jury might have returned a different verdict had it been received. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the prosecutor's failure to volunteer Sewell's criminal record, absent a request, deprive respondent of a fair trial under the Due Process Clause, and what standard of materiality governs such a duty?","holding":"The prosecutor has a duty to volunteer obviously exculpatory evidence even without a request, but the duty is violated only if the omission is significant enough to deny a fair trial. If the omitted evidence creates a reasonable doubt of guilt that did not otherwise exist, constitutional error has been committed, judged in the context of the entire record. Here there was no such doubt, so the nondisclosure was not error.","reasoning":"The Court distinguished three situations: knowing use of perjured testimony, which calls for a strict materiality test; a specific request, as in Brady; and a general or no request, which it treated alike. It held that the Constitution does not demand disclosure of everything that might influence a jury, and the mere possibility that undisclosed information might have helped the defense does not establish materiality. The duty is not measured by the prosecutor's culpability but by the character of the evidence. Because the evidence was in the prosecutor's hands, the defendant need not meet the strict newly-discovered-evidence standard, but because not every nondisclosure is error, the standard must impose a higher burden than ordinary harmless error. The trial judge applied this standard, recalled the trial in detail, and remained convinced of guilt, noting that the record was largely cumulative of the two knives, and the Court found his appraisal thorough and reasonable.","separate":"Justice Marshall dissented, joined by Justice Brennan. He agreed there is a duty to volunteer exculpatory evidence but argued the Court's standard is at least as severe as the newly-discovered-evidence standard, makes the judge the trier of fact, and gives prosecutors little incentive to disclose. He would ask whether there is a significant chance the evidence, developed by skilled counsel, would have induced a reasonable doubt in enough jurors, and would affirm.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Stevens delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Blackmun, Powell, and Rehnquist; Justices Marshall and Brennan dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep427/usrep427097/usrep427097.pdf"},"1974/74-337":{"facts":"Three corporations (M & L, Salem Inn, and Tim-Rob) operated bars in North Hempstead, N.Y., and offered topless dancing. On July 17, 1973, the town enacted an ordinance making it unlawful for bar owners and others to permit waitresses, barmaids, and entertainers to appear with breasts uncovered or so thinly draped as to appear uncovered. The corporations complied at first, then on August 9, 1973 sued under 42 U.S.C. § 1983 in federal court, claiming the ordinance violated the First and Fourteenth Amendments. The next day, after a temporary restraining order was denied, M & L alone resumed topless dancing and was served with criminal summonses on that and the following three days. Salem and Tim-Rob did not resume until after the District Court granted a preliminary injunction.","procedure":"The District Court preliminarily enjoined enforcement of the ordinance against all three corporations, reasoning that it would be anomalous to deny M & L relief given to the other two, and rejected the argument that Younger v. Harris required dismissal. The Second Circuit affirmed by a divided vote, holding the ordinance would have to fall and that relief was appropriate for all three to avoid contradictory outcomes and conserve judicial energy. The town attorney appealed; the Supreme Court dismissed the appeal and treated the papers as a petition for certiorari, which it granted.","question":"Must each of three co-plaintiffs be treated separately for purposes of Younger v. Harris and Samuels v. Mackell when only one faces a pending state prosecution, and was the preliminary injunction against enforcing the topless-dancing ordinance an abuse of discretion as to the two plaintiffs who faced no prosecution?","holding":"Each plaintiff must be placed in the position it would occupy standing alone; M & L, which was prosecuted just after the federal suit began, is barred by Younger and Samuels from both injunctive and declaratory relief. Salem and Tim-Rob, with no pending prosecution, could seek declaratory relief and also preliminary injunctive relief without Younger's restrictions, and on this record the District Court did not abuse its discretion in granting it to them.","reasoning":"The Court first set aside doubts about its appellate jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. § 1254(2) and took the case by certiorari. It held that interests in avoiding conflicting outcomes and conserving judicial manpower must give way to federalism, and that the three corporations, though sharing counsel and similar problems, were unrelated in ownership, control, and management. M & L could have followed the others' course; because the federal case was in an “embryonic stage” when the summonses issued, its request for an injunction was “squarely governed by Younger”, and Samuels likewise barred declaratory relief. For Salem and Tim-Rob, no state proceeding was pending, so Steffel v. Thompson permitted declaratory relief, and because no declaratory remedy is comparable to a preliminary injunction before final judgment, they could seek interim injunctive relief without Younger's restrictions. Applying the abuse-of-discretion standard, the Court found irreparable injury in their uncontested allegations of substantial business loss and possible bankruptcy, and a likelihood of success because the ordinance reached places beyond those serving liquor (distinguishing California v. LaRue) and was overbroad, a claim they had standing to raise.","separate":"Justice Douglas concurred in the judgment in part and dissented in part. Adhering to his Younger dissent, he joined the judgment as to Salem and Tim-Rob but, like the Court of Appeals, saw no compelling reason to treat M & L differently, and would have affirmed in all respects.","disposition":"Appeal dismissed and certiorari granted. The Second Circuit judgment was reversed as to M & L and affirmed as to Salem and Tim-Rob. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Brennan, Stewart, White, Marshall, Blackmun, and Powell.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep422/usrep422922/usrep422922.pdf"},"1975/74-940":{"facts":"Colorado divided its waters into seven Water Divisions, each with a continuous adjudication system run by Water Referees and Water Judges, with a State Engineer administering distribution. On November 14, 1972 the United States sued some 1,000 water users in federal District Court in Denver, invoking 28 U.S.C. § 1345, to adjudicate reserved water rights for itself and certain Indian tribes, plus state-law rights, in rivers in Division 7, about 300 miles away. The Government was already participating in adjudications in Divisions 4, 5, and 6. Shortly afterward, a defendant in the federal suit had the United States served in the Division 7 state court under the McCarran Amendment so all its claims could be adjudicated there.","procedure":"The District Court granted a motion to dismiss on abstention grounds without deciding jurisdiction. The Tenth Circuit reversed, holding the suit was within § 1345 jurisdiction and that abstention was inappropriate. The Supreme Court granted certiorari and reversed.","question":"Did the McCarran Amendment end federal district-court jurisdiction over water-rights suits brought by the United States, including for Indian reserved rights, and, if not, was dismissal of the federal suit in favor of the concurrent Colorado proceeding nonetheless proper under abstention or other principles?","holding":"The McCarran Amendment did not repeal § 1345 jurisdiction; it gave consent to concurrent state jurisdiction, which includes reserved rights held for Indians. Abstention in its recognized forms did not apply, but dismissal was justified in this case under principles of wise judicial administration, chiefly the Amendment's policy against piecemeal adjudication of water rights in a river system.","reasoning":"The Amendment's text and legislative history show no intent to repeal § 1345, and implied repeals are disfavored; concurrent state and federal jurisdiction are not irreconcilable. Following Eagle County and Water Div. 5, the Amendment reaches Indian reserved rights, given its all-inclusive purpose, its history, and the fact that subjecting those rights to state adjudication does not imperil them or breach the federal trust obligation. Abstention was unavailable because the case fits none of the three categories (a constitutional issue that state law might moot, difficult state-law questions of public import, or interference with state criminal and similar proceedings), and abstention is “an extraordinary and narrow exception”. Separate from abstention, the Court said that when state and federal courts exercise concurrent jurisdiction, dismissal is permitted only in exceptional circumstances, given the “virtually unflagging obligation of the federal courts to exercise the jurisdiction given them.” Here the McCarran Amendment's policy favoring unified adjudication, Colorado's continuous system that predated the federal suit, the absence of any district-court proceedings beyond the complaint, the extensive state-law involvement from 1,000 defendants, the 300-mile distance, and the Government's participation in other Divisions together justified dismissal.","separate":"Justice Stewart, joined by Justices Blackmun and Stevens, dissented: the federal court had jurisdiction, the abstention doctrine did not apply, and the cases on control of property do not require the federal court to yield here because the suit asks only for a determination of rights. He argued that relegating the Government to state court would not avoid piecemeal litigation, and that federal law issues and Indian claims make the federal forum especially appropriate, so he would affirm the Tenth Circuit. Justice Stevens also dissented separately, joining Stewart and adding that the holding is anomalous, may restrict private plaintiffs' access to federal court, and that the Court should defer to the Court of Appeals on weighing the factors; he agreed with Parts I, II, III-A, and III-B.","disposition":"Reversed (the Tenth Circuit judgment) and the District Court's dismissal affirmed. Justice Brennan delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Marshall, Powell, and Rehnquist; Justices Stewart, Blackmun, and Stevens dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep424/usrep424800/usrep424800.pdf"},"1976/75-443":{"facts":"New York Education Law § 6811(8) made it a crime (1) to sell or distribute any contraceptive to a minor under 16, (2) for anyone other than a licensed pharmacist to distribute contraceptives to persons 16 or over, and (3) for anyone, including pharmacists, to advertise or display contraceptives. The appellees included Population Planning Associates (PPA), a North Carolina mail-order seller of nonmedical contraceptives that advertised in New York periodicals and filled orders from New York residents without age limits. State officials had told PPA that its activities violated the statute and had threatened referral to the Attorney General.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court for the Southern District of New York declared § 6811(8) unconstitutional in its entirety under the First and Fourteenth Amendments as applied to nonprescription contraceptives and enjoined its enforcement to that extent. The state officials appealed, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Does the Constitution permit New York to bar distribution of nonprescription contraceptives to anyone other than licensed pharmacists, to bar distribution to minors under 16, and to prohibit all advertising or display of contraceptives?","holding":"All three restrictions are unconstitutional as applied to nonprescription contraceptives. Regulations that burden the decision whether to bear or beget a child by substantially limiting access to contraceptives are justified only by compelling state interests, which the pharmacist-only rule did not serve, and the total ban on advertising or display could not stand under the First Amendment. A majority agreed that the under-16 ban was invalid, though no single rationale commanded a majority.","reasoning":"Justice Brennan's majority opinion (Parts I, II, III, and V) found that PPA had standing, in its own right and for its customers, under Craig v. Boren. It reasoned that the right of privacy in decisions about childbearing covers contraception, and that limiting access to contraceptives burdens that right much as a ban on use would, so regulations of this kind require “compelling state interests” and narrow tailoring. Confining adult sales to pharmacists significantly burdened the right, and the asserted interests in health, quality control, preventing young people from selling contraceptives, and ease of enforcement did not meet that test. As to advertising, the Court relied on Virginia Pharmacy Board: the ban suppressed truthful information about lawful, constitutionally protected products, and the claims that ads would offend or legitimize sexual activity were not valid grounds for suppressing protected expression. In Part IV, a four-Justice plurality reasoned that minors' privacy rights in procreation decisions follow from Planned Parenthood v. Danforth, that the State's deterrence rationale lacked support and had been rejected in related contexts, and that the physician exception did not save the ban.","separate":"Justice White joined Parts I, III, and V and concurred in the result in Part IV, because the State had not shown the ban measurably deters minors' sexual activity. Justice Powell joined Part I and concurred in the judgment, objecting that the plurality applied overly strict scrutiny to regulation of sexual matters and minors, but finding the under-16 ban defective because it infringed privacy interests of married minors aged 14 to 16 and prohibited parents from distributing contraceptives to their children. Justice Stevens joined Parts I, II, III, and V and concurred in the judgment on Part IV for different reasons, calling frivolous the claim that a minor has a constitutional right to use contraceptives over parental and state objection, but finding the prohibition irrational because it increases the risk of disease and unwanted pregnancy. Chief Justice Burger dissented without opinion. Justice Rehnquist dissented, arguing that nothing in the Constitution prevents New York from acting on its judgment to discourage sexual activity by unmarried minors.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Brennan announced the judgment and delivered the opinion of the Court in Parts I, II, III, and V, joined by Justices Stewart, Marshall, Blackmun, and Stevens (with Justice White joining in all but Part II, and Justice Powell joining Part I). Part IV was joined by Justices Stewart, Marshall, and Blackmun. Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep431/usrep431678/usrep431678.pdf"},"1978/77-1439":{"facts":"Oklahoma law made it unlawful to transport or ship out of the State, for sale, natural minnows seined or procured from Oklahoma waters; hatchery-bred minnows were exempt, and licensed dealers could take unlimited numbers of natural minnows and sell them freely inside the State. William Hughes, who held a Texas license to run a commercial minnow business near Wichita Falls, was arrested by an Oklahoma game ranger for carrying a load of natural minnows, bought from an Oklahoma-licensed dealer, from Oklahoma to Texas.","procedure":"Hughes argued that the statute violated the Commerce Clause, but he was convicted and fined. The Oklahoma Court of Criminal Appeals affirmed, relying on Geer v. Connecticut and the idea that a State owns the wild animals within its borders, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on appeal.","question":"Does an Oklahoma statute forbidding the export for sale of natural minnows taken from state waters violate the Commerce Clause, and should Geer v. Connecticut, which upheld a similar ban on exporting game, still control?","holding":"Geer is overruled, and state regulation of wild animals is judged under the same general Commerce Clause rule applied to other natural resources. Oklahoma's statute facially discriminates against interstate commerce and is not the least discriminatory way to serve its conservation interest, so it is repugnant to the Commerce Clause.","reasoning":"The Court traced how Geer's theory (state ownership of wildlife meant no interstate commerce was involved) had been steadily eroded by cases on natural gas, shrimp, and fishing, and by statements that state ownership is a \"legal fiction\". It concluded that time has revealed the error of the early resolution reached in Geer, and that Geer also produced the anomaly that total embargoes were most immune from challenge. Under the Pike v. Bruce Church framework, the Court asks whether the law is evenhanded or discriminatory, whether it serves a legitimate local purpose, and whether nondiscriminatory alternatives would work as well. The statute on its face blocks commerce at the border, which invokes the strictest scrutiny. Conservation is a legitimate local purpose, but Oklahoma chose the most overtly discriminatory means, placing no limit on how many minnows dealers may take or how they are used within the State, so nondiscriminatory alternatives would likely serve conservation better. The Court rejected the State's late bait-use rationale as a post hoc rationalization with no record support, while stressing that States still may protect wildlife in ways consistent with the national economic unit.","separate":"Justice Rehnquist, joined by Chief Justice Burger, dissented. He argued that the Court knocked down a strawman by treating Geer as resting only on the no-interstate-commerce theory, when Geer also rested on the State's police power to preserve its wildlife. He would have said the ownership concept remains a useful shorthand for states' strong interest in conserving natural resources, and that Oklahoma's evenhanded ban on exporting natural minnows imposes little or no burden on interstate commerce on this record, since hatchery minnows are freely exportable and appear fungible.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Stewart, White, Marshall, Blackmun, Powell, and Stevens; Justice Rehnquist dissented, joined by Chief Justice Burger.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep441/usrep441322/usrep441322.pdf"},"1978/77-1645":{"facts":"A shareholder of Mortgage Trust of America sued, as a derivative action for the Trust and as a class action for its shareholders, the Trust, several trustees, the Trust's investment adviser (TAMA), and two affiliated corporations. He alleged frauds and breaches of fiduciary duty in violation of the Investment Advisers Act of 1940: that the advisory contract was unlawful because TAMA and Transamerica were unregistered and compensation was grossly excessive, that inferior securities were bought from an affiliate, and that investment opportunities were misappropriated. He sought an injunction, rescission, restitution of fees, an accounting of profits, and damages.","procedure":"The District Court ruled that the Act confers no private right of action and dismissed the complaint. The Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit reversed, holding that a private right of action for injunctive relief and damages should be implied, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Investment Advisers Act of 1940, which does not expressly provide for private suits, create an implied private cause of action for damages or other relief under section 206 (fraud by advisers) or section 215 (voiding of contracts that violate the Act)?","holding":"Section 215 implies a limited private remedy to void an investment advisers contract, including rescission and restitution. Section 206 does not create a private cause of action for damages, and the Act confers no other private causes of action, legal or equitable.","reasoning":"The Court treated the question as one of statutory construction, asking whether Congress intended to create the private remedy, and began with the statutory language. Because section 215 declares certain contracts void, the Court reasoned that Congress intended that the customary legal incidents of voidness would follow, including a suit for rescission or an injunction against continued operation of the contract, and restitution. Section 206, by contrast, simply proscribes certain conduct and does not in terms create or alter civil liabilities. Congress expressly provided criminal penalties, SEC injunction suits, and administrative sanctions to enforce section 206, and where a statute provides particular remedies a court must be chary of reading others into it. Other securities statutes expressly authorized private damages suits, the Act's jurisdictional section omitted any reference to actions at law or liability, and legislative history was silent, all of which weighed against implying a damages remedy. The fact that section 206 was designed to protect advisers' clients does not by itself require a private damages action, because the dispositive question remains congressional intent.","separate":"Justice Powell filed a short concurring statement joining the Court's opinion as compatible with his dissent in Cannon v. University of Chicago. Justice White, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall, and Stevens, dissented. He argued that the Court confused the existence of a right of action with the available relief, and that each Cort v. Ash factor favors an implied damages action for clients defrauded in violation of section 206, noting that every court of appeals to consider the question had recognized one.","disposition":"Affirmed in part, reversed in part, and remanded. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Blackmun, Powell, and Rehnquist; Justices White, Brennan, Marshall, and Stevens dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep444/usrep444011/usrep444011.pdf"},"1970/5342":{"facts":"Rewis and Williams ran a numbers lottery in Yulee, Florida, a few miles south of the Georgia line. They were Florida residents and there was no evidence they crossed state lines for the operation. Two Georgia residents traveled to Florida to place bets there. All four were convicted under the Travel Act, 18 U.S.C. § 1952, which prohibits interstate travel with intent to promote, manage, establish, carry on, or facilitate certain unlawful activity, including gambling.","procedure":"The District Court instructed the jury that bettors who traveled interstate to gamble violated the Act and that a defendant could be convicted as an aider and abettor under 18 U.S.C. § 2 without personally performing every act. The Fifth Circuit reversed the Georgia bettors' convictions, holding that merely crossing a state line to bet is not a crime under § 1952, but upheld petitioners' convictions on the ground that operators of gambling establishments are responsible for their customers' interstate travel. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does conducting a gambling operation that is frequented by out-of-state bettors, without more, violate the Travel Act, 18 U.S.C. § 1952?","holding":"No. Conducting a gambling operation frequented by out-of-state bettors does not, without more, violate the Travel Act, and the convictions rest on an interpretation the Court rejects.","reasoning":"Justice Marshall agreed with the Court of Appeals that interstate travel by mere customers is not covered, because the statutory language suggests the traveler's purpose \"must involve more than the desire to patronize the illegal activity.\" The legislative history shows the Act targeted organized crime and persons who live in one State while running illegal activity in another, and Congress did not discuss the major effects on federal-state relations and federal resources that a broad reading would have. Any ambiguity in a criminal statute should be resolved in favor of lenity. The Court rejected the Government's alternative theory of reasonable foreseeability of interstate patronage as almost as expansive as the rejected readings. It left open whether active encouragement of interstate patronage could violate the Act, but the jury was not charged on that theory and nothing showed petitioners did more than run the lottery.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Marshall delivered the opinion of the Court, in which all members joined except Justice White, who took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep401/usrep401808/usrep401808.pdf"},"1977/77-262":{"facts":"The Price-Anderson Act limits aggregate liability for a nuclear accident at licensed private nuclear power plants to $560 million, requires those indemnified to waive defenses after a substantial accident, and states that Congress will take whatever action is deemed necessary and appropriate to protect the public if damages exceed the limit. Duke Power, a utility building two nuclear plants in the Carolinas, and the Nuclear Regulatory Commission were sued by an environmental group, a labor union and about 40 nearby residents seeking a declaration that the Act is unconstitutional. The district court found present effects on the plaintiffs such as thermal pollution of lakes and low-level radiation emissions, and a substantial likelihood that the plants would not be built or run but for the Act.","procedure":"The three-judge District Court held that the plaintiffs had standing and that the claims were ripe, then held that the Act violated Fifth Amendment due process (the recovery limit was not rationally related to potential losses and there was no quid pro quo) and equal protection. The defendants appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Do the plaintiffs have standing, and is the case justiciable and within federal jurisdiction, to challenge the Price-Anderson Act, and if so does the Act's liability limit violate the Fifth Amendment's due process or equal protection guarantees or the Just Compensation Clause?","holding":"The district court had jurisdiction under the federal-question statute, the plaintiffs had standing and the claims were ripe, and the Act is constitutional. The liability limitation is rationally related to a legitimate legislative purpose and provides a reasonably just substitute for the remedies it replaces; the equal protection claim also fails.","reasoning":"On jurisdiction the Court read the complaint as stating a substantial claim against the NRC directly under the Fifth Amendment, sufficient for 28 U.S.C. § 1331. On standing, present injuries such as thermal pollution and radiation emissions satisfied injury in fact, and the unchallenged finding of a \"but for\" link between the Act and the plants satisfied causation and redressability; no special nexus between injury and the constitutional claim is required outside taxpayer suits. The claims were ripe because delay would hurt all parties. On the merits the Act is economic legislation presumed constitutional and upheld unless \"demonstrably arbitrary or irrational\"; the $560 million figure, though arbitrary in a sense, was reasonable given the remote risk and Congress's commitment to further relief. The Act does not undermine safety incentives, and it provides \"a reasonably just substitute for the common-law or state tort law remedies it replaces,\" so no more is required. The equal protection argument fails for the same rationality reasons, and the taking claim fails because the Tucker Act remedy remains.","separate":"Justice Stewart concurred in the result but would have found no justiciable controversy, since no accident had occurred, and that the injuries relied on for standing had no direct relationship to the constitutional claim; he would have remanded to dismiss. Justice Rehnquist, joined by Justice Stevens, concurred in the judgment, arguing the district court lacked jurisdiction because the due process claim arises under state tort law and the taking claim could not support declaratory relief, so the complaint should be dismissed. Justice Stevens, also concurring in the judgment, said the chain of contingencies was too speculative for ripeness or standing and the decision was effectively an advisory opinion; he joined Justice Rehnquist's opinion.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for proceedings consistent with the opinion. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, White, Marshall, Blackmun and Powell.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep438/usrep438059/usrep438059.pdf"},"1972/71-738":{"facts":"The Mescalero Apache Tribe operates the Sierra Blanca ski resort on land outside its reservation boundaries, though adjacent to the reservation. The resort was developed under the Indian Reorganization Act of 1934 with a federal loan, on land the Tribe leased from the United States Forest Service for 30 years. New Mexico collected a gross receipts tax on the resort's sales of services and property, which the Tribe paid under protest, and assessed a compensating use tax on materials used to build two ski lifts. The Tribe sought a refund of the sales taxes and contested the use tax.","procedure":"The State Commissioner of Revenue denied the refund and the protest, and the New Mexico Court of Appeals affirmed, holding that the State could apply nondiscriminatory taxes to the enterprise and that the Reorganization Act neither made it a federal instrumentality nor granted immunity from these taxes. The state supreme court denied certiorari, and the Supreme Court granted the Tribe's petition.","question":"Does federal law, whether through the federal-instrumentality doctrine or § 5 of the Indian Reorganization Act, bar New Mexico from imposing a nondiscriminatory gross receipts tax and a use tax on a tribal ski resort operated off the reservation on land leased from the Federal Government?","holding":"The State may impose the nondiscriminatory gross receipts tax, because neither the instrumentality doctrine nor § 5 of the Act bars taxing income from the resort. The use tax on personal property permanently installed as improvements on the exempt land is barred by § 5.","reasoning":"The Court rejected the claim of exclusive federal jurisdiction, noting that tribal activities off the reservation are generally subject to nondiscriminatory state law, and that New Mexico's Enabling Act preserved the State's power to tax property outside Indian reservations unless Congress provides otherwise. It declined to treat the resort as a federal instrumentality automatically immune from taxation, explaining that the broad intergovernmental-immunity doctrine had been consistently rejected in modern cases and that the Reorganization Act aimed to free tribes from bureaucratic control, not make off-reservation enterprises an arm of the Government. As to § 5, the Court read the exemption of \"lands or rights\" as covering land and rights in land, not income from its use, and said tax exemptions are not implied absent clear statutory guidance, a view the legislative history supported. A use tax, however, is in effect a tax on the property itself, and permanent improvements are so intimately connected with the exempt land that the exemption must reach them.","separate":"Justice Douglas, joined by Justices Brennan and Stewart, dissented in part. He would have held the state income tax barred by § 5, reasoning that the ski enterprise is a federal tool created to aid the tribe, that the lease brought it within § 5, and that taxing income from the rights in land is a way of taxing those rights. He also argued that any ambiguity in § 5 should be resolved in favor of the Tribe.","disposition":"Affirmed in part and reversed in part. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Marshall, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist; Justice Douglas, joined by Justices Brennan and Stewart, dissented in part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep411/usrep411145/usrep411145.pdf"},"1974/74-156":{"facts":"In November 1973 police, acting under four warrants, seized four copies of the film Deep Throat from the Pussycat Theatre in Buena Park, Orange County, California. A misdemeanor obscenity complaint was filed in Municipal Court against two theater employees, and the Superior Court, after a hearing that appellees refused to take part in beyond reserving federal questions, declared the film obscene and ordered all copies at the theater seized. Appellees did not appeal that order and instead filed a federal suit against the police officers and prosecutors seeking an injunction against enforcement of the state obscenity statute, return of the films, and a declaration that the statute was unconstitutional. After service of the federal complaint was completed, the state complaint was amended to name appellees as defendants.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court declared the California obscenity statute unconstitutional, ordered the films returned, and rejected the claim that Younger v. Harris required dismissal, finding no pending charges against appellees and in any event bad faith and harassment. After this Court dismissed the appeal in Miller v. California (Miller II) for want of a substantial federal question, the District Court refused to treat that as binding, adhered to its judgment and amended the injunction to require appellants to petition the Municipal Court to return three prints. Appellants appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Did the Court have jurisdiction over the appeal, and did the District Court err in reaching the merits instead of dismissing under Younger v. Harris when state criminal charges were brought against the federal plaintiffs after the federal complaint was filed, and in finding bad faith and harassment?","holding":"The Court had jurisdiction, but the District Court erred in reaching the merits. Where state criminal proceedings are begun against the federal plaintiffs after the federal complaint is filed but before any proceedings of substance on the merits have taken place in the federal court, the principles of Younger v. Harris apply in full force. The finding of bad faith and harassment was unsupported.","reasoning":"On jurisdiction, the Court agreed that a summary dismissal for want of a substantial federal question is a decision on the merits that binds lower courts, so the District Court could not disregard Miller II. But because appellees also challenged the application of the search warrant statutes to the multiple seizures, an issue Miller II did not touch, the three-judge court properly remained in session, and its injunction interfering with the pending prosecution was appealable. On the merits, appellees' interests were intertwined with those of their employees who were being prosecuted, and the Court held that Younger applies where state charges follow the federal filing before substantive federal proceedings. The District Court's bad faith finding was vague and conclusory, because each seizure was authorized by judicial warrant or order and the court made no effort to impeach the officials' reliance on them. Otherwise bad faith would be present whenever a state statute is held unconstitutional, and the exception would swallow Younger.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred, adding that the district judge who initiates a three-judge court must by statute serve on it, and that when that is impossible the record should state why, although no timely objection was made here. Justice Stewart, joined by Justices Douglas, Brennan and Marshall, dissented only from Part III, arguing the holding makes Steffel v. Thompson inoperative, turns on a race to the courthouse, and invites state officials to answer federal complaints with state indictments.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist; Justice Stewart, joined by Justices Douglas, Brennan and Marshall, dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep422/usrep422332/usrep422332.pdf"},"1974/73-1869":{"facts":"The 1954 New Orleans charter provides for a seven-member city council, with five members elected from councilmanic districts and two elected at large. The city is about 55% white and 45% Negro in population, and about 65% white and 35% Negro among registered voters. Under the 1961 districting, only one district had a Negro population majority, only about half of its registered voters were Negro, and no Negro was elected to the council from 1960 to 1970. After the 1970 census the city adopted Plan I, which the Attorney General rejected, and then Plan II, which created Negro population majorities in two districts and a Negro voter majority of 52.6 percent in one. The Attorney General objected to Plan II as well, so the city sued for a declaratory judgment under § 5 of the Voting Rights Act.","procedure":"The District Court for the District of Columbia held that Plan II would have the effect of abridging Negro voting rights, concluding that Negroes would probably be able to elect only one councilman. As a separate ground it held that the plan's failure to change the two at-large seats had that effect in itself, and it refused to let the plan take effect. The city appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Under § 5 of the Voting Rights Act, may a reapportionment plan be rejected because it leaves unchanged at-large seats that have existed since 1954, and what is the standard for deciding whether a reapportionment has the effect of denying or abridging the right to vote on account of race?","holding":"The at-large seats, unchanged since 1954, were not subject to § 5 review, so the plan could not be rejected for failing to eliminate them. A reapportionment that enhances the position of racial minorities in exercising the franchise cannot violate § 5 unless the new apportionment itself so discriminates on the basis of race as to violate the Constitution, and Plan II was not shown to have a prohibited effect.","reasoning":"The Court noted that § 5 by its language applies only to proposed changes in voting procedures, and the at-large seats were created by the 1954 charter and were not altered by the ordinance adopting Plan II. On the effect question, the Court looked to the legislative history, which showed that the purpose of § 5 has always been to insure that no voting-procedure changes would be made that would lead to a retrogression in the position of racial minorities with respect to their effective exercise of the electoral franchise. A change that improves minority position therefore cannot have a discriminatory effect under § 5 unless it is itself unconstitutionally discriminatory. Applying this test, Plan II improved on the 1961 plan, since Negroes would be a population majority in two of the five districts and a clear voter majority in one, so on the District Court's own assumption of bloc voting at least one and perhaps two Negroes would likely be elected. The Court noted that the United States made no claim that the plan was unconstitutional.","separate":"Justice White dissented, arguing that § 5 is not limited to changes that worsen a minority's position, and that given bloc voting and segregated housing the new districts must, to the extent practicable, give the Negro minority an opportunity for roughly proportional representation, which would be at least three of seven seats, so he would have affirmed. Justice Marshall, joined by Justice Brennan, dissented, arguing that the retrogression inquiry has no basis in the statute's language or purposes, that the proper test is the constitutional standard of dilution applied to the plan itself with the burden on the city, and that the District Court's findings of exclusion and the city's weak justifications show Plan II should be rejected. Justice Stevens took no part.","disposition":"Judgment vacated and case remanded for further proceedings consistent with the opinion. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist; Justices White, Marshall and Brennan dissented, and Justice Stevens took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep425/usrep425130/usrep425130.pdf"},"1971/70-5064":{"facts":"The Texas Constitution caps state spending on welfare at a fixed amount, so Texas applies a percentage reduction factor to the standard of need in each federally aided category. The factor was 100% for Old Age Assistance, 95% for Aid to the Blind and to the Permanently and Totally Disabled, and 75% for AFDC. Texas applies the factor first and then subtracts a recipient's outside income from the reduced standard, whereas some States subtract income first and then apply the percentage to unmet need. AFDC recipients brought class actions claiming the computation violated § 402(a)(23) of the Social Security Act and that the lower AFDC percentage denied equal protection, since the share of Black and Mexican-American recipients was higher in AFDC than in the other programs.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court rejected the constitutional claims but accepted the statutory claim. After the Supreme Court decided Rosado v. Wyman and vacated that judgment, the District Court on remand denied all relief, rejecting also a new claim about the computation procedure. Appellants appealed to the Supreme Court.","question":"Does Texas's method of applying a percentage reduction before subtracting outside income violate § 402(a)(23) of the Social Security Act, and does paying a lower percentage of need to AFDC recipients than to other categories violate the Equal Protection Clause?","holding":"No to both. Section 402(a)(23) does not require a computation procedure that maximizes individual eligibility for subsidiary benefits, and Texas's lower AFDC percentage is neither racially discriminatory on this record nor invidious or irrational, so it does not violate equal protection.","reasoning":"On the statute, the Court said Rosado identified two purposes of § 402(a)(23): to make States face the true extent of unmet need and to prod them toward more equitable apportionment of payments. Texas complied by raising its standard of need and adopting a percentage-reduction system. Nothing in the legislative history showed Congress meant to invalidate computation methods that do not maximize eligibility for subsidiary benefits, and the work-incentive and prompt-furnishing provisions did not impose a general federal computation rule. On equal protection, in social welfare a State does not violate the clause merely because its classifications are imperfect, so long as they are rational and not invidious. The three-judge court's finding of no racial prejudice was not clearly erroneous, and a bare statistical difference in racial makeup among grant classes, whose eligibility outlines Congress sets, does not show discrimination. The Court could not say that Texas's decision, perhaps based on the aged and infirm being less able to bear hardship, was irrational.","separate":"Justice Stewart joined Part III of the opinion. Justice Douglas, joined by Justice Brennan, dissented, reading the Act against the background of discrimination and arguing that Texas's formula obscures the standard of need contrary to Rosado, denies cash and noncash benefits to families with income below need, penalizes work, and violates § 402(a)(10) under Townsend v. Swank. Justice Marshall, joined by Justice Brennan, and by Justice Stewart as to Part I only, dissented, arguing that Texas's computation violates several provisions of the AFDC statute and Rosado, and that since Texas set the same standard of need for all programs, the federal statutes require equal treatment of recipients; he would not reach the constitutional claim and criticized the Court's handling of it.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Rehnquist delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Blackmun and Powell; Justice Stewart joined Part III. Justices Douglas, Brennan and Marshall dissented as described.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep406/usrep406535/usrep406535.pdf"},"1972/72-147":{"facts":"In 1970 the Texas Legislative Redistricting Board adopted a reapportionment plan for the 150-member Texas House of Representatives with 79 single-member and 11 multimember districts. The ideal district was 74,645 persons; districts ranged from 5.8% overrepresented to 4.1% underrepresented, a total variation of 9.9%, while the average deviation from the ideal was 1.82%. Plaintiffs challenged the plan for population deviations and argued that the multimember districts in Bexar and Dallas Counties diluted the voting strength of racial and ethnic minorities.","procedure":"A three-judge District Court held the House plan invalid statewide for impermissible population deviations and held the Dallas and Bexar multimember districts unconstitutional, but allowed the plan for the 1972 election except that those two counties had to use single-member districts. Appellants appealed to the Supreme Court under 28 U.S.C. § 1253.","question":"Does a total population variation of 9.9% among Texas House districts, without more, violate equal protection, and were the Dallas and Bexar County multimember districts properly found to invidiously discriminate against Black and Mexican-American voters?","holding":"The population variations alone did not make out a prima facie equal protection violation, so the District Court was reversed on that point. The Court affirmed the invalidation of the Dallas and Bexar multimember districts, because the record showed the groups' members had less opportunity to participate in the political process and elect legislators of their choice.","reasoning":"The Court first held it had jurisdiction over the whole appeal, since the injunction concerning the two counties came from a properly convened three-judge court and the declaratory judgment underlay it. On population, it said state reapportionment is not subject to the strict standards for congressional districts, relying on Mahan v. Howell and Gaffney v. Cummings. Given the 1.82% average deviation and the few districts deviating by more than 3% or 5%, appellees failed to prove a prima facie case from the deviations alone. On multimember districts, the Court said they are not per se unconstitutional, and that to sustain a claim a plaintiff must show that the political processes leading to nomination and election were “not equally open to participation by the group in question”. For Dallas, the District Court relied on a history of official discrimination, the majority-vote and place rules, only two Black members of the delegation since Reconstruction, and a white-dominated slating group that was not responsive to the Black community. For Bexar County, it relied on the history and present condition of the Mexican-American community, low registration, and a legislative delegation insufficiently responsive to its interests. The Court declined to disturb these findings.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall, concurred in part and dissented in part. He joined Part I and agreed with the affirmance on the multimember districts, but dissented from reversing on population deviations, arguing that under Reynolds and Kirkpatrick the State must justify deviations from equality, that the State offered no justification, and that the Court's approach in effect treats deviations under about 10% as needing no justification.","disposition":"Affirmed in part, reversed in part, and remanded. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court; all Members joined Parts I, III and IV, and Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist joined Part II.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep412/usrep412755/usrep412755.pdf"},"1975/75-88":{"facts":"Robert Cuffee lived with Belmira Lucas from 1948 to 1966 without marrying her, and two children, Ruby and Darin, were born to them. After they separated, Cuffee died in 1968 without having acknowledged paternity in writing or having been adjudicated or ordered to support the children. Mrs. Lucas applied for surviving child's insurance benefits for them under the Social Security Act. The Act requires a child to have been dependent on the insured parent at death, which may be shown by living with or being supported by the parent, but it presumes dependency for legitimate children, children who could inherit under state intestacy law, and certain others, including those with a written acknowledgment, paternity decree, or support order. The agency found Cuffee was the father but that the children showed neither dependency nor any presumption.","procedure":"After the agency denied benefits, the children sought review in the District Court for the District of Rhode Island, which affirmed the factual findings but held the statutory classifications constitutionally impermissible under the Fifth Amendment and ordered benefits paid. The Secretary appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Do the Social Security Act provisions that presume dependency for some children but require other illegitimate children to prove actual dependency at the parent's death deny those children equal protection under the Fifth Amendment's Due Process Clause?","holding":"No. Classifications based on legitimacy do not require the most exacting scrutiny, and these classifications are permissible because they are “reasonably related to the likelihood of dependency at death.”","reasoning":"The Court declined to treat legitimacy classifications like race or national origin for scrutiny purposes. It acknowledged that illegitimacy is beyond the child's control, but said that discrimination against illegitimates has not approached the severity of historic discrimination against women and Black people, so the scrutiny is less demanding though not toothless. It accepted the Secretary's explanation that the scheme aims to replace support lost through the parent's death, and found no meaningful legislative history to the contrary. The presumptions were adopted for administrative convenience, and are allowed if the lack of precise fit does not exceed the substantiality tolerated by the level of scrutiny, with the burden on the challengers. Unlike the schemes in Gomez, Weber and Jimenez, this one does not conclusively deny benefits, since any child can qualify by showing support or cohabitation. The Court found it reasonable to presume dependency for legitimate children, children of invalid marriages, and children with a support order, paternity decree, written acknowledgment, or intestate inheritance rights, and said whether Congress's judgments are precise is a matter for Congress.","separate":"Justice Stevens, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented. He saw no relevant difference from Jimenez v. Weinberger, regarded administrative convenience as merely another name for the interest rejected there, and argued that a classification the Court itself called illogical and unjust required a weightier justification. He found the connection between the presumption criteria and actual dependency tenuous, and thought the classification more likely reflected a tradition of disfavoring illegitimate children.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Blackmun delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Powell and Rehnquist; Justices Brennan, Marshall and Stevens dissented in Justice Stevens's opinion.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep427/usrep427495/usrep427495.pdf"},"1976/75-6933":{"facts":"Nathaniel Brown stole a 1965 Chevrolet from a parking lot in East Cleveland, Ohio, on November 29, 1973. Nine days later he was caught driving it in Wickliffe, where he was charged with joyriding (taking or operating a car without the owner's consent) and pleaded guilty, receiving 30 days in jail and a $100 fine. After his release, Cuyahoga County indicted him for auto theft and for joyriding, both alleged to have occurred on or about November 29. He objected on former-jeopardy grounds, pleaded guilty to the theft count while preserving that claim, and received a suspended six-month sentence with probation.","procedure":"The Ohio trial court overruled the double jeopardy objections. The Ohio Court of Appeals affirmed; it agreed that joyriding is a lesser included offense of auto theft and the same statutory offense for double jeopardy purposes, but held the second prosecution permissible because it rested on a separate act on a different date. The Ohio Supreme Court denied leave to appeal and the U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Double Jeopardy Clause of the Fifth Amendment, applied to the States through the Fourteenth, bar prosecution and punishment for auto theft after a prior prosecution and punishment for the lesser included offense of joyriding, where the two charges name different dates within a single nine-day period of possession?","holding":"Yes. The Fifth Amendment forbids successive prosecution and cumulative punishment for a greater and lesser included offense, and a State cannot avoid that bar by dividing one continuous offense into temporal units, so the second prosecution and conviction violated the Double Jeopardy Clause.","reasoning":"The Court began from the Blockburger test: two offenses are distinct only if each provision requires proof of a fact the other does not. Taking Ohio's own definitions from the Court of Appeals, joyriding requires no proof beyond what auto theft requires, so the greater and lesser offenses are the same offense. If two offenses are the same for barring cumulative punishment at one trial, they are the same for barring successive prosecutions, a principle traced to In re Nielsen, and the order of the two prosecutions does not matter. The Court rejected the Ohio court's reliance on different dates. It said that prosecutors cannot avoid the Clause by \"the simple expedient of dividing a single crime into a series of temporal or spatial units,\" because Ohio law, as written and construed here, makes theft and operation of a single car one offense. A footnote noted the result might differ if the legislature had made joyriding a separate offense for each day of operation, which Ohio had not done.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, concurred. He would also reverse because the State did not try all charges arising from one transaction in a single proceeding, and he thought the majority's footnote about daily offenses would not affect that single-transaction rule. Justice Blackmun, joined by the Chief Justice and Justice Rehnquist, dissented. He accepted the general double jeopardy analysis but said the Ohio court found separate acts on November 29 and December 8, that nine days apart could be treated as distinct, and that the Court was circumventing an authoritative Ohio holding on Ohio law.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Powell delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart, White, Marshall, and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep432/usrep432161/usrep432161.pdf"},"1978/77-1337":{"facts":"California residents were severely injured on a California highway in 1968 when their car collided with a car owned by the State of Nevada and driven by a University of Nevada employee on official business. The driver was killed. The residents sued Nevada, the University, and the driver's estate in a California superior court, serving process under a California statute covering nonresident motorists. Nevada argued that a Nevada statute capped tort recovery against the State at $25,000, but a California jury awarded $1,150,000.","procedure":"The trial court quashed service on Nevada, but the California Supreme Court reversed, holding as a matter of California law that Nevada could be sued in California courts. On remand the trial court refused to apply the Nevada cap under the Full Faith and Credit Clause and entered judgment on the verdict, and the California Court of Appeal affirmed. The U.S. Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Federal Constitution, through Article III, the Eleventh Amendment, the Full Faith and Credit Clause, or the structure of the Union, bar a State from being sued without its consent in the courts of another State, or require the forum State to honor the defendant State's statutory cap on damages?","holding":"No. A State is not constitutionally immune from suit in the courts of another State; any such immunity is a matter of comity, and the Full Faith and Credit Clause did not require California to apply Nevada's $25,000 limit in violation of California's own policy.","reasoning":"The Court separated sovereign immunity into two ideas. The rule that \"no sovereign may be sued in its own courts without its consent\" rests on the sovereign's authority over its own courts, but it gives no support to immunity in another sovereign's courts, which depends on agreement or on comity, as in The Schooner Exchange. The Framers did not discuss protection against suit in a sister State's courts, and Article III and the Eleventh Amendment concern federal-court jurisdiction, so they furnish no basis to limit California. On full faith and credit, Pacific Insurance Co. v. Industrial Accident Comm'n establishes that a State need not apply another State's law in violation of its own legitimate public policy, and California had a strong policy of fully compensating those injured on its highways. Constitutional provisions limiting state sovereignty, such as the Commerce Clause, extradition, and the Privileges and Immunities Clause, do not imply a general constitutional immunity, and the Tenth Amendment counsels caution about inferring unstated limits. Footnote 24 reserved whether different state policies might require a different result.","separate":"Justice Blackmun, joined by the Chief Justice and Justice Rehnquist, dissented. He argued that the Court's logic lets any State be sued in another State's courts like any litigant, risking retaliation and strain on cooperative federalism, and that sovereign immunity is an implied component of federalism, like the right to travel, supported by the Eleventh Amendment and by Paulus v. South Dakota. Justice Rehnquist, joined by the Chief Justice, also dissented. He argued that the constitutional plan, the Eleventh Amendment, and prior decisions such as Hans v. Louisiana and Monaco v. Mississippi presuppose that unconsenting States cannot be sued in sister-State courts, and he warned of Balkanization and enforcement problems.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stevens delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart, White, Marshall, and Powell.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep440/usrep440410/usrep440410.pdf"},"1979/78-738":{"facts":"Kuapa Pond on Oahu, Hawaii, was a 523-acre shallow lagoon separated from Maunalua Bay and the Pacific Ocean by a barrier beach. Under Hawaii law it was private property, long used as a fishpond. In 1961 the Bishop Estate leased the area to Kaiser Aetna, which dredged and filled it, built bridges and retaining walls, and created the Hawaii Kai Marina. The Army Corps of Engineers told Kaiser Aetna no permits were required, and later acquiesced in a dredged channel connecting the pond to the bay. Kaiser Aetna controlled access to the marina, and lot lessees paid fees for its maintenance.","procedure":"In 1972 the United States sued after a dispute over Corps permit authority and public access. The District Court held the pond was navigable water subject to Corps regulation under the Rivers and Harbors Appropriation Act, but that the Government could not open it to the public without compensation. The Ninth Circuit agreed on regulatory authority but reversed on access, holding that the improved pond became subject to the federal navigational servitude and a public right of access. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"When private owners dredge a private pond and connect it to navigable water, does the federal navigational servitude give the public a right of access to the pond without the Government invoking eminent domain and paying just compensation under the Fifth Amendment?","holding":"No. Although the improved pond is within Congress's Commerce Clause regulatory authority, the navigational servitude is not a blanket exception to the Takings Clause, and imposing a public right of access on the privately owned marina would be a taking requiring just compensation.","reasoning":"The Court held that the concept of navigable waters has been used for different purposes, so a pond that is navigable for delimiting Congress's regulatory power is not necessarily subject to the servitude. Congress's Commerce Clause power over waters is broad and could support a requirement of free public access, but whether such a requirement is a taking is a separate question, answered through the ad hoc factors of Penn Central, including interference with investment-backed expectations. The servitude cases involved the Government's condemnation of fast lands riparian to naturally navigable streams, which is quite different from a private pond connected to navigable water with the Government's consent. Here \"the Government's attempt to create a public right of access to the improved pond goes so far beyond ordinary regulation or improvement for navigation as to amount to a taking.\" The pond had never been a continuous highway for interstate commerce, was private under Hawaii law, and had been improved at great private expense with the Corps's acquiescence. The right to exclude is a fundamental element of property, and forced public access would be a physical invasion.","separate":"Justice Blackmun, joined by Justices Brennan and Marshall, dissented. He would find the pond navigable water of the United States under the ebb-and-flow test even before development, and would hold that the navigational servitude extends to all such waters, however created. He would also balance the interests and conclude that the developers acted at their own risk and are not entitled to compensation for access to a navigable highway, and he said state property law cannot defeat the federal servitude.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Rehnquist delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Powell, and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep444/usrep444164/usrep444164.pdf"},"1974/73-1596":{"facts":"Five lawfully admitted Chinese resident aliens living in San Francisco were each denied federal employment solely because they were not citizens. One was fired from the Post Office Department after ten days when his record showed he was not a citizen, others lost or were refused jobs with the General Services Administration and were barred from a civil service examination for a position at HEW, and a fifth could not take a typing test. A Civil Service Commission regulation admitted to competitive examination and appointment only citizens or persons owing permanent allegiance to the United States, in practice natives of American Samoa. The five sued as a class, alleging the rule was arbitrary and violated the Fifth Amendment's Due Process Clause and an Executive Order barring discrimination on the basis of national origin.","procedure":"The District Court ruled for the defendants, holding the federal power over aliens was almost plenary and the rule needed only a rational basis. The Court of Appeals reversed, holding the regulation violated Fifth Amendment due process because it swept indiscriminately across all competitive positions. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on the Commission's petition alone; the Postal Service had meanwhile dropped its own citizenship requirement.","question":"Does a Civil Service Commission regulation that bars lawfully admitted resident aliens from employment in the federal competitive civil service deprive them of liberty without due process of law under the Fifth Amendment?","holding":"Yes. The Court held the Commission's rule invalid. When the Federal Government asserts an overriding national interest to justify a discriminatory rule that would violate equal protection if adopted by a State, due process requires a legitimate basis for presuming the rule was actually intended to serve that interest, and the Commission, which is concerned only with the efficiency of the civil service, could not rely on interests that are not its concern.","reasoning":"The Court rejected the argument that federal power over aliens is so plenary that any federal agent may subject resident aliens to different rules, because the rule deprives an identifiable class of an interest in liberty on a wholesale basis. It agreed that overriding national interests can justify federal rules a State could not adopt, and reasoned that the question is who made the decision and for what reasons. It stated that \"due process requires that there be a legitimate basis for presuming that the rule was actually intended to serve that interest.\" Reviewing the history of the Pendleton Act, appropriation riders, and Executive Orders, the Court found that neither Congress nor the President had required the citizenship rule; the Commission adopted it and had discretion to keep or drop it. The interests the government offered (treaty bargaining, encouraging naturalization) are not matters within the Commission's responsibility. Administrative convenience, the one interest within its concern, was unacceptable because the record showed no considered evaluation, classifying positions would not be especially burdensome, and the deprivation was too serious. The Court assumed without deciding that Congress or the President could have imposed the rule.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, filed a concurring statement joining the opinion on the understanding that the equal protection questions raised by a congressional or Presidential bar on alien employment are reserved. Justice Rehnquist, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices White and Blackmun, dissented, arguing that the Court invented a novel due process doctrine that mixes delegation theory with equal protection, that neither aliens nor citizens have a protected liberty interest in obtaining federal employment, and that policy toward aliens is a political matter committed to Congress, which validly delegated its power to the Commission.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stevens delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart, Marshall, and Powell (five Justices); four Justices dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep426/usrep426088/usrep426088.pdf"},"1975/74-1520":{"facts":"In December 1970 Richard Elrod, a Democrat, replaced a Republican as Sheriff of Cook County, Illinois. The respondents were Republican employees of the Sheriff's Office who held non-civil-service jobs with no statutory protection against arbitrary discharge. It was the practice of an incoming Sheriff of a different party to replace such employees with members of his own party unless they joined or obtained sponsorship from that party. Three respondents were discharged, and a fourth was threatened with discharge, solely because they did not support or belong to the Democratic Party and had no sponsorship from a Democratic leader.","procedure":"The respondents sued under the First and Fourteenth Amendments and federal civil rights statutes. The District Court denied a preliminary injunction for lack of irreparable injury and dismissed the complaint for failure to state a claim. The Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit reversed, held the complaint stated a cognizable claim, and directed entry of preliminary injunctive relief.","question":"Do public employees who allege they were discharged or threatened with discharge solely because of their partisan political affiliation or nonaffiliation state a claim under the First and Fourteenth Amendments?","holding":"Yes. A plurality of three Justices held that patronage dismissals of public employees violate the First and Fourteenth Amendments, so the respondents stated a valid claim, and that the preliminary injunction was properly directed. Two concurring Justices agreed on a narrower rule: a nonpolicymaking, nonconfidential employee may not be discharged from a job he is satisfactorily performing solely because of his political beliefs.","reasoning":"The plurality first dismissed the political-question and separation-of-powers objections as inapplicable to the federal judiciary's review of state action. It reasoned that patronage dismissals force employees to pledge allegiance, work for, or contribute to the favored party, or lose their jobs, which burdens belief and association at the core of the First Amendment; under Keyishian and Perry, government may not use the denial of a public benefit to obtain what it could not command directly. Such a burden must survive exacting scrutiny: the government must show a vital interest and use means that are least restrictive of protected freedoms. The plurality found the efficiency argument inadequate because employees can be discharged for cause and merit systems exist. It found the interest in political loyalty satisfied by \"Limiting patronage dismissals to policymaking positions is sufficient to achieve this governmental end.\" It also rejected the claim that patronage is needed to sustain the democratic process, since parties survive without it and patronage impedes associational freedom. Finally, because First Amendment interests were threatened or impaired, \"The loss of First Amendment freedoms, for even minimal periods of time, unquestionably constitutes irreparable injury.\"","separate":"Justice Stewart, joined by Justice Blackmun, concurred in the judgment, declining to join the plurality's broad opinion and saying the only question is whether a \"nonpolicymaking, nonconfidential government employee can be discharged or threatened with discharge from a job that he is satisfactorily performing upon the sole ground of his political beliefs,\" which he agreed cannot be done. Chief Justice Burger dissented, joining Justice Powell and adding that the Court intrudes on legislative and state policy choices, here Illinois's decision to leave about half the Sheriff's staff to the elected head. Justice Powell, joined by the Chief Justice and Justice Rehnquist, dissented, calling patronage \"a practice as old as the Republic\" that serves important state interests in stimulating political activity and strengthening parties, and arguing that the intrusion on First Amendment interests is modest, that the respondents may have knowingly benefited from the system, and that local legislatures are better placed to weigh the matter.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Brennan announced the judgment and wrote a plurality opinion joined by Justices White and Marshall; Justices Stewart and Blackmun concurred in the judgment; Chief Justice Burger and Justices Powell and Rehnquist dissented. Justice Stevens took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep427/usrep427347/usrep427347.pdf"},"1975/74-775":{"facts":"A New Orleans ordinance, as amended in 1972, prohibited pushcart food vending in the Vieux Carre (French Quarter) but exempted vendors who had continuously operated the same business there for eight or more years before January 1, 1972. The Quarter is the heart of the city's tourist industry. The appellee had pushcart-vended in the Quarter for only two years and was barred from continuing. Only two vendors, a hot dog seller and an ice cream seller who had each operated there for over 20 years, qualified for the exemption.","procedure":"The appellee sued in federal District Court claiming the grandfather clause denied her equal protection, and the District Court granted summary judgment for the city. The Fifth Circuit reversed, relying chiefly on Morey v. Doud, and remanded to decide whether the clause was severable. The Supreme Court postponed the jurisdictional question to the merits.","question":"Does the ordinance's eight-year grandfather clause, which lets long-established pushcart vendors continue operating in the Vieux Carre while barring newer vendors, deny the appellee equal protection of the laws, and does the Supreme Court have jurisdiction over the appeal?","holding":"The Court has jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. 1254(2), and the grandfather clause does not violate the Equal Protection Clause. In local economic regulation, a classification that neither burdens fundamental rights nor rests on suspect distinctions need only be rationally related to a legitimate state interest, and Morey v. Doud was overruled.","reasoning":"On jurisdiction, the Court held that any finality requirement was met because the Court of Appeals had definitively decided the ordinance's unconstitutionality as applied, leaving only a state-law severability question. On the merits, the Court described the ordinance as \"solely an economic regulation aimed at enhancing the vital role of the French Quarter's tourist-oriented charm in the economy of New Orleans.\" It reasoned that courts defer to legislative judgments in such areas, that legislatures may proceed step by step, and that only invidious discrimination or wholly arbitrary acts violate equal protection. The city could rationally pursue its purpose by first eliminating newer vendors, who were less likely to have built up reliance interests, while the two long-established vendors had themselves become part of the Quarter's distinctive character. The Court held that Morey v. Doud was a needlessly intrusive judicial infringement on legislative power, was the only case in half a century to strike down purely economic regulation on equal protection grounds alone, and was overruled.","separate":"Justice Marshall concurred in the judgment without a written opinion. Justice Stevens took no part in the consideration or decision of the case.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings consistent with the opinion. Per curiam opinion; the text states no vote count, only that Justice Marshall concurred in the judgment and Justice Stevens took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep427/usrep427297/usrep427297.pdf"},"1977/76-446":{"facts":"Navarette, an inmate at Soledad Prison in California, sued six prison officials, three subordinates and three supervisors, under 42 U.S.C. 1983 and 1985. Three of his nine claims concerned his outgoing mail between September 1971 and December 1972. The third claim alleged that the subordinate officers had negligently and inadvertently misapplied the prison mail regulations, so that various letters were never mailed, and that the supervisors negligently failed to train and direct them, all in violation of his constitutional rights.","procedure":"The District Court granted summary judgment for the officials on the first three claims. The Ninth Circuit reversed, holding that prisoners have First and Fourteenth Amendment protection for outgoing mail, that negligent deprivation states a 1983 claim, and that summary judgment on the third claim was improper. The Supreme Court granted certiorari limited to whether negligent failure to mail a prisoner's letters states a 1983 cause of action, but treated the officials' immunity as a subsidiary issue fairly comprised in that question.","question":"Were the prison officials entitled to summary judgment on the claim of negligent interference with a prisoner's outgoing mail, on the basis of qualified immunity from damages under 1983?","holding":"Yes. The officials, who had only qualified immunity, were entitled to judgment as a matter of law because there was no clearly established constitutional right protecting a state prisoner's mail in 1971-1972, and the negligence claim does not allege the malicious intent that would also defeat immunity.","reasoning":"The Court agreed that prison officials are not absolutely immune but may rely on the qualified immunity of Scheuer v. Rhodes and Wood v. Strickland. Under Wood, immunity is lost if the official \"knew or reasonably should have known that the action he took within his sphere of official responsibility would violate the constitutional rights of the student affected,\" or acted with malicious intention to cause a deprivation or other injury. As to the first branch, the Court reviewed the law of the time. Procunier v. Martinez left open the prisoner's own rights, lower court decisions were inconsistent, and the cases the respondent cited did not deal with convicted prisoners' mail. It concluded there was no \"clearly established\" First and Fourteenth Amendment right with respect to the correspondence of convicted prisoners in 1971-1972, so officials could not have known their conduct was unconstitutional. The access-to-courts theory was foreclosed by an earlier dismissal. As to the second branch, malicious intent contemplates intended consequences, whereas the third claim charged only negligence.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger dissented because the Court decided a question not comprised in the question on which certiorari was granted; he would have held that one who neither intends nor is deliberately indifferent to causing harm is not liable under 1983 and remanded to construe the ambiguous complaint. Justice Stevens dissented, arguing that the Court reached an affirmative defense prematurely on a sketchy record, that the good-faith defense turns on how the official did his job and was not established given the conflicting affidavits, and that officials might be charged with knowing of the well-established right of access to the courts.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice White delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart, Marshall, Blackmun, Powell, and Rehnquist; Chief Justice Burger and Justice Stevens dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep434/usrep434555/usrep434555.pdf"},"1977/76-1382":{"facts":"Scott, a Muskegon, Michigan police officer, was indicted on three counts of distributing narcotics. Before trial and twice during trial he moved to dismiss the two counts concerning transactions in the preceding September, claiming that preindictment delay had prejudiced his defense. At the close of all the evidence the District Court granted the motion, finding sufficient proof of prejudice on count one, and the jury acquitted him on the third count.","procedure":"The Government sought to appeal the dismissals under 18 U.S.C. 3731, which allows appeals unless the Double Jeopardy Clause bars further prosecution. The Sixth Circuit, relying on United States v. Jenkins, held further prosecution barred and dismissed the appeal. The Supreme Court granted certiorari, limited to the dismissal of count one.","question":"Does the Double Jeopardy Clause bar a Government appeal from a trial court's midtrial dismissal of an indictment, granted on the defendant's motion on a ground unrelated to factual guilt or innocence, when reversal would require further trial proceedings?","holding":"No. Where a defendant himself seeks to have the trial terminated without any submission to judge or jury of his guilt or innocence, a Government appeal from his successful effort to do so does not offend the Double Jeopardy Clause and is not barred by 18 U.S.C. 3731. United States v. Jenkins was overruled.","reasoning":"The Court reviewed the history of the Clause, noting that a successful defense appeal of a conviction (other than for insufficient evidence) permits retrial while an acquittal is final even if mistaken. It explained that when a defendant obtains a mistrial, retrial is permitted because the motion is a deliberate election to forgo his right to the first trier of fact, and it concluded that Lee v. United States showed a midtrial dismissal can be treated like a mistrial. Jenkins had stretched the principle against repeated Government attempts at conviction to cases where the defendant is responsible for the second prosecution. A defendant is acquitted only when the ruling actually resolves some or all of the factual elements of the offense, as with insanity or entrapment defenses, whereas dismissal for preindictment delay is a legal judgment that a culpable defendant may not be punished. The Court reasoned that the Clause \"does not relieve a defendant from the consequences of his voluntary choice,\" and that only the public was deprived of its opportunity to convict. It found that the lessons of experience justified overruling Jenkins.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justices White, Marshall, and Stevens, dissented. He argued that Jenkins and Lee rightly barred retrial after any midtrial final judgment favorable to the accused, because the rule against retrying after acquittal rests on the policy against multiple trials, not on factual innocence. He also contended that the Court's narrow definition of acquittal is unprincipled, since preindictment delay, like insanity and entrapment, is decided on the trial evidence, and that the decision will create confusion for lower courts.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Rehnquist delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, and Powell (five Justices); four Justices dissented.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep437/usrep437082/usrep437082.pdf"},"1978/77-1511":{"facts":"The Secretary of Health, Education, and Welfare determined that respondents, Social Security beneficiaries, had been overpaid and sought to recoup the overpayments by withholding their future benefits under section 204 of the Social Security Act. Under the Secretary's practice, an ex parte overpayment determination is made and the recipient is notified. The recipient may then file a written request for reconsideration (section 204(a)) or for waiver of recovery (section 204(b), for a person without fault where recovery would defeat the Act's purpose or be against equity and good conscience). If written review goes against the recipient, recoupment begins, and an oral hearing is available only if the recipient keeps objecting. Respondents in Hawaii and Washington, who had failed to obtain administrative relief, sued, claiming the procedures lacked adequate notice and a prerecoupment oral hearing.","procedure":"Both District Courts certified classes (the Washington class was nationwide), held the recoupment procedures unconstitutional, and granted injunctive relief. The Ninth Circuit consolidated the cases and, after remand in light of Mathews v. Eldridge, held that due process required a prerecoupment oral hearing when waiver was requested but not in routine section 204(a) reconsideration cases. The Supreme Court granted the Secretary's petition for certiorari.","question":"Must the Secretary give recipients an oral hearing before recoupment begins, either on a request for waiver under section 204(b) or on a request for reconsideration under section 204(a)? And does section 205(g) of the Act permit a federal district court to certify a (nationwide) class and grant injunctive relief?","holding":"Recipients who file a written request for waiver under section 204(b) are entitled to the opportunity for a prerecoupment oral hearing, but those who merely seek reconsideration under section 204(a) are not. Section 205(g) does not bar class certification (including a nationwide class, within the district court's discretion) or injunctive relief, though the classes were broader than section 205(g)'s final-decision requirement allows.","reasoning":"The Court first took up the statutory question before the constitutional one and read section 204 to require a prerecoupment decision, because both recovery and waiver are phrased in mandatory terms. Reconsideration requests under 204(a) usually involve computation, so written review suffices, and the Due Process Clause looks to the generality of cases rather than rare credibility disputes. Waiver under 204(b) turns on fault and equity, which depend on credibility, and the Court said \"written submissions are a particularly inappropriate way to distinguish a genuine hard luck story from a fabricated tall tale.\" The fact that about 30% of the conferences held under the injunctions reversed the Secretary's decision supported this reading. On jurisdiction, nothing in section 205(g) shows a clear intent to displace Rule 23, and Rule 23 does not limit a class's geographic scope, so certification was within the district court's discretion. The classes included people who had filed no request and so lacked a final decision, but the relief actually granted reached only those who claimed waiver. Finally, \"Absent the clearest command to the contrary from Congress, federal courts retain their equitable power to issue injunctions in suits over which they have jurisdiction.\"","disposition":"Affirmed in part and reversed in part. Justice Blackmun delivered the opinion of the Court, in which all other Members joined except Justice Powell, who took no part in the consideration or decision.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep442/usrep442682/usrep442682.pdf"},"1969/606":{"facts":"Allen was tried by an Illinois jury for armed robbery. He rejected appointed counsel and tried to act as his own lawyer, with appointed counsel sitting in. During jury selection he argued abusively with the judge, threatened him, and tore up his lawyer's file. The judge warned him, \"One more outbreak of that sort and I'll remove you\" from the courtroom, but Allen kept up the abuse and said there would be no trial. He was removed, was told he could return if he behaved, and was removed again when he again threatened to disrupt the trial. He stayed out for most of the State's case, apart from brief appearances for identification, and returned for his defense after giving some assurances of proper conduct.","procedure":"Allen was convicted and sentenced to 10 to 30 years; the Illinois Supreme Court affirmed and this Court denied certiorari. The federal District Court denied his habeas corpus petition, but the Court of Appeals (Judge Hastings dissenting) reversed, holding that his right to be present was absolute and that the judge should have restrained him, even by shackling and gagging. This Court granted review.","question":"Can a defendant who persists in disorderly and disruptive conduct, after a warning, lose the Sixth and Fourteenth Amendment right to be present at his own trial, and what may a trial judge constitutionally do with such a defendant?","holding":"Yes. A defendant can lose his right to be present if, after being warned that he will be removed, he insists on behaving so disruptively that the trial cannot proceed with him in the courtroom; he may reclaim the right once he is willing to behave with decorum. A judge may bind and gag him, cite him for contempt, or remove him until he promises to behave, and removal was within the judge's discretion here.","reasoning":"The Court rejected the Court of Appeals' view that the right to be present is absolute, noting that earlier broad statements to that effect had been rejected and accepting Justice Cardozo's statement in Snyder that the privilege of confrontation may be lost by consent or misconduct. It stressed that dignity, order and decorum are essential to criminal justice and that judges need discretion because no single formula fits all situations. Binding and gagging may prejudice the jury, offends courtroom dignity and impairs the defendant's ability to consult counsel, so it is a last resort. Criminal contempt may be inadequate against a determined defendant or one facing a very severe sentence, and civil contempt with a continuance risks letting a defendant profit from his own wrong. On this record Allen was repeatedly warned, told he could return on good behavior, and would not have been deterred by contempt, so the judge acted within his discretion.","separate":"Justice Brennan concurred, joining the Court's opinion: a defendant who is fully warned and persists may surrender the right to be present, and shackling and gagging is the least acceptable option. He added that the court should make reasonable efforts to let an excluded defendant communicate with his attorney and keep informed of the trial. Justice Douglas wrote separately, agreeing that a trial cannot go forward in a bedlam but objecting to using this case to set guidelines: the record was stale (the trial was nearly 13 years earlier), it suggested the defendant might be mentally ill, and political trials and trials used by minorities to destroy the system raise different problems. He would not reach the merits and would reverse for staleness of the record and affirm the District Court's denial of relief.","disposition":"Reversed (Court of Appeals judgment reversed). Opinion of the Court by Justice Black; the text shown does not state a vote count. Justice Brennan concurred and Justice Douglas filed a separate opinion.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep397/usrep397337/usrep397337.pdf"},"1970/46":{"facts":"Appellants were voters and candidates in Canton, Mississippi, a jurisdiction covered by the Voting Rights Act of 1965. They sought to enjoin the 1969 city elections, alleging that the city had changed polling place locations, annexed adjacent areas that enlarged the electorate, and moved from ward to at-large election of aldermen without submitting the changes under Section 5. The at-large change was required by a 1962 Mississippi statute, but Canton had elected aldermen by wards in 1965.","procedure":"A single district judge temporarily restrained the elections, but a three-judge court dissolved the injunction and dismissed the complaint after examining whether the changes had a discriminatory purpose or effect. The elections were then held with the changes in effect, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on appeal.","question":"Do changes in polling place locations, in municipal boundaries by annexation, and from ward to at-large election of aldermen require prior federal approval under Section 5 of the Voting Rights Act, and what may a court deciding that coverage question consider?","holding":"A court deciding whether a change is covered by Section 5 may ask only whether the change is a covered \"standard, practice, or procedure with respect to voting\", not whether it is discriminatory. Each of the three Canton changes was covered and required prior submission, and the remedy was left to the District Court on remand.","reasoning":"Following Allen v. State Board of Elections, the Court held that the three-judge court wrongly examined the merits of discriminatory purpose or effect, which Congress reserved to the Attorney General or the District Court for the District of Columbia. Because the record was adequate, it decided coverage itself rather than remanding. Under Allen's broad reading of Section 5, polling place locations affect a person's ability to vote and carry potential for racial discrimination, and legislative history confirmed they were covered. Annexations change who may vote and dilute the weight of existing voters' votes, and Fairley v. Patterson had applied Section 5 to dilution through at-large elections; the Attorney General's administrative interpretation supported this reading. For the at-large change, the procedure in force on November 1, 1964 was election by wards, since Canton in fact elected by wards in 1965, so the 1962 statute's bearing was for the federal authorities to decide. On remedy, the Court did not order new elections but left the District Court to decide, giving weight to the nature of the changes and whether coverage was reasonably clear.","separate":"Justice Blackmun, joined by Chief Justice Burger, concurred in the judgment, joining the reversal and remand given Allen. Justice Harlan concurred in part and dissented in part: he agreed that polling place changes were covered, but would hold that annexations affect voting only incidentally and are not covered, and that the procedure in force on November 1, 1964 was the one required by state law (so the 1962 statute made the at-large change not covered). He would also order new elections only if the city failed to obtain federal approval. Justice Black dissented, adhering to his view that Section 5 is unconstitutional, arguing the changes were local and non-discriminatory, and objecting to the possible remedy of a new election.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded with instructions to enjoin enforcement of the changes until compliance with Section 5 is shown, and for the District Court to determine the remedy. Opinion of the Court by Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas, Stewart, White and Marshall.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep400/usrep400379/usrep400379.pdf"},"1971/49_orig":{"facts":"Illinois moved for leave to file a bill of complaint in the Supreme Court's original jurisdiction against four Wisconsin cities and two Wisconsin sewerage commissions. It alleged that the defendants discharged some 200 million gallons daily of raw or inadequately treated sewage and other waste into Lake Michigan, an interstate body of water, and asked the Court to abate the public nuisance. Illinois argued that the defendants were instrumentalities of Wisconsin, making this a suit against a State that could be brought only in this Court.","procedure":"The case came directly to the Supreme Court on Illinois's motion for leave to file an original action; there were no lower court proceedings.","question":"Must the Supreme Court hear Illinois's pollution suit against Wisconsin cities and agencies under its original jurisdiction, or can a federal district court hear it, and does federal common law govern interstate water pollution?","holding":"The Court's original jurisdiction is not mandatory because Wisconsin need not be joined and political subdivisions are not States under 28 U.S.C. 1251(a)(1). A federal district court has jurisdiction under 28 U.S.C. 1331(a) over the suit, because federal common law governs interstate water pollution and counts as laws of the United States, so the Court denied leave to file.","reasoning":"The Court said its original jurisdiction should be invoked sparingly and is obligatory only where no other appropriate forum exists. Although prior cases show that actions of state agencies can be attributed to a State so that it can be joined, Wisconsin need not be made a defendant, and because political subdivisions count as citizens for diversity purposes, the term States in the statute does not include them. They may be sued in the Court under its non-exclusive jurisdiction over a State's suits against citizens of another State, but that jurisdiction is not mandatory if a district court can hear the case. Section 1331(a) is satisfied because the amount in controversy is plainly sufficient, laws includes federal common law, and a State may sue under it. The Court then held that federal common law governs air and water in their ambient or interstate aspects, drawing on cases such as Georgia v. Tennessee Copper and the interstate water apportionment cases, and that federal statutes, including the Water Pollution Control Act, do not displace it, though they may guide it; state standards are relevant but not controlling.","disposition":"Motion for leave to file denied without prejudice, with the parties remitted to an appropriate district court. Opinion of the Court by Justice Douglas, for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep406/usrep406091/usrep406091.pdf"},"1974/73-1018":{"facts":"Martin and Margaret Mazurie operated a bar, the Blue Bull, on non-Indian fee land on the outskirts of Fort Washakie, an unincorporated village in the Wind River Reservation in Wyoming. Federal law banned introducing liquor into Indian country, but a 1949 amendment excluded fee-patented lands in non-Indian communities, and a 1953 law let tribes regulate liquor in Indian country if state law was also followed. In 1971 the Wind River Tribes adopted an ordinance requiring both tribal and state licenses. The Mazuries' application for a tribal license was denied after a hearing, yet they kept operating the bar, and federal officers eventually seized its liquor.","procedure":"After a nonjury trial the District Court found the bar was in Indian country and convicted both respondents, fining each $100. The Court of Appeals reversed, holding that the term non-Indian community was too vague, that the prosecution had not proved the exception inapplicable, and that Congress could not delegate authority over non-Indians on non-Indian land to the tribes. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Is 18 U.S.C. 1154 unconstitutionally vague in using the term non-Indian communities, may Congress regulate liquor sales by non-Indians on fee land inside a reservation, and may Congress delegate that authority to tribal councils?","holding":"The statute is not unconstitutionally vague as applied to the Mazuries, Congress has authority under the Indian Commerce Clause to regulate liquor distribution at establishments like the Blue Bull even on non-Indian fee land, and Congress could validly delegate that authority to the tribal council.","reasoning":"Because the vagueness claim did not involve First Amendment freedoms, it was judged on the facts of the case. The evidence of the bar's location near Fort Washakie, the Indian majority of nearby families and school pupils, and nearby Indian housing justified the conclusion that the bar was not in a non-Indian community, and the Constitution did not require precisely defined concepts of Indian or community. On power, the Court relied on precedent such as Perrin v. United States that the Indian Commerce Clause lets Congress regulate liquor in Indian country wherever situated, and on Seymour v. Superintendent as showing congressional authority over non-Indian land within a reservation. On delegation, limits are less stringent where the recipient has independent authority over the subject; tribes are not private voluntary organizations but have attributes of sovereignty over internal and social relations, which include the use of intoxicants. That non-Indians cannot join the tribe did not change the result, citing Williams v. Lee, and the Court left open whether any Fifth Amendment protection exists against arbitrary tribal action since respondents did not raise it.","disposition":"Reversed; the Court of Appeals' judgment was reversed and the convictions reinstated. Opinion of the Court by Justice Rehnquist, for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep419/usrep419544/usrep419544.pdf"},"1978/77-1554":{"facts":"Three adult men (respondents) and a 16-year-old girl, Jane Doe, were riding in a car stopped for speeding on the New York Thruway. An officer saw part of a large-caliber pistol protruding from Jane Doe's open handbag, which held two loaded handguns and was on the front floor or seat on the passenger side where she sat. Police also found a machinegun and heroin in the trunk. The four were tried jointly, and the prosecution relied on a New York statute making the presence of a firearm in a car presumptive evidence of its possession by all occupants, except where the weapon is found upon the person of an occupant.","procedure":"The trial court overruled objections, treated whether the weapons were on Jane Doe's person as a jury question, and instructed the jury it could infer possession from presence in the car. The jury convicted all four of possessing the handguns and acquitted them of the trunk contraband. The state appellate courts affirmed, and the federal District Court granted habeas corpus because the presumption was unreasonable as applied. The Second Circuit affirmed on the different ground that the statute was unconstitutional on its face, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a federal court hear on habeas corpus respondents' challenge to the presumption, may it decide the statute's facial constitutionality, and is the New York presumption constitutional as applied to respondents?","holding":"The federal courts could hear the claim because the New York courts did not rest on an independent state procedural ground, but the Court of Appeals erred in deciding facial validity. Because the presumption here was only permissive, it is judged as applied to the facts, and as applied to respondents it satisfies due process because a rational connection exists between the proved and presumed facts.","reasoning":"The Court found no basis in New York law or the history of the case to infer that the state courts relied on a procedural default, so the federal claim was properly before it. It then explained that a challenger may attack a permissive presumption only as applied to him, whereas a mandatory presumption, which the jury must accept, is tested on its face by how accurate it is across cases. The jury instructions here made the presumption merely part of the prosecution's case, one the jury could ignore, so facial analysis was error. As applied, the guns were too large to be hidden in the open handbag and one was in plain view within easy reach of the occupants, and it was highly improbable that a 16-year-old girl alone possessed them, so it was rational to infer that each respondent knew of them and could exercise dominion and control. The inference therefore met the Leary test that the presumed fact be more likely than not to flow from the proved fact. Respondents' argument for a stricter reasonable-doubt test failed because that applies to mandatory presumptions that may be the sole basis for an element, not to permissive ones.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred, emphasizing that the jury could readily have reached the same result without the presumption and apparently reached a compromise verdict holding the passengers responsible for the two handguns in plain view. Justice Powell, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart and Marshall, dissented: he agreed there was no procedural bar, but argued that due process requires that a presumption commended to the jury be more likely than not true, that mere presence in a car does not make it more likely than not that a person possesses a handgun in it, and that the Court wrongly relied on other evidence when the instruction allowed the jury to infer possession from presence alone.","disposition":"Reversed (the Second Circuit judgment was reversed). Opinion of the Court by Justice Stevens, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices White, Blackmun and Rehnquist; Justice Powell dissented, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart and Marshall.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep442/usrep442140/usrep442140.pdf"}});